      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</p>
        <p>DISAPPOINTING PUBLIC SUPPORT.</p>
        <p>LOCAL MAN'S FINE WIN.</p>
        <p>After the recent Olympic games, there have not been ivanting candid critics who absert dogmatically that the English race is a decadent one iu the world of sport and athletics, and this falling ofl is greatly deplored by all patriots who wish to see the country first and foremost in tverything. But whatever is the use of wringing cur hands, tearfully bewailing our loss and standing by doing practically nothing tangible to regain that which ve have apparently lost, when we can solve the whole problem ourselves if we will cease to blubber over spilt milk and encourage more those organisations which promote amateur sports? It is only by cultivating the amateur that we can ever bore to gain any distinction profession- ally and nationally and the onus lies with the tuhlic to supp,rt materially any institution under whose auspices amateur sports are held. The Warrington Football Club is one of th-ee organi,a- Cons fully deceiving of lupport for having airanged and held annually for over a score of years a vo„ rts aud gala for which only amateurs are eligible. This year's event, which was the 2.7 th f the series, was held on the fa tball field, Wilde/spool road on Monday. The Warrington public did not rise to the occasion and they are much to be blamed for having, by ccmparatively scant attendance, given such a poor encouragement to the Committee. It was a fine day for the function and despite the fact that it waft holiday week-end and mat there were other attractions in the town, one would have expected to see far more than ,</p>
