      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>U. POVEY WINS THE 220 YARDS HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>U. POVEY WINS THE 220 YARDS HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>ab, ut 2,000 penis on the field. Such, however, was not the case. Surely the attractit n should appeal to all the sportsmen in the town tor the arrangements were excellent and the whole affair was well con- ducted and managed by a hand of willing workers. There was a good enti y which, however, was not quite up to that of last year, and the various contests proved most t sziting and interesting. Many valuable prizes were to be won and most of the com- petitors entered into the races with that whole-hearted zest which is often not so conspicuous in the wcrld of professionalism. It speaks well for the committee and officials that there was not a hitch in the programme from start to fibisb and every event was run df to the letter. There was one regrettable feature and that was that the locals did not figure so well in the prize limas one would have liked,but nevertheless the various races were well contested and the st ectators were kept fairly well entertained. The officials, who are to be congratulated on the excellent arrangements and the smooth carrying out of the programme were : Clerk of the course, Mr T Davies; starter, Mr C Bryan, Prescot ; judges, Messrs T Locker, D Tinnion, E Osborne, T F Darber, T Bowers, J Evans, and J E Warren ; Committee, Messrs T Barnes, jun., T Bunch, T P Btadsbaw, J F Forster. J H Whitham, W Chalmers, J Dutton, I Hackett, Hy Hope, F Heeaom, W Hewitt, W Nevins. F Turner, 3 Wiustanley ; handicappers, Mr H N Ellis, Mr H L Colborne; N.C.U. representative, A Booth; hon treasurer, Mr T Pemberton ; hou secretary, Mr H Ashton. Appended is a list of the winners in the heats and finals with a short description of the most interesting events. The figures in parenthesis indicate the yard mark from which the competitors started. Scholars 100 Yards Handicap (under 15 years of age). —Heat 1: 1, E B Lea, St. Albans (14) ; 2, F Wayvvell, Heathaide (scratch.) Heat 2 : 1, William Brady, St. Mary's (6) ; 2, E B,re, Christ Church (2). Heat 3: 1, Wilfred Brady. St. I%lary's (4) ; 2, A Dover, Stockton Heath (23.) Final : 1, E B Lea ; 2, F Waywell ; 3, William Brady : 4, E Bate. 100 Yards Handicap (Open).—Heat 1 : H Pony, Warrington AC. (Pi.) Heat 2 : 1, S Green, Salford Harriers (91.) Heat 3 : 1, .1 A Jones, Birkenhead (11.1 Heat 4 : I Thorpe, Leigh (11.) Heat 5 : H Kelly, Si. Helena (9.) All ran well in the final, and at the 75 yards mark the runners were clustered. Green spurted and broke the rape a few inches in front of Kelly, who ran second with Jones a good third. One Mile Novice Bicycle Handicap —Heat 1 : 1 Harold Swift, St Helens (45) ; 2, David Bay, Warring.</p>
        <p>injured, Povey showed himself to be a most expert runner and be was loudly applauded on receiving his prize: One Mile Handicap —Warrington was well repre- sented in this race ar.d the majority of the locals stuck it well, although none came amongst the winners. A fair number finished and as was exrected,T 0 Donoghue, Liverpool (155) gave the other competitors a hot time and ran second to .1 E Cteminson, Salford Harriers (104) who won essily, in spire of his heavy handicap. W. 3 Knowles, St Helens (148) was a good third. 440 Yards Hanoicap.—Hest I : I, H Tomlin, Leigh (32) Heat 2: 1, If Harden, Birkenhead (30) ran the course alone. beat 3 : 1, A Brown, Swinton (28). Heat 4 : 1, George Palmer, Dublin (34. Heat 5: 1, W. 3 0 Twist, Widnes (29). In the heat Brown showed splendid form and when the final was run he was soon to the front—a position he maintained by good, steady I running to win trom Harden who put up a good show. Palmer" touched "—a good third place man. Two Miles Bicycle Handicap.—The winner of the one mile handicap, Campbell, was penalised 30 yards in this race, his handicap being 250 yards, but nevertheless he name in second to Cherie* Hutton, Widnes (300) in his heat. J H Ardern, High Leigh (295) rode third. Heat 2 : 2, J 0 Clare,Risley (235) ;2, Robert Monk, Earles- town (200) ; 3, Frederick Stadivell, Warrington A.O (325). The final proved to be a mist exciting one and it was ratuer unfortunate that rain commenced when the last two laps were entered upon by the leaders. The going was made much harder and no doubt the sprint some of tho cyclists had reserved themselves for was keocked out of them by the heavy rain. When the eiehtli lap was nearly completed, there were only about three lengths separatiog the first of the six riders from the last, and all set themselves for one supreme effort. Ardern, who hung back at the first spurted forward on the last lap and etruggled hard with Clare,who,however, had a nice lead fifty yards from home but this was con- siderably reduced and Ardern wrie only a few yards behind when he finished second t Clare. Hutton, who was awarded the third prize, showed very good form' and kept a good steady pace throughout. Two Miles flat Race.—Although over 20 competitors lined up for the start of this race in which a great deal of stamina is needed, only three finished the course When the first mile had been run there was a very scattered field and the last two laps were only run by a few. T Fennah, Crewe ((65) won from J Hogan, Rad- cliffe (255) who gained five yards on the leader in the last fifteen and only finished five yards behind the Crewe runner. C Vose, Warrington' AC., ran well and was the only other man who finished the !distanoe, Mrs T Looker gracefully presented the prizes and congratulated each winner.</p>
      </div>
