      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FORESTERS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>High Chief Garner spoke at some length on the Insur- ance Act at the opening meeting of the Foresters' High Court, at Leicester, on Monday. They shtuld now, be said. rake every possible effort to secure the best results from the Act for their members and their Order. He hoped it would be made perfectly clear that those who had obtained their insurance cards through the Post Office had still the right to join an approved society. With regard to the doctors, be as certain that it was not those who had been associated with the Foresters who had shown themselves to be irreconcil- able. If the profession continued their present attitude be hoped that medical fees would be handed over to the Order, which he bad no doubt would be able to make satisfactory arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</p>
        <p>CRICKET NOTES,</p>
        <p>The recording of Warrington's weekly performances is verging on the monotonous, end supportors of the locals will be pleasantly surprised whenever they do hear of a notable victory by the town eleven. It is possible that this result might have been forthcoming on Saturday, if the rain had not unkindly interfered. Wigan were the visitors to the Arpley enclosure and the match had just reached an interesting stage when stumps were drawn. The Warringtonians had first turn at the wicket but,with two exceptions, the batting was very indifferent, no fewer than five " ducks" figuiing on the score sheet. Broughton's bowling! was deadly, his swerving deliveries giving the Warrington batsmen an uncomfortable time. Be secured 6 wickets for 18 runs. The two exceptions to the rule of meagre scores were S L Partridge and W Richardson who batted brilliantly. Partridge treated Broughton's bowling with refreshing freedom, and carried out his bat with 59 to his credit. Richardson compiled a fine 48. When rain stopped further play, half the Wigan wickets were down with the score at 85. The only double figute scores were obtained by Tyler (31), C A Armstrong (25) and N A Selkirk (21 not out).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Owing to the holidays,Warrington 2nd were unable to fulfil their fixture at Wigan and it is probable that a mid-week match will be arranged.</head>
        <p>Owing to the holidays,Warrington 2nd were unable to fulfil their fixture at Wigan and it is probable that a mid-week match will be arranged.</p>
        <p>There was a meeting of the giants in the Warrington and District League, Sankey, ho are going strongly for the championship, and Warrin.;ton Conservatives, the plesent holders of the honour, trying conclusions. Each side played with great keenness and Sankey had the satisfaction of winning by three runs. The bowlers and fielders did their work well, as is evidenced by the fact that the respective totals were only 28 and 25.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recreation made good use of the week-end to place two decisive victories to their account. On Saturday they nut Walter's Brewery, whose plight is indeed a melancholy one. Batting first the wiremen put together 101 for six wickets, but tae dimensions ot the score were chiefly accounted for by the fine not out innings of F Jolley and A Brarnhall, who made 44 and 38 reFpeorively. The Brewery eleven were all dismissed tor 38,3 Coyne (10r being the only hatsman who made anything use a show. On Monday Rylands' inflicted a crushing defeat on Cairo-street Unitarians, who were all out tor the paltry score or 9. Way well and fdoGoa an being the destructive agenta.The reply of the wire men was 53 for five wickets, A. Brumhall and A. Renwick each knoLling up 16.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</p>
        <p>[By " Silver Street."'</p>
        <p>Builth Wells is the cen4re of Welsh inland spas. It stands on the Wye which there receives two tributaries of very similar name, the Ithon and the Irfon. Llandrindod Wells is in the former valley, and the others, Glanne Wells, Llangarnmarch Wells and Llanwrtyd Wells are all near the Irfon. • 11 Passing Builth we took the road parallel to the latter river, for it would be ridiculous to say "up the valley." It chases upend down from stream to stream, over hills too step to ride up either one dirsction or the other till we reach Garth. At the three lane ends there we made a big mistake. Ilsving the C.T.C. edition of Bartholomew's map we found the direct road through Llangammarch to Llanwrtyd marked as a first-class r,ad. We were, however, perplexed to find the finger-post point the opposite way "to Llanwrtyd," though the map showed that road squally good, but about two miles longer. Moral Be eute the local authority° knows the road better than any map maker. We took the nearer road, and immediately the switch-back started again, and the last six miles were a weariness to the flesh on account of the bad surface. We picked up a Guide Book at Llaogam- march ; really, the analysis of the water is like read- ing the labels on the drawers in a chemist's shop, and includes every rare metal there is - barium, strontium, calcium and all the rest. What must it be in Welsh ?</p>
        <p>The top of the valley is marked by a oplendid peak, "the Sugar Loaf,' and the road is splendid, too, led round and round the hills, making a five miles free wheel down to Llandovery on the far famed salmon river, the Towy. As that finished nearly 50 miles for the day, we sought out Miss Isaac whose name we had found in the C.T.C. list. A lady of middle age who lives at Brecon Villa, she made us so comfortable that we regretted we were not stopping our tour there and then, and spending a fortnight in Llanclovery. Next morning I went up the stream two miles to photograph a fine example of the " spectacle bridge," and also a foot bridge, so fragile and dithery that it is well called the Devil's bridge. It hangs over a torrent by half-inch wire cables, and reminds you of the pictures of Carrick-a-Rede in Antrim. We left the splendid Carmarthen road at Llandilo, and commenced the ascent to the colliery district, and the descent to Pont Ardulais—a very busy industrial centre, and eagerly quitted by a tourist. But we reached a point only three miles from Swansea centre before we became conscious of approaching a big town. The road between those two centres, Pont A rdulais and Swansea is being entirely remade,— hills and twists removed and in six months it will be a splendid highway made from a series of wretched little lanes. Thus we saw something of the working of Lloyd George's Development Act, and b‘came aware of the enormous amount of work that remaking our roads would involve. _ _ _</p>
        <p>After securing rooms at the Central Cafe, and good and reasonable they were —Gs. 3d. bed and breakfast for two,—we spent the evening at Mumbles Head. Swansea Bay is a horse shoe with a fine road nalfway round, from the big port in the middle to the holiday resort at the western end. Swansea people are exceptionally fortunate to have such a place five miles away. We certainly did not bless the London and North-Westeta Railway who have made an embankment for three miles, between our road and the sea, in order to run their trains into Swansea But there is another railway line running all the way to Mumbles, called the S.O. and M. In order to allow its passengers to enjoy the sea views,the carriages are all tramcars, 5 or G in a train, and the people gener- ally sit on top, the first class quite separate from the other two, but not a whit better in accommodation. There also seems to he a fourth class for working men and their people, soldiers, sailors, and all other pestilential subjects of the King. They have no upper deck at all, but just merely sit on seats like dry toast in a rack.</p>
        <p>Next morning the Ilfracombe boat started at 8,30, and though we were ten ,minutes late in starting, several folks came down too late, and were left. One man was allowed to slide down ft ,m tit, quay side on to the hand rail and then jump on deck. He only lost his walking stick which fell lot° the dock. Most of us expected him to lose something else. I was disappointed with Ilfracombe. It is just like a swelled-headed Blackpool on a hill-side. "To those who like that sort of a place,it is just the sort of a Place that they like," but the awful crowds, and the touts, and tea and coffee houses, ugh ! and there is no sand for children except at some distance. Of course no-one can deny the magnificence of the rock scenery, but the crowded streets don't seem to fit in with such sentiments, so we took the Barnstaple-road. That was our first taste of Devonshire. Our second was worse. Up a hill 800 feet in 4 miles, with roads made pasty with a thunderstorm whose dripping edges we had felt on the steamer and a hot humid air, hemmed in with hedges 15 feet high, wonder- ful in their vegetation. We got to Two Pots Cross, expecting to find a rest hnuse and instead a solitary farm-stead "Plain teas 6d." No one answered my knock and a passing carter volunteered the information "They've gone down to town.; but you can try at yon farm." We tried; a delicious cup of tea with real Devonshire milk, and for the rest a great dish of fresh gathered raspherries and clotted cream. That was our third experience of the big country, and made up for the other two at once.</p>
        <p>We slid down to Barnstaple eight more miles almost without a mcment's work, and stayed the week- end at Appledore. A more .attractive spot I have rarely seen. But as it has been the object of this tour to find a real seaman's village it will take a good Lit more telling than I have room for this week. WARRINGTON WESLEYAN CYCLING- CLUB. This club held its run to Astbury near Congleton on Monday. The route taken was through N,.rthwich, Davenham, Middlewich and Sandbach. Uri arriving at Astbury the old 13th century church received the attention of the club and was well worth their visit. Proceeding on the Macclesfield road through Congleton the deserted village of Havanna, which presents a somewhat weird appearance, was called at. Continu- ing on the Macclesfield road to Warren. the club arrived at Chelford whore tea was thoroughly enjoyed. The teturn journey was made through Peover and Knutsford. Although the weather at the start was unfavourable, it developed into a lovely day and those who braved the elements were fully repaid, the whole club unanimously deciding it to be the best ride of the season. The distance covered was GI miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., AND ACCOUNTANCY. .</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., AND ACCOUNTANCY. .</p>
        <p>ATTEMPT TO CLOSE THE PROFESSION.</p>
        <p>Revolution is impending in the accountancy profes- sion of Great Britain and Ireland (says the " Stand- ard"). The Public Accountants and Auditors Bill now before Parliament is the cause of it, and the details contained in the text of the bill, which has just been published, have split the thousands of practising accountants into two opposing clamps. The bill has been presented by Mr. Harold Smith M.P., and has the support of Mr. Allen Baker' M.P., Mr. Marshall Hall, Mr Joynison Hicks, and ,Mrl? E Smith. The object of the bill is to secure the I proper registration of persons practising as public accluntants and auditors, and to further efficiency in the profession. A representative seeking opinions of the text of the bill gathered some widely different points of view. At the Institute of C.hartered Account- ants it was stated that the bill was certainly not inspired by that body. " Whoever are responsible for the bill," said an othcial, " recognise our institute, but we do not recognize them. It is in many ways desirable to close the accountancy profession against outsiders and unauthorised persons, but to admit every member of these young societies mentioned in the bill, as well as all outsiders rho happen to be practising at present, will lower the standard, and it is much too high a price to pay for the desirable end of closing the profession. Some three years ago our institute supported a bill before Parliament to register all public accountants who could prove that they were prsctising and capable. That wi.s blocked by a City member, Sir Frederick Banbury, on a question of Parliamentary procedure. This latest bill will possibly meet a similar fate. It should have little chatce of becoming Jaw."</p>
        <p>„ At the Society of Incorporated Accountants much the same attitude was adopted against the bill. At the London Association of Accountants quite the opposite view was expressed. Also, it was stated that Mr. F. E. Smith and Mr. Marshall Hall, two sponsors of the bill, are both vice-presidents of the Liverpool centre of the London Association,lwhich had, all told, over 1,300 members. The London Association of Accountants hold that the desirability of closing the profession is very great, and the terms outlined in the oill are fair and reasonable, and meet with the associa- tion's complete support. This view is endorsed by the Central Association of Accountants.</p>
