      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNEbDAY.—Before Councillor Henry. Roberts (in the chair) and Mr CI Mather:</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNEbDAY.—Before Councillor Henry. Roberts (in the chair) and Mr CI Mather:</p>
        <p>, THE STORY OF A TOWEL.—lsaiah Clay, labourer, Bostocic street and George Melia, iron worker, Bostock street, were charged with stealing./ a towel from the lavatory of the Chapel House Inn, Sankey on Sunday.— From the evidence of the prosecution it appeared that on the day in question the landlord William Unsworth, received information to the effect that the prisoners bad a towel which bad come from his lavatory. He gave them in charge after having kept them in sight until an officer arrived. He bad missed about a dozen towels since Christmas.—Sergeant Smith, who appre- hended the prisoners, said that in reply to the charge both prisoners replied" Not guilty."—Clay stated that as they were going from the public-house they met a farm labourer who was wet through. He asked for some money and he (Clay) gave him threepence where- upon the labourer gave him a towel which was wringing wet. Melia corroborated and there being a doubt about the case the prisoners were given the benefit of it end the case was dismissed. A FORMER ATTENDANT'S TIIEFT.—Chris Barker, Winwick-street, a former attendant at the Winwick Asylum, pleaded guilty to a charge of having at Winwick, on :My 14th stolen a bicycle and accessories, valyed at .E 7 us 6d, the property of Thomas Starkey. Prosecutor, a signalmal in the employ of the London and North Western Railway Co., and living at Stone Cross-lane, Lowton, said that on the date in question he left his bicycle outside the Swan Hotel at Winwick between the wall of the house and a motor cycle. After an absence of four minutes be came back and dis- covered that the machine had disappeared.—Thomas Burton, broker, Stanley, Liverpool, said that on the 13th July prisoner came to his shop and offered him the bicycle for £3. Witness said he would give him £2 for it on the following day. He reported the matter to the police, as he was suspicious.—P.S. Cooper, Newton, arrested the prisoner who when charged admitted the theft.—Prisoner, who said he was under the influence of drink, was sent to prison for one month with hard labour.</p>
