        <p>LABOUR MEMBER'S CLEVER SON.</p>
        <p>ELECTED TO THE GILCHRIST TRAVELLING STUDENTSHIP.</p>
        <p>The eldest son of Mr Stephen Walsh, the well-known Labour member for Gig Ince Division of Lancashire, who was recently successful in gaining the first-class honours degree of B.A. in modern languages and litera- ture at the Victoria University, Manchester has just, been notified that the Gilchrist Trustees have elected him to the Gilchrist Travelling Studentihip. and he has also been awarded the University Graduate Scholarship in modern languages. The Gilchrist Travelling Studentship is of the value of LW, and enables the holder to take a course of study at one of the Con- tinental Universities, generally in France or in Germany. The University Graduate Scholarship is of the value of £25. It is interesting to note that Mr. Walsh, jun., has won his way, by means of scholarships, unaided from the elementary day school to the University. He has only just turned 20, and his scholastic successes have brought congratulations from far and near.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons I THE END OF put in quite an IHE SESSION, interesting day's work on Wednesday in con- clusion to the first portion of an unusually interesting session. Mr. MAC CALLUM SCOTT apologised to the Speaker so graciously, and with so much frankness, that the PRIME MINISTER promisedlto propose his readmission to the House immediately on the re-assembling. Mr ACLAND announced in reply to a question from Mr. GEO. GREEN- WOOD, that the International Agricultural Association is requiring into the vast slaughter of migratory birds in the South of France and Italy, which appears to be interfering very seriously with the means provided by the nation for keeping down destructive insects in this country, a condition of things which enforces the moral of Longfellow's poem, "The Birds of Killingworth." The perennial topic of food supply in war time was not neglected. This is a matter which is being considered by the Imperial Defence Committee, said Mr. AsuiTH. The question of the use of arms by the London strike-breakers was raised by a Conservative member, who would flatter himself very much if he supposed that he scored anything which was not against himself by his inquiry. Then there was a long and lucid statement by the Postmaster- General concerning what, we suppose, may be called the Marconi "deal." It does not seem at all likely to turn out so bad a thing for the nation as Sir JAMES FERGUSSON'S arrangement with the National Telephone Company seems likely to do. The Opposition was ill-advi,•ed enough to open a discussion on the intention of the Government to withdraw from the Sugar Convention. The object seems to have 139 en to influence the election at Manchester. The charge of doing precisely the same thing was advanced against the Government by Mr. MITCHELL THOMSON, who introduced the subject to the House. He would like to know, he said, whether the Government's decision had been arrived at before or after the announcement of the Manchester election. There was a strong electioneering flavour about it, he added. The reply to that insinua- tion was easy and crushing. The decision of the Government was arrived at last December, long before any election at Man- chester was thought of. Retirement from the Sugar Convention is, of course, a very sad blow to the Tariff Reformers. Our parti- cipation in the sugar arrangement was, after the tax on coal, the first and by much the most important object-lesson on Tariff Rer.orm,and a sadly disastrous but thoroughly enlightening object-lesson it has been. It had cost the country several million pounds a year through the increased price of sugar. It has lost us much trade abroad, and it has been peculiarly injurious to the poor at home. Its main purpose, as alleged, was to enable our sugar-growing Colonies in the West Indies to trade more advantageously with the Mother Country ; but the West Indies have not increased their supply of sugar though the consumers at home have had to pay considerably enhanced prices for that co. amodity.</p>
        <p>There were some matters which did not come immediately before the House because they were raised in questions to which no oral reply was desired. Lord CHARLES BERESFORD led the way. The noble lord wished to know whether, during the late manceuvres, the reserve battleships had on board each of them from 400 to 500 reserve men, made up of all classes from pensioners to coastguards. The suggestion was, of course, that we were exhibiting a Navy made up very much in the fashion of an army in a pantomime. Mr. CHURCHILL'S reply threw much light on the facts and much more on the sincerity of Lora CHARLES BERESFORD. The question, says the First Lord of the Admiralty, was plainly designed to mislead the public. He then states facts by which the insinuations of Lord I CHARLES are simply smashed, destroyed, and pulverised. There were other questions from the same source, dealt with in the same conclusive manner. One of them asked for particulars of matters which, as Mr.</p>
        <p>CHURCHILL said, are well known by Lord CHARLES BERESFORD to be matters of confidence. What are we to think of 'the questioner? Why does he expose himself in this obvious way? To put another question, why does he give up to party what, if it was not meant for mankind, might at least be of service to his country?</p>
        <p>A GREAT To have completed the ACHIEVEMENT. Million Penny Fund and raised a surplus of £4OO is proud achievement upon which all who were associated with the promotion of the Old English Fair are entitled to congratulate 'themselves. Thursday was appropriately described as the "joy day" of the Million Penny Fund, for the members of the committee assembled to receive the statement of accounts and allocate the pro- ceeds of their recent great enterprise. The £4OO remaining to the credit of the com- mittee after paying off the balance of the 'Million Penny Fund is to be devoted to two objects the necessity for which has recently forced itself upon the authorities of the institution—the provision of a Rontgen rays apparatus and the erection of a new mortuary. The Million Penny Fund Committee has thus done a great deal more than it set out to accomplish, for not only has it relieved the Infirmary of financial embarrassment to a great extent, but it has also added to its equipment, which it is highly important should be kept thoroughly cup-to-date and efficient. While all the members of the committee are entitled to share the credit for this wonder- ful result of the Fair, a special measure of satisfaction must be felt by Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman) and Mr. Henry Collin (hon. secretary and acting treasurer) whose excellent example of untiring effort was a powerful stimulus to their army of workers.</p>
        <p>The electors of North-</p>
        <p>„,---- --- NORTH-WEST w est</p>
        <p>Manchester</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER. appear to look upon a by-election as an oppor- tunity for doing something fantastic. When- ever there is a general election and Free Trade is assailed they are Free Traders, but when a by-election comes round they vote Conservative. So much is clearly disclosed by the political history of the div'sion in recent years, and it is probably only reasonable to admit that in its heart of hearts "North West" is a Tory constituency. Nevertheless it possesses a strong leavening of Liberalism and carries an absolute majority of Free Traders. The fiscal policy of the country was not considered as being in any danger from the candidature of Sir John Randles—he made it . quite clear that he was not fighting on Tariff Reform—and many erstwhile Free Trade voters allowed themselves to be swayed by an inherent Conservatism, or by a prejudice against stamp licking, or by a fear that legislation was becoming to advanced. Had Free Trade been at stake we should without doubt have seen a different result, and we are confident that time will remove many of the doubts and fears which beset the average elector to-day. He will eventually be as pleased with State Insurance as he is with Old Age Pensions, and then the Govern- ment will come into its own again. So far as Manchester is concerned we regret the loss of the North-West seat, but we congratulate the Progressives of the division in having found a brilliant leader and champion in Mr Gordon Hewart. No one doubts his capacity to reverse the verdict of Thursday when the North-West Manchester citizen is more at ease, and political portents are more pro- pitious.</p>
        <p>What is the position, THE POSITION AT as regards the Liberal THE RECESS. programme, in which</p>
        <p>the recess leaves Parlia- mentary business? The shortest and simplest account can be contained in a single phrase. The decks are now cleared for the three great Liberal measures—Home Rule, Welsh Dis- establishment, and Electoral Reform. All three have passed their second reading. One clause of the Home Rule Bill has been dis- posed of in the Committee stage. This may seem to leave a very long journey to traverse at the fag-end of the Session. The Liberal Chief Whip prophesies that the Home Rule Bill will be through the House of Commons by Christmas. The Session will, as in 1893-4, have to be pro- tracted well into the new year in order to round off the Liberal programme satisfac- torily. This is not the effect of any lack of generalship on the part of Liberal leaders, nor of strenuousness on the part of their followers. Nor is it any symptom of over- ambitiousness in the framing of the pro- gramme. It arises solely from the peculiar conditions under which a House of Commons, to which the electors have returned a Pro- gressive majority, is forced to do its work, even with the restriction upon the veto of the House of Lords imposed by the Parlia- ment Act.</p>
        <p>The Tories pretend to A ONE-SIDED regard the Parliament ORDEAL. Act as a measure which was contrived to let Liberal measures slip through Parliament softly, silently, insidiously, while the country is scarcely aware of the legislation which is being carried out by the representatives that they chose. A more ludicrous travesty of I the facts could not be imagined. In order to pass into law measures for which the country has given a Liberal House of Commons a mandate, but which the House of Lords dis- likes and decides to obstruct, the Liberal Government has to call upon its followers in the House of Commons for great sacrifices of time and energy, and in many cases of health in order to pass in the first part of the life of a Liberal Parliament the measures which the House of Lords can obstruct for at least two years. And for at least two years filo Liberal Government must be prepared to weather all those sudden gusts, and evade all those ambushes to which every Government is liable, before they can place their measures on the Statute Book. But a Tory Govern- ment need not arrange its time-table with any view to the House of Lords. There is no suspensory period during which a Tory measure, however reactionary, however flagrant in its contravention of the pledges given at a General Election, will hang in the balance and give the country a chance, by agitation and at by-elections, of making it history.</p>
        <p>At the present moment A VERY the sheet-anchor of the. UNSUBSTANTIAL Tory Party Nou SHEET-ANCHOR. appear to be the con- tention that the verdict of the electorate at the last General Election was won by false pretences. The electors, it is suggested, were trapped into voting for the Liberals by the representation that a vote for a Liberal was a vote for the Parlia- ment Act—especially for the preamble !—and that a Home Rule Bill would not be intro- duced without another appeal to tile nation. If this Tory argument means anything, it means that a large num- ber of A. voters polled for Liberal candidata, on the usurance that the Liberal Government (1) would, at the earliest oPP°r; tunity, propose Reform of the House 01 Lords, and (2) would not introduce a Horn° Rule Bill without a further appeal to the nation. If the Tory Party really believed this nonsense, it would have fought every by-election since December, 1910, on Bons° of Lords Reform and the Home Rule question. No Tory candidate has gone to the electors with Lord Lansdowne's scheme of House 0 Lords Reform on his programme, and the part which the Home Rule question bas played in the programme of Tory candidat.es has generally been conspicuous by it 9 absence.</p>
        <p>"A working model ef A WORKING MODEL Protection "—such was OF TARIFF REFORM. the happy description of the Sugar Conventio which a Tory Government entered into as conducive (1) to the revival of the sugar-ca,l industry in our West Indian Colonies; (4 as likely to raise the price of sugar. The sugar-cane industry in the West Indies hr not shown any noticeable symptom ' revival, while the price of sugar has risen t° an alarming extent. Under this " workirig model of Protection," where bad harvests elsewhere created a shortage of sugar, vfe could not benefit by the good harvest la Russia. That is the way Tariff Reform 'works. It pretends to give a great impetus to industries which can only benefit sliglltW from its operation, and prevents the corn' munitKat large from getting the benefit of a. world-wide supply, in which the failure ,iat crops in one regipn is compensated by a go' harvest in another. A careful scrutinY TORY BY-ELECTION the Tory speeches a" TACTICS. re cent by-election makes it clear that all the talk about the electorate being tricked into Home Rule, or deceived by a pretence that the Liberals would immediately On return to office produce a schemer for a reformed Second Chamber, is nothing hat an afterthought. The National Insurance scheme, which the Tories professed t° approve when it was introduced, struc,l them as being vulnerable on one side. I insisted on payment by the insured and th,,e employers; and the Party which denounce° the Old Age Pensions scheme because it Nva not contributory has tried to make caPita out of National Insurance because it Was.l contributory. The Insurance system 3' working more smoothly than they expectedi They are now running on another tack, ha if one tithe of what they say now about the blindfolding of the nation to the Home Rale question is true, their electioneering tactic 9 have made them accomplices to a crime.</p>
        <p>--- "IF SO, WHO ? " modification Aclua Tory version of wha,' happpened at the last General Election crop: up in Mr. Bonar Law's letter to the Unioni-b candidate at Manchester. The Tory Pc quaintly hu of orothu; for a long time stuck to the legend that electors had no idea a Liberal victory wOl lead to a Home Rule Bill. Their own utt ances proved the falsity of this plea. NOW Mr. Bonar Law, says, of course he wartl„eo the electors over and over again that it Liberal Government was put in office, the); would introduce a Home Rule Bill. He w 3,. "told that Home Rule was not an issue./,' The issue was the Parliament Act, wite Home Rule as a corollary. Did any (313 " tell " Mr. Bonar Law that, if the Parll3:: ment Act was passed, it would not be used tt", pass Home Rule, Welsh Disestablishrnell and other Liberal measures. If so, who LNOTES AND NOTIO-NS.</p>
        <p>- Other empires have crumbled and decaYera but the British Empire is built on more soil t foundations—the free will of the con-wale° parts to remain associated with the EmPirei _sir Rufus Isaacs, at the National Liber3 Club. No little excite •ed</p>
