        <p>he Tories are very angry just now at at they call the " political apathy" of the :untrY. In particular, they do not like the tilllinnved way in which the electors regard a:, impassioned appeals made to them ti.°„a.!nt ,Horne Rule for Ireland. It is quite lia`te that the electors will not dance to the a Ile Piped unto them by the Tories, and for i,artily in he verY simple reason—public opinion is favour of a settlement of the {lBll question. som * e of the many members of Parliament 110 are going to Canada in the recess are or,,strangers to the country. Mr. Hamar th -enwnod and Mr. J. A. Baker were born baere• The former's father was a Canadian inrtlister, and Mr. Greenwood was educated A country, serving in the Department of Ivigrieulture and in the Canadian Militia. Macmastel was a well-known barrister at .`,ada for many years before he entered hatillincoln's Inn, and •Mr. Norton Griffiths large business interests there. The Trade Unions passed its second ''lng on Tuesday by a Government 'ritY of 100. It accepts the natural con- that trade union combination to get</p>
