        <p>* - Pleading for continued support for the , I,ry Mr. J. H. Smethurst, in his speech Million Penny Fund Committee on Jay evening, called attention to a lt incident which gives the institution cial claim upon the consideration of the rerritorials. It appears that soon his arrival in camp one of the War- " citzen soldiers" became so seriously lat the doctor ordered his immediate re- 'al to the Warrington Infirmary. He was lit from camp late on Monday night; 'lice ambulance was awaiting him at : Quay Station, and he was promptly eyed to the Infirmary. It was one 3.ck on Tuesday morning when he reached institution but every preparation had Made for his reception, with the result ,an operation was performed at 1.30 and ' progressing i very satisfactorily. * * * Bona,. Law's speech, justifying the to arms on the part of the Ulster lists, is naturally the subject of much (lent in the lobby, and it may be fairly that there is no great enthusiasm on his tor his repetition in the House of the lents to violence in Ulster which he at Blenheim. The instinct of the Con- , 4tive party is, after all, on the side of and order and constitutional govern- 'nd a leader who incites part of a to revolt in view of the passage of in law, and makes a deClaration of Parliament is a new phenomenon in orY party (says the "Westminster The party is nervous about this 'lllication of the new style which thus it on the side of disorder and rebellion debate on the Belfast outrages. .d 1 there is no sign of open revolt Mr. Bonar Law's authority, there are )hogs beneath the surface, * * * London correspondent of a well-known 41 Paper has some interesting comments :e on recent political events in this He thinks that the importance of nheim demonstration has been dted. The threat cf civil war in and Tariff Reform are not likely, he to assist the Unionists. One is too .- and the other, the policy of Protec- not popular: weakness of the Unionist Party, which, ever, is full of enthusiasm, arises 