      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NONCONFORMITY. ---- Portbcoming Articles by Mr. A. Mounfleld.</head>
        <p>NONCONFORMITY. ---- Portbcoming Articles by Mr. A. Mounfleld.</p>
        <p>folbis month Warrington Noncon- -1:1114Y will reach its 250th anniversary. of this date. Mr. Arthur ea'Anofield has prepared an account of the Y. Years of local Nonconformity,which 'kill Th,„ anPear in due course in our columns. nniversary falls upon St. Bartholo- ar;Z:s Day, August 24th, and we have thod to publish the articles during -A trlonth of September. rrl account will be given of Robert virj'es, the ejected Rector of Warrington, , znil°, became the first Nonconformist loililiter• Many interesting documents ue, quoted and the history of local relies will be traced. roll:. no history of Warrington Noncon- ' ' thes ltY has yet been attempted we think . d.e articles will have more than '.,/.lary ir.terest to a large number of TLaininer" readers. first article will appear on Satur- b-,„ August 31st. 41,,,,,A1it1R th.ryLo—ke p the avagee of Time at Bay by 144:.1.„e1VR1T Streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHURnaNler,iwiZenhsdeeguefill3fttiolaapnpaeturalnecoelouri 1 Largeboets ' Peet 1 reell6. int ord Laboratory, London, SE</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>P•</p>
        <p>Local Will. The will has been proved at £2,124 of Mr. John I Henry Mounfield, of Walton-road, Stockton Heath. Territorial Promotion. "The London Gazette" announces that Lieut. Fred R. Monks, of the 4th Battalion the Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) has been appointed captain. Sudden Death. The death ( ccurred with painful suddenness at 8 35 on Tuesday evening of Mrs Eccleston, wife of Mr. Thomas Eccleston,of 16, Arthur-street. Mrs Eccleston, who was 55 years of age, was ironing, when, being seized with illness, she sat on a chair to rest, and shortly afterwards expired. Death is said to have been due to heart failure. Personal. The engagement is announced of Carlton, eldest son , of William Collingwood and Mrs. Collingwood, Mere House, Newton-le-Willows, Lancashire, and May Campbell Carnegie, youngest daughter of the late Alexander Carnegie Cavendish, Madras, and Mrs. Cavendish, Einchampstead, Berkshire. A Runaway Horse. At 11 p.m. on Tuesday a horse attached to a light vehicle was standing in Parker-street when it was startled by passing trains and bolted. A postman, Mr. C. Holt, gave chase, and after a severe struggle succeeded in bringing the horse to a standstill, thereby preventing serious damage being done. Music in the Park. Messrs. Walker's band will play the following pro- gramme in the park on Sunday :—Hymn" Osbourne," W Rimmer ; march "Morning breezes," Manley •, overture, "Veronica," Greenwood ; selection " 12th Mass," Mozart ; cornet solo " Ora-Pro-Nobis," Piccollornini ; selection " The Bohemian airl," Balfe ; selection, " Scotch Airs," Roche ; march "The Olive Branch," Allen ; Gcd save the King. Conductor, W. Winstanl y. DRESS Bastur:s, 193. 64., 21s. 6d., 2.4 e. 6d., specify value in Hand Bas, trunk,', Snit cases, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. National Union of Clerks. The monthly meeting of the Wart ington Branch of ' the Natiot.al Union of Clerks was held at the Labour Exchange on \,‘ eduesday. '1 he Secretary reported that the Union ens continuing to make good progress During the rcontb of June 519 new members were enrolled and six new branches f raiel Carlisle, Gloucester, Leven shulme, B ornford, Manchester (central), and South Bank. It was complained that in I the working of the Insurance Act an army of female Iclerks was being employed and also that a large firm in London had given notice to reduce their clerks' salaries Is. per ~eek in consequence of the Act. TENNIS, Croquet, Cricket ana other outdoor gamer.— J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Anv:.</p>
        <p>-•— . Old Lady's Fatal Fall. At the Warrington Coroner's Court on Saturday Mr. S Brighouse conducted an enquiry into the circu- stances surrounding the death of Mary Ward, seventy years of age, of Refuge Square. Front the evidence it appeared that at 5.45 a.m, on the 23rd ult, the deceased, who was almost blind, was proceeding along with a little girl named Davies, from Bewsey-road to Priestly-street by way of the gullett. When they had gone down a number of steps in the Gullett a man came up and took hold of the deceased's arm and said " I will help you." As soon as he touched the old lady she fell down the other fur steps and broke her thigh. She was attended by a doctor and died on Friday morning. —The jury retnined a verdict of "Accidental death." Scalded to Death. Mr. b. Brighouse held an inquest at the Warrington Coroner's Court on Saturday on the body of George Medley Taylor, the infant son of Mr. arid Mrs. Taylor, of Barnar-d-atreet. The mother gave evidence to the effect that or, the 20th July it was her intention to bath her son, and whilst having her breakfast she placed him in a chair against the table. she after- wards put some hot water in the bath but she found that she would have to cool it down. Leaving the haby in the chair she went for some cold water. On her way she heard a noise and on lcoking round she e.w the baby had fallen out of the chair and upset the bath, home of the water going down his back. In consequence of the very severe scalding he had received, she took him to the Infirmary where he died on Tuesday evening. She draught the child must have over-balanced and fallen into the bath.—A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned. BABY CRRI AGES, special value at 355. Cd. Go-carts ftom 10s. b. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge St.., Warrington.—Advt. Salvation Army Festival. The forty-seventh anniversary of tbe Salvation Army was celebtated on Monday, when the various corps in the Liverpool divisian gathered at Allerton Park. The wretched weather of the weetc-end interfered somewhat with the attendance, though it way estimated during the day that about 1,000 soldiers were present from the thirteen Liverpool corps, and from Warrington, Runcorn, Rock Ferry, Sea- combe, Birkenhead (2), Thatto Heath, Prescot, St. Helens, Wigan, Rhos, Wrexham, and Oswestry. The proceedings began in the morning with a thanks- giving meeting, conducted by Brigadier and Mrs Bennett, followed by a musical meeting given by the Warrington Band. In the afternoon the trophies meeting was held, at which various reports of rescue work were given. In the evening a musical festival was held by the bands. The following bands occupied the stand throughout the day and rendered selections: Bootle, Walton-road, China-street, Dingle, Pembroke Hall, Oswestry, Warrington, Prescot, Garston, and Wrexham. Among the officers of the division present were Major and Mrs Parkin, Adjutant and Mrs Powley, and Staff Captain Potter. Coronation Medals for Police. Prior to the ordinary business at the Police Court yesterday, the Mayor presented Coronation medals to those officers, including pensioners, who could not be present on the occasion of the recent official ceremony. His Worship paid a high tribute to the efficiency of the force in Warrington and said it spoke well for their discipline, obedience and good sense of duty. They acted under the expert guidance of the Chief and those in authority, and they had earned and merited the good will of the people of Warrington. The police forces throughout the country exercised an important duty in the administration of the law but those duties were performed with moderation, tact and kindness. Nowhere did those qualities obtain more than in , Warrington. Ten of the officers completing the list then received their medals from the Mayor. The Chief Constable, on behalf of the officers, thanked the Mayor for the kind words be had said both then and at the official presentation. The whole force had been delighted with his words and with the way the Watch Committee had received them a few weeks ago. Mr. A. Browne said he had had a very I long experience in that Court and be could endorse • what the Mayor had said in regard to the moderation , and kindness shown by the police in al cases where leniency was desired. They did not persecute as at one time, but they prosecuted and gave defendants 'what was just and due to them. If anything could be said in favour of the defendant it had always been I voiced by the late Mr. Talbot and the nresent Chief, Mr Nicholls. FIRE GRATES.—If you want a new grate our large and varied stock will interest you.—J. S. GA.RLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>- - A Warringtonian's Enterprise. A valuable addition, says the "Essex County Chronicle," has recently been made to the Chelmsford Public Library, through the efforts of the enterprising and enthusiastic librarian, Mr. J. W. Howaorth (formerly assistant librarian at Warrington). The addition takes the form of a donation from H.ll Stationery Office of 196 volumes, consisting chiefly of printed copies of the original manuscript written by contemporary chronicles of the Middle Ages, dealing with a period up to about 1500. These volumes are the original sources of information to our national historians, and are of great value to local historians. One of the works is the " Chronicon Auglicanum " of "Ralph of Coggleshall," referred to by a modern bibliographer as "The most eminent Cistercian writer." This addition to the library may not appeal to many at present, but seeing that Chelmsford is a centre for various educational institutions, and that this fact and also the fact that the town's industries attracl numerous students—two features of the county town that are bound to largely develop in the future—these volumes most become extremely useful, as well as at present. being of great historical and scientific value. It may be stated that the total published value of the donation is over £l4O, and Chelmsford and also its librarian are to be con- gratulated upon obtaining donations which are only granted to some libraries, and the more so as it is probable that Chelmsford will now be included among the few public libraries as recipients of future pub- lications of Record Office and the British Museum. The other donations, which Mr. Howarth, in his zeal for creating a library worthy of the name, has obtained, are 93 volumes from the Trustees of the British Museum, and an interesting collection of documents relating to parochial administration of various parishes in the county.</p>
        <p>I -.41</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</p>
        <p>• New Education Committee Member. The Rev. CI. C. Briggs, Vicar of Christ Church Latchford, is to be appointed a member of the Educa- tion Committee, on the recommendation of the Association of Church of England Elementary Schools, in place of the Rev. James Davies, resigned. A.O.H. Presentation. The members of Divisions 122 (Ladies) and 422 Ancient Order of Bibernians held a very successful conversazione in the U.I.L. Rooms, Winwick-street, on Thursday evening. Mrs M Conroy, V.P., Ladies Division, presided, supported by Mrs Kelly, treasurer, Miss K Percival, assistant secretary, Miss M Currie, Sister at Arms, Miss O'Shea and Mrs Currie. In her opening remarks Sister Conroy referred to the strength of the A OIL in Warrington and the power for good they were already exercising. There are now, she continued, something like 700 members between the two divisions and it was their intention to double their present membership before the close of 1912. Con- cluding she explained that the primary reason for the gathering was t•ellhonour Sister Nellie Reardon, who was leaving for New Orleans during the coming week and later in the evening would receive a presentation from the members of the two Divisions. The audience was then entertained to songs, recitations, flute, cornet and melodion selections and solos. until 10.30 when Sister K. Percival on behalf of the two divisions made the presentation to Sister Reardon of a beautiful solid gold Celtic cross and necklet. Bro. D. Currie, President of the male division, was then called upon, and in the course of his remarks, referred to the struggle be had in getting a division opened in Warrington, but fortunately the opposi- tion to the A.O.H. on' y lasted so long asl its opponents were in ignorance of its principles, and he was happy to say there was not now, any opposition to the order in Warrington Referring to America, the land to which Sister Reardon was going, Brio Currie said from small beginnings the Order in America became so influential and powerful that to-day it is reckoned as one of the strongest bodies in the New World. Even if Sister Reardon had no relations in America, she would with the transfer she carries with her from Warrington be assured of hosts of good and true friends. (Applause.) After a few more songs had been rendered Bro Currie proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Sister Conroy for the abl. manner in which sbe had conducted the evening's enjoymant Sister Conrog suitably replied. The singing it the Irish National Anthem brought a most ss ecessful and enj yable evening's entertaim,nt to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Collier— Meakin.</p>
        <p>------- A pretty wedding took place at Christ Church, Croft, on Saturday afternoon, when Mr. Frank Collier of Glazebury, was married to Miss Beatrice Makin, youngest daughter of Mr. William M-akin, Heath- lane, Croft. The Rector (Rev. 11. I. Wadeson) officiated. Mr. T. Hurdus was at the organ and played the wedding march at the clo•e of the ceremony. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in a dress of cream silk figured. voile, trimmed with cream silk fringe and lace, and wore a wreath of orange blossom with veil. She carried a bouquet of cream and red roses, the gift of the bridegroom. The bride,inaids, the Misses Annie and lily Collier (sisters of tl e bridegromi) were attired in Shantung Weaves, with black picture bats trimmed with roses, and carried bouquets of roes and wargueritss, also ti e gift of the bridegroom. Mr. W. T. .Meakin acted as best man. A reception was afterwards held at the residence of the bride's parents. During the afternoon several congratulatory telegrams wete received. The hon-y- -moon is being spent in the Isle of Man. The bride's travelling costume was of elephant cloth, and she also wore a black and white straw hat tiimmed with black tulle. Many handsome presents were received. Tne bride's gift to the bridegroom was a gold scarf- pin, while the bridegroom gave the bride a gold bangle. Meld rum —Slinn.</p>
        <p>A m trriage of cotraidera.ble local in'erest was s flemnized at Christ Church, Timp-rley, on Saturday afternoon, in th-+ pres.tnce of a large congre.ratiou. Toe bride was Miss Dora Slinn, of Houtetton Mount, Stockoort-road, Altrincham, arid the bridegroom Mr. Malcolm Robert Meldium, of Ti,nperley, eldest sou of Mr. R. H. Meldrum, of Bellfield House, A pp'et al, near Warrington. The Vicar (Rev. G. D. Wray), performed the ceremony. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a beautiful dre-ts of ivory crepe royal, trimmed with ivory satin and Honiton lace. The train was caught up under the shoulders with satin fl Avers embroidered with peal,. She wore a wreath of orange blossom and veil, and carried a shower bouquet of white roses and white heather. The bridesmaids, Missess Ella Slinn (sister of the bride) and Gertrude M Johnson, were attired in Liberty bloom silk in a pretty shade of apricot, trimmed with painted chiffon to blend. Their large Leghorn hats were trimmed with deep red roses and black velvet ribbon, and they carried bouquets of deep red roses. Mr. Cedric Meldrum dis- charged the duties of best man, and Mr. Harold Weet- msn was at the organ. The reception was held at Houghton Mount. The bride travelled in a costume of grey whipcord cloth, with white hat trimmed with white wings. The following is a list of presents : Mr and Mrs John Shinn, house linen Mr and Mrs R H Meldrum, cheque Mr and Mrs B Aldred. silver pepperette Mr Atherton, silver sugar tongs Mr and Mrs Bass and family, cheque Mrs Blomeley, Coalport dinh Miss Brundrett, silver brush and leather Misses Brandrick, silver hat pin stand • Mr and Mrs Curnick, tea cosy Miss Champness, embroidered table centre Mrs Calvert, hook Mr and Mrs Coop, silver photo frame Mr and Mrs Chorlton, silver cream jug, pictures and cheque Mrs Cooper, silver mustard pot Mis Cotton, pictures Mr and Mrs Crowther and family, silver sugar basin and cream jug Dr P R and Mrs Cooper, travelling clock Mr Daly, picture Miss Duke, pictures Mrs Dutton. eider down quilt Mr Duncan, silver pepperettes Directors of Herbert Morris, Ltd., silver cigar case and m3tch box Friends at Linwood School, silver teapot and sugar sifter Mrs Faller, silver crumb scoop Mr and Mrs Goldsack, antique silver spoon Mrs Goble embroidered table cloth Mrs Goulden, embroidered dressing jacket Mr and Mrs F Greenhalgh, clock Mrs Gisi-Faller, silver mustard pot Misses Goulden. pearl and amethyst pendant Mr and Mrs H Greenhalgh, silver marmalade spoon and horse shoe Harry, wooden spoons Mr Gemmel! and Miss Williams, mounted hunting</p>
        <p>MINI11111111.11i•immoNIMIPAUIMMIIIIIIIMI</p>
        <p>trophy Mrs Hyde and Mrs Taylor, embroidered tray cloth Miss F D Hughes, Moore, bowl Mr and Mrs Hoyle, canteen of cutlery and table plate Mrs and Miss Holland, cheque Mrs Jucker and family, cheque Mr and Mrs Johnson and family, silver tea knives Miss G H Johnson, photo and trame Jessie, china bowl Mr and Mrs Knight, bread beard and knife Miss Carrie Knight, silver cork-screw Mr and Mrs George Knight, plate baskets Miss Lee, d'oyley Mr and Mrs John Melclrum, knives and forks Mr and Mrs MoNicol, dessert and table spoons Miss Rona Me!drum, embroidered table centre Miss Helen Meldrum, embroidered table centre Mr and Mrs F Me!drum. celery glass Mr and Mrs W It Me'drum, cake knife Mr Cedric Meldrum, cheque Mr P Plevin, copper vase Mr Roylance, silver manicure set Mrs A Bruce Reckie, silver jam jar Miss Reborn, picture Mrs Roberts, set of brushes Mr and Mrs F Simpson, cusWons Mr and Mrs George Simpson, silver teispoons and sugar tongs Mr and Mrs A H Simpson, silver fish eaters Mr J J Simpson, book Mr and Mrs Slack and family, cheque Mr and Mrs James Slinn, jun., photo and cheque Mr and Mrs Shinn, tahle cloths Mr end Mrs James Shinn and family, cheque Mr George Slim and Miss Lernaire, cheque Miss Ella Slinn, diamond ring Mr and Mrs Silcock, eiderdown quilt Bryan and Spencer Silcock, Royal Worcester vase Mr and Mrs Sneath, cheqae Mrs Thomas and family, bilver hot water jug 111 r and Mrs F Vickers, book Miss C Wood, d'oyleys Rev U D and Mrs Wray, silver purse Mrs Wilkinson, book Mrs Williams, nut crackers Mr A Williams, grape scissors Misses Wood, Bruges lace handkerchief Mrs and Miss Walker, enamel brooch Mrs Yates, embroidered supper cloth 1 Ellen, embroidered tray cloth Pixi, Sambo and Eira, pincushion "Lark," tea cosy</p>
