      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Bunty Pulls the Strings."</p>
        <p>The visit of Mr. Cyril Maude's company of Scottish players in that delightful comedy " Booty pulls the Strings" had been anticipated with considerable interest, for Mr. Graham Moffatt's play has every- where been acclaimed as one of the most conspicuous theatrical successes of recent years. The enterprise of the management in securing this high-class attraction was rewarded with crowded " houses " on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday evenings. Judging from the expressions of appreciation, all who witnessed the performances were prepared to eadorse the many pleasant things which have been said of the play. It is a refreshingly natural piece of character- drawing and a quaiat picture of home life in a Scottish village where ultra- Sabbatai ianisrn persists and any tendency to impart a degree of pleasure t 3 Sunday observance is repressed with a stern hand. It is little wonder that the.high-spirited Rab Biggar rebels against the harsh rule of his father who insists that he shall devote the Sabbath exelusively to kirk and catechism and visits him with pains and penalties for daring to whistle on that solemn day. As for Bunty, the fascinating daughter of the house, she is the antithesis of the dour and stolid Scot. She manages affairs in a quietly businesslike way and by clever "pulling ot the , strings" contrives invariably to get her own way. In the end she conquers the prejudices of her austere father, and prevails upon him to take a more human view of life. As may be gathered from the foregoing, the comedy is not of the " screaming " variety but depends for its success upon a dry and pawky type of humour which is irresistible. We have nothing but praise for Mr Maude's company who presented the play without the semblance of exaggeration and made the various characters really live. Miss Jean Clyde was a charming Buuty, and captivated everyone by her ingenious handling of complicated situations. She dominated the play without giving her Rat any undue prominence. Mr David Urquhart achieved a fine success as Weedum Sprunt, the stupid, blundering elder, whose first Sunday at " the plate " was fraught with so much disaster. Mr. Walter Roy 1 impersonated Tammas Biggar, the Puritanical, unbend- ! ing Scot, in a realistic manner which was wholly convincing. Mr. Abie Barker soon installed himself as a favourite in the role of Rab Biggar, whose mercurial temperament is ill-adapted to his dull environment and causes him to defy his father. Miss Mary Campbell was admirable as the designing Susie '-;impson, and she had a capital foil in Miss Jesephine Woodward as Eellen Dunlop, who prevails upon the cautious Tammas to take the matri- monial plunge fur a second time. Miss Ella Young was an attractive Teenie and the other parts were in capable hands. As a curtain-raiser Harold Brighouse's one act play The price of coal " was much above the average aid conveyed a vivid impression of the trials and auxieties of life in a colliery village. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. ,</p>
        <p>At the Royal Court Theatre, Mr. G. F. Fry will sublnittnext Neek a play bearing the rather striking title "The Rid from the jam factory," a story from the pen of Mr. Frank Dix.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Atlss MAUDE STE WART-DREWRY.</head>
        <p>Atlss MAUDE STE WART-DREWRY.</p>
        <p>The play is described as a new and original domestic drama, and is certainly original, inasmuch as it has no "hero" ; but deals in a striking fashion with the life story of a worker in a jam factory. Miss Maude Stewart-Drewry plays the title role—a most interesting character, showing her fall, her rise, and ultimate salvation. It was written specially to suit Miss Drewry's particular talent, and the author con- siders he has written nothing better. Miss Maude Stewart-Drewry, it will be remember, played Thora and Elen —mother and daughter—in the dramatic version of Hall Caine's famous book "The Prodigal Son," and was the first of the very few actresses whe have been able to sustain this double role. •</p>
        <p>' " •,••• , 21A, I*. . • .• : . •••.• , •-•...• • . . . ....• . • • • • • • ••• g•-•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Companies' Experiences in</p>
        <p>Camp.</p>
        <p>AN EARLY RETURN.</p>
        <p>On Friday last week the Warrington Battalion of the C.L.8., sent a composite company to Ripon for camp. Before their departure the Officer Commanding War- rington Battalion,L eutenant-Colonel Appleton, spoke to the lads, asking them to keep) up the good name the battalion had made for itself in former years. Hot refreshments were served and about midnight the Warrington lads entrained under the command of their Adjutant, Captain Sankey. At ( ilazebrook they joined the Wigan Battalion in a special train and from here to their destination Captain Sankey assumed command of the whole train.</p>
        <p>Ripon was reached about six o'clock, camp was soon sighted and after being settled down in their lines a parade was ordered for 10.30, which was preceded by a short religious service. When theso wore concluded duties were over for our local lads, and many were to be seen "exploring "the city and its outskirts. Retreat was played at .7, First Post sounded at 0.30, tattoo at 10, and "lights out" at 10.15. .</p>
        <p>A feature of the C.L B camp is the prayer silence which lasts for three or four minutes and is observed ten minutes after reveille and ten minutes before lights out. On Sunday rain commenced to fall and continued steadily all the morning, clearing up in time for , service in the Ripon Cathedral at 2 30. At this service the chief camp chaplain (the Rev R W White, of the Guards, London) officiated. Befor tea the rain again started and the tents were being flooded, which made it impossible for the lads to sleep in camp. However, through the kindness of the Mayor of the city, quarters were found for our lads in the Opera House, whilst other companies were accommodated in the Town Hall, Fire Station, and various schools. Again on Monday morning the rain was early on the scene and was still coming down when it was time for the 10.30 parade. Dinner rations were served out and both battalions, with over 1,000 in each, took leave of camp and proceeded by a round-about route to Fountains Abbey which, together with the large grounds, were put at the disposal of the Brigade, through the kindness of the Marquis of Ripon. A short religious service was held in the ruins of the old church and there is little doubt that it will be long remembered by all whose privilege it was to be present. A tour was afterwards made of the ruins and as the sun had began to shine, a most enjoyable and profitable few hours were spent. The state of the camping ground can be judged when it is mentioned that it was deemed advisable not to allow the lads to go on it that night. The dining utensils were brought by the orderlies. Accommoda- tion for the night was found in some schools. Tuesday's proceeding were again marred by the rain which came down unmercifully. The schools were again requisitioned as they were for the rest of the week. There was a short route march on Wednesday morning which was followed by the dinner being served on the road leading to camp. The camping field was in an awful state and utterly unfit to tramp upon, as the nand was inches deep. During the day a</p>
        <p>meeting of officers decided to strike camp on the following day and this was done. On Thursday the local lads departed from Ripon at 11 20 and arrived horae about five, none the worse for their wet but neverthelesi enjoyable time. Ripon was designated by the campers as an inland " watering" place and there is little doubt we saw it at its best in this lole.</p>
        <p>llt is a singular thing that there were very few cases in hospital—none from Warrington—whereas last year with fine weather there were many eases. On Wed- nesday night the members of the N.C.O.'s men con- nected with the Warrington Battalion had a supper at the Victoria Cafe, and here Sergt.-Alajor Bottomley, who presided, spoke of the way the Warrington N.C.O.'s had backed up their captain, and how every private had done his best for the honour of the battalion. Captain Sankey was unavoiaably absent from the supper as was also Captain Clever, from one of the Manchester Companies, both of whom had been invited, but they made an appearan:e when all was over and took the opportunity of saying how much they appreciated the work done by the Warrington N.C.O's. Several honours fell to the Warrington line, for besides always being the first on parade, with one exception, the captain WBl3 chosen field officer for two days out of the five and the Sergeant-Major was selected as the assistant Sergeant-Major f.tr the whole camp,— which was very creditable considering that it was his first time at a C.L.B.camp and that there were men with South African experience also in the field. Sergeant-Instructor Essenhigh was appointed assi,t- ant sergeant-major for the Ist Battallion. On dismissing at the Central station Captain Sankey said that they had taken a good name to camp but had brought a better one away. In the ordinary course the camp would hive been inspected yesterday by Lieutenant-General Sir H C 0 Plumer K.O B. but as camp was struck on Thursday this most interesting part of the programme was abandoned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Week after week we have bad pleasure in testif:ing to the able manner in which the management of the Hippodrome have catered for the people of Warrington with high class vaudeville programmes, but for the August holiday they have excelled themselves and large houses have been highly delighted and well enter- tained each night throughout the week. One of the finest acrobatic performances we have seen for some time is that provided by The Three Laurels. With ease and grace they perform the most daring feats and their band-to-hand balancing and the swiftness and dexterity with which they get through their show is little short of marvellous. An excellent comedian makes light work of his tasks in a bright and breezy manner. His antics are just funny enough and not too boisterous to be in harmony with the other part of the per- formance. A dramatic sketch brimful of emotional situations, "A Policeman and a Man "is ably presented by Stuart Cleveland and his company of accomplished artistes. The theme is a good one and the acting of the officer as he mentally struggles between love and duty is very realistic —altogether a successful sketch. Many vaudeville artistes to-day travel under the descriptinn of " comedians " but how many of these self-styled mirth-makers really deserve the title?' A style all his own, catchy songs, witty gags and many other excellent qualities truly give Harry Levaine an indisputable claim to be con- sidered a good comedian. As the " lady in white " he is a pronounced success and he is well received for other numbers. Although there is a sameness in juggling turns which gets monotonous, a really clever artiste can make us forget we have seen things similar and if he bass style like Halma, the crazy dancing juggler, success is assured for him, in War- rington at least " Wet Paint "is the title given to a clever refined eccentric act by Maro and Laurier. The audience is kept in one state of animation during the whole time the couple occupy the boards as expert tumblers and comedy merchants. A really clever exposition of second' sight is given by the Marvellous Days to whom nothing seems impossible to describe. Myrtle Grove, a clever child actress, thoroughly deserves all the praise which has been lavished upon her, for although she is only young she has the style which "gets there." Mayer and Barton give a clever trick and comedy cycling turn, although they might have received more applause if it were not for the fact that Warrington people have rather tired of this class of entertainment. Ross Warren is billed sea comedian but his ludicrous make-up is perhaps the chief factor in provoking laughter.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BONDAGE BREAKER AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>BONDAGE BREAKER AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Tom Jack "The Ice King" who is the " star " at the Palace and Hippodrome next week, is said to be the most expert bondage breaker of all times. Up to now it is claimed that it has been found impossible to tie or bind bim with ropes, chains, or in a strait jacket in such a manner that be cannot release himself in full view of the audience. At no time during his performance does he leave the stage, nor does he avail himself of the use of a cabinet or screen. The public are invited to bring their own ropes and chains.</p>
        <p>Tom Jack was born in Iceland in 1881, and is therefore 28 years old. His hair, not only his head, but also his eyebrows, are naturally snow white and the pupils of his eyes are red. Ile has been examined by many leading scientists and doctors, who have pronounced him to be a phenomenon. Tom Jack was appointed and still holds, the position of special instructor to the police force in Berlin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ROYAL THEATRE OF VARIETIES.</head>
        <p>ROYAL THEATRE OF VARIETIES.</p>
        <p>The Royal Stock Company's presentation of "Our Mary Ellen" at the Royal Theatre of Varieties has proved an exceptionally good holiday attraction and large crowds have witnessed this-great laughter pro- voking play each evening. The humour in which the piece abounds is worked round a matrimonial affair and each incident is thoroughly enjoyed. Nliss Leah Corenters appears in the title role with her usual skill and grace, and is a pronounced success The other parts are in capable hands, Mr. Harry Val Vorsden showing much talent as William Tipson. As the spendthrift Charles Shackleton, Mr. C. Vivian Charles invests the part with sufficient recklessness; Mr. C. Nelson Turner makes an excellent Mr. Kershaw and the whole play is most interesting. For next week the Royal Stock Company will occu he boards in . • .</p>
        <p>apy the the highly sensational play "The Ticket of Leave man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WELSH WING THREE-QUARTER FOR WIGAN It is officially annotmed that after considerable negotiations the officials of the Wigan Rugby Football Club have succeeded in signing on Llewell)n, of Ebbw Vale. Llewellyn, who is the Welsh wing three-quarter, is expected to turn out in the practice games at Central Park. Last season, the Warrington club were in negotiation for the clever end speedy little Welshman, but the price was " too stiff" for Wilderspool. Llewellyn would certainly have been an acquisition, and would have helped to fill the nap which has been so apparent ever since Fish retired; With Woodburn on the transfer list, Waywell on the decline, and Bradshaw injured, the local club looks like being again deficient in scoring power. Practice for Warrington F.C. commences on Monday, and there will be a practice match on Saturday next, when one or two new players are expected to be on trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TAXING LAND VALUES.</head>
        <p>TAXING LAND VALUES.</p>
        <p>The Experience of Australia.</p>
        <p>By John Paul. The question of lard values taxation has again forced itself to the front in British politics. Ten years ago the municipalities of the country brought it from the obscure regions of academic agitation right alongside the questions of lccal Wing, housing and municipal expansion. In the 1009-10 Budget it appealed to the imagination of the people as a much- needed and long-looked-for reform. Meanwhile the valuation of the land is in ccurse of progress, and its completion is keenly anticipated as the basis cl a thorough and radical change in our systems cf raising national and local taxation. With the apptoval of the Prime Minister, Mr. Lloyd George has appointed a committee for the purpose of investigating the facts of the case for land reform. This looks like business.</p>
        <p>Land values taxation has long since passed beyond the stage of experiment. Some fifteen years ago, to be precise, on October ]4th, 1897, Sir George Reid, High Commissioner of the Commonwealth of Australia, then Premier of New South Wales, vindicated the land tax his Government had imposed the year previous in the fullowing words : " 1 have no hesitation in saying that the land tax, small as it is, has killed the trade of the land gambler" TRADE FOLLOWS THE TAX. The tax, as Premier Reid declared, besides providing revenue, had a penalising influence. It penalised the land gambler holding f..r a use, and in doing so brought a measure of prosperity to the rest of the community ; in four short years it vi as officially claimed that there had been an increase of nearly one million acres of cultivated lands following the adoption of the land tax, and that the condition uf the Colony was prosperous. In this report we read : The excess cf arrivals over departures for the three years since the passage of the Land lax Bill were in New South Wales, 5,159,whi1e in the adjoining Colony of Victoria, which had about equal population and resources, but din not have the Australasian system of taxation, the emigration exceeded the immigration by 50,403._</p>
        <p>_ _ The lesson was taken to heart, and in 1905 New South Wales took a further step in land values taxa- tion. In that year a Local Government Act was passed dividing the Colony into shires similar to our counties, and providing in a generous way for the rating of land values and the unrating of improve- ments. Nor has this brought ruin to New South Wales. On the contrary, the Colony is basking in the sunshine of material abundance, as the following cable from Sydney, December 17th, 1911, indicates: The city of Sydney is growing at a marvellous pace, and the building operations in 1911 constitute a record. ExCluding public buildings, 6,503 were completed this year at a cost of ;C5,656,759. In the past tour years 22,214 buildings have been erected, costing over £12,000,000. This great expansion is still proceeding, and all industries are feeling the pinch of the shortage of labour supply.</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIA TO NEW ZEALAND. Such signs of industrial activity and the news which comes to us of high wages and wcrk for all in the building trade of Sydney may not altogether be due to the taxation of laud values, but it is a sufficient reply to those who urge that the tax will injure the building trade and drive capital from the country. The average rate on land values in New South Wales is 31d. in lite £. on capital value. The trouble with the building trade in Sydney, the calital city of New South Wales, finds expression in the want of men to do the work, arid nibt, as here, in the want of work for the men available. "Wages average 10s. and 12s. a day of eight hours," writes an emigrant from our shores, "and this is a glorious country." New Zealand led the way for Australia in land values legislation with its national tax of 1891. Says Senator Bucklin. in his report to the State Gevernment of Colorado, 1899: In the four years immediately preceding the land tax, in spite of G'evernment ownership and manage- ment ot railroads, telegraphs, telephones, insur- ance, etc., there was an actual decrease of immigration over emigration of 17,789 persons, being a loss each year. At once, on the passage of the land values tax. the tide of emigration turned,population has increased 122,447, and in the first two years after the adoption of the land tax the immigration of New Zealand exceeded the emigration 15,370 persons, and has con- tinued in excess each of the eight years since its passage From 1891 to 1898 the cultivated lands of New Zealand increased 3,522,091 acres, sown grass lands increased 3,278,501 acres, the value of improvements increased nearly £8,000,000, and nearly all businesses greatly impro't ed. LOCAL RATES AND LAND VALUES. In 1890 a local rating Act was passed giving powers to local rating authorities to put rates on land values. This was tried, and with enduring success. Take one example of many similar ones. The Town Clerk I of Wellington, in his annual report to the Mayor and Councillors of the city, 1902-3, says: "The result of the first year's trial of this system of rating must be considered a gratifyiLg one, and leaves no room for regret at its adoption. That which was claimed by its exponents has been amply fulfilled ; it encourages improvement, stimulates the use of land, secures the unearned increment to those who have added the value, mulcts all lands in their fair share of taxation, and paralyses the old system under which rental values on lands could, by simple manipulation, reduce local taxation to a farce. It is to be hoped that in the near future legislation will enable the unimproved system to be adopted for all those pur- poses for which it is now necessary to also undertake an assessment in the annual value basis. It is only stating a fact tl say that much, if not all, of the activity of the building operatfons of the city and surroundings during the past year is due to the influence of this healthy measure. " In 1900 the late Prime Minister (J. R. Seddon), in reply to the question, "Has the land tax, as imposed in New Zealand, been a success ? " said that the answer was in the affirmative and that there was not a single candidate who advocated its repeal. The Australasian system of taxing laud values has done much by its success in breaking through land monopoly, but it is vitiated and weakened by the dis- criminating way in which the taxes ate imposed. The system of graduations and exemptions adopted is un- fair as between land-owners, and leads to land with- holding on the part of the smaller monopolists. It tends to burst up the big estates and to leave in their place a greater number of smaller speculators who, exempt from the operation of the tax, are thereby encouraged to keep desired land out of use. But this s another story.</p>
        <p>In an article describing early telegraphy upon the Great Western Railway,the August " Railway Magazine " recalls the first case of a criminal being brought to justice by means of the electric telegraph. This event took place a few months after the extension of the system to Slough in 1844. A murder had been committed at Salt Hill, and upon hearing of the occurrence the Vicar of Upton- cum-Chalvey (the Rev. E. T. Champnes) was informed that the last person seen to leave the house was a man wearing the dress of a Quaker. Mr. Champnes at once conceived the idea that the suspected person had pro- bably fled to London, and acting upon this suspicion, hastened to Slough Station, where a man answering to the description was seen by the Vicar to pass through the booking office. Communicating his suspicions to Mr. Howell, the station superintendent,the latter caused a telegram to be sent off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>REMOVING OLD PUNT.</head>
        <p>REMOVING OLD PUNT.</p>
        <p>•:)ld paint is best removed before repaint- and the removal can be effected with a --.-.arning-lamp and blunt chisel or with a :-.quid preparation. In. burning off with a lamp, some of the paint remains in the grain of the wood, and is so hard that an inverfect attachment takes place between the new colour and the old. The lamp is also liable- to scorch prominent parts 02 the work, and the edges of mouldings are apt to get charred. On the other hand, in using alkalies, such as soda or lime, some of the salt is likely to remain in the grain of the wood, to come out afterwards in a blister. The water, too, is apt to swell the grain, and to leave damp to be sealed down by the paint afterwards applied, causing blisters. The advantage, therefore, of burning off with a lamp consists in the absence of damp. Alkali removes the paint more com- pletely from the grain of the wood and i there s no charring; but the alkali is apt ito swell the grain and to injure the new paint.</p>
      </div>
