        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>When a woman's back aches from morning to night When she can't work—can't eat—can't sleep When her head is bad—her heart bad— When she has dizzy spells and bearing-down pains, and feels weak, nervous and irritable—</p>
        <p>It is a warning that the kidneys are not: WARRINGTON M WOAN doing their duty—they areleavilig kidney poisons in the blood, and these poisons are spreading --- disease to all parts of the system. Mrs. S. Howell, of 13, Albert Street, Bewsey 1 Road, Warrington, says :—" I've been wonder- Dropsy, stone, inflammation of the I ledder, fully better in health since I used Doan's back- and urinary stoppage, as well as rheumatism, ache kidney pills. I took them for the kidneys, lumbago and sciatica, arise from the sue cause, which had been causing me a great deal of misery for about six months. "The trouble started with lumbago, the pains in my back and kidneys were frightful; I had to keep to bed, but was quite unable to turn over. " The kidney excretions were disordered. , There were also bad headaches. I suffered It is just such honest, heart-felt testimony as terribly with these at times. this that has established Po much confidence in I "I kept to my bed for four or five weeks, and Doan's Backache Kidney Pills here in Warrington. then got up, but was still bad. Then two months afterwards I was laid up again for three weeks. I The symptoms were just the same as before. e When I first began using Doan's backache kidney pills, ease came after the first four or five doses ; and after that I steadily im- proved, until I became quite well. (Signed) MRS'. S. ErowEi Nearly -I years later, Mrs. Howell said :—" Doan's pills cured me some time ago, and I am as well now as I have ever been in my life." In 2/9 hires only, 6 boxes 139 ; NEVER SOLD LOOSE. Of all chemists and stores, or from. Foster-McClellan, Co., 8, Well', St., Oxford Street, London, W.</p>
        <p>Your neighhonr tells here how Doan' s Backache Kidney Pills help speaks to you from personal experience.</p>
        <p>Like Mrs. Howell had. `iS3</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday. before Mr. W. Roberts and other magistrates, Harry Bennett, confectioner, Derby House, Lovely lane Warrington, appeared in answer to two summonses for driving a motor car at a speed dangerous to the public and not having a licence to di ive the same on the 3rd inst. The Chief Constable said that P.C. Skelton saw the defendant coming along Lovely-lane in charge of a motor car which was travelling at a dangerous speed having regard to the general circumstances of the traffic. The defendant rushed past a coal furry and between that and a milk float, just escaping both of them by the barest margin. The officer followed him, to where the motor was kept and the defendant was somewhat excited, and when asked for his licence be- came abusive. He went in the house and produced a licence which it was afterwards ascertained had not the defendant's name on it, bit another man's. The mother of the defendant offered the officer 5a., whether for a new licence or as a bribe he would not say, but he sug- gested it was intended fcr a bribe for the officer, judg- Inc by the language that was used. P.C. Skelt,n estimated the speed of the motor at between 15 to 20 miles per hour and he considered that very dangerous under the circumstances. The defend- ant only missed the other vehicles by about flvellinches on either side, and it was a wonder there was not a very serious accident. Defendant said the car broke down , in Manchester and he had to go and fetch it or he would not have been driving it. The defendant's I mother offered witness ss. to say nothing about the matter.—ln answer to the Chief, the officer said there was no sound of a horn and he was only ten yards away from the defendant.</p>
        <p>Defendant : When you came to me you said "I want to see your licence " and I did not know you were an officer and that is why I asked " What do you want my lictnce for."</p>
        <p>Tbe Chairman : Have you anything further to say ? Defendant : Nothing. The Chief Constable said the speed of the motors generally through the town was becoming a very serious danger to the public. The licence of the defendant expired in June and it would have been easy for him to have got it renewed. There was one endorsement on the old licence. _</p>
        <p>Defendant was fined (with the costs) .£6 15s. in all.</p>
        <p>Messrs Stirling's, Ltd., Bridge-street. The prisoner then elected to be dealt with at the Quarter Sessions and the case was adjourned so that arrange- ments might be made for his committal. It, had been decided to offer evidence against the prisoner for loitering with intent, as the case was not one which should wait until the October Quarter Sessions,having regard as to how much it would cost the Corporation. At 1.30 on the lot April the prisoner was seen loitering in Bridge-street.He went to the shop and stole a pair of boots. He then went into the yard of Messrs. Hutchinson, basket-makers, and the manager, after receiving information, followed him. The prisoner threw the boots on the heap and the manager picked them up, whereupon the prisoner wanted to fight him. A crowd collected and the prisoner cleared off and was not heard of again until he was seen by a witness in a public-house. Ile was immediately picked out of a large Dumber of men and banded over to the police. He had been twice convicted for begging and on taking his finger prints and making inquiries, they found he had been convicted at Strangeways for being on enclosed premises.—Morgan was sent to prison for three months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>EXCURSIONIST IN TROUBLE.—Reuben Crompton, 27, , Lordshill-Equare,Oldhato, was summoned for interfering with the comfort of passengers on an excursion train from iihyl to Oldham on the 22nd June.—Mr R L Mason who prosecuted, said that when the train was passing the Ship Canal Bridge the train was brought to a standstill through the pulling of the communication cord. The guard ran down the line and saw the defen- dant standing on the ballast. It was found out that he had misbehaved himself in the carriage and bad insulted a lady passenger. A struggle had taken place and a child in the carriage might have been very seriously injured. The lady pulled the cord. The defendant made his escape by running down the embank- ruent.—Prisoner said it was the first time he had been in such a position. He had been in the Workhouse and had a wife at home.—A fine of 40s. including costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>TIIOSE WINDOW SMASHERS —John Johnson, 128, Knutsford-road, was summoned for haying done malicious damage by breaking windows on tbe premises of Daniel Johnson at Blackbear Bridge, to the extent of 3s 6d.—Mr Browne, who prosecuted, said that on the Sunday the defendant was seen to ram his first right through the plate glass window, without any cause whatever. Mr. Johnson, who resided in Koutsford- road, desired him to press the case as heavily as he could as the windows most not be broken for mere fun or wantonness. There were notices posted on all the windows to the effect that a reward would be given to anyone furnishing information RS to persons who broke the windows. One would think the defendant was a suffragette but to all intents and purposes be was not and he (Mr. Browne) could not understand why he had done it.—Defendant said be was pushed against the winnow and his'elbow wenethrough. Mr Browne pointed out that the window was higher than his elbow.—The Chief Constable said the damage done by window smashing in the town, and at Latchford in particular, was enormous. Complaints bad been frequent and most serious, and the police had been unable to cope with it The damage was not done by young boys, as they would see the defendant was a man. People must be protected. —Mr Browne mentioned the great expenqe his client had teen put to through having his windcws smashed.— Johnson wan lined 3ds , or in default one month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Evans and Pierpoint, Messrs G A J Jolley and W Furness.</p>
        <p>A WrtacicErt OF HomEs.—Victoria Charlwood, 53, Garabaldi-street, was charged with stealing between the sth and 7th last, a sheet and a pair of troutera value Os., the property of her father. The Chief Constable said that the father of the prisoner, a gentle- man 74 years of age, came to the Court the previous morning and made a complaint to the effect that lie had living at his house his daughter (the prisoner) and her husband and he was quite unable to do anything with them. He had endeavoured to get rid of them but had not been successful. He com- plained that the daughter had been stealing goods from the house and he desired some help. Enquiries were made by the police and Sergeant Iteddington found that the prisoner bad been stealing from the house for some considerable time and the parents of the daughter felt that they were bound to prosecute in order to break her off her drunken habits. The articles enumerated in the charge bad been pawned and they were only a small portion of what had actually been stolen. He (the Chief Constable) asked for a seven days' adjournment of the case, as Mr. Firth, the Police Court Missonary, would probably be able to suggest something. The only thing the parents desired was that the prisoner and her husband should leave them. The daughter and her husband had had several homes and they had had to be broken up through drink. Drink was the cause of all the trouble. The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
