      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Councillors Henry Roberts (in the ebair) and C Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>' rAItMER. FlNED.—William Haslam, Lancaster Farm, Buttonwood, was fined with the costs (Os) fur having caused a horse to be worked in an unfit condition.— From the evidence of the It.S.P.C.A. Inspector, it appeared that on the 26th ult. the defendant was in charge of a horse attached to a float. The animal was very lame, and on examination it wag found to be suffer- ing from an old-standing sprain of the fetlock joint on the hind leg. The animal was afraid to put its toot on the ground.</p>
        <p>WEDNRSDAY.—Before Mr .1 W Hayward (in tha chair),</p>
        <p>'' '''''''''''''''''''Cks-on Holmes, Mr w T 'Williams and</p>
        <p>Dr Naden. ALLEGED THErr.—Harrist Massey, formerly of Stanley-street, was charged with stealing between the 24th June and the 10th July articles or bed clothing from a lodging-house. The Chief Constable, in asking for an adjournment, said the prisoner had been engaged hy a Mr McCabe as deputy in his lodging-house in Stanley-street. She came to the town six months ago in company with a man. She went to lodge with McCabe and:he engaged her as deputy. On Mrs McCabe making the beds she missed the things which it was afterwards ascertained had been pledged with different pawnbrokers in the town.—The case was adjourned for seven days.</p>
        <p>THI'RSDAY.—Before Mr W Roberta (in the chair). Messrs A J Jolley and W T Williams and Dr Naden. THREE MONTHS FOR LOITERING.—WiIIiam Morgan, 10, Hopwood-street was charged with loitering in Bridge-street on April lst.—The Chief Constable said the man was before the Court a week ago charged with stealing a pair of boots value is lid, the property of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL SUMMER SHOW.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Garden Society made an experi- ment this year in holding its summer show on August Bank Holiday. There was much to commend the choice for, notwithstanding the inviting programmes of the railway companies, a fairly large proportion of the public always remains to be catered for at home, and an interesting attraction like a flower show and file is welcomed. For once the uncertain weather stood the society in good stead. The conditions on Sunday and in the earlier part of Monday were so forbidding that many people who would otherwise have been " seeking fresh woods and pastures new " were unwilling to risk the prospect of a cold and cheerless day at more or less distant resorts. Before noon, however, there was a wonderful improvement in the outlook and for the greater part of the afternoon the sue shone brightly from a blue sky. Consequently the Wilderspool Park in which the society was privileged to hold its show, was thronged with visitors, and the patronage was such as to afford the society every encouragement in its estimable work. While the show is some small index of the useful purpose which the society is fulfill- ing, the best way to judge of its good influence is td take a walk through the village and notice the beautifully kept gardens which give Stockton Heath a fair claim to the title " garden village." There is a healthy rivalry among the inhabitants to secure the , most striking displays of blooms, and this spirit is ' fostered by the garden socieiy which, it is gratifying to note, has now a most enthusiastic body of workers at its command. Monday's show was the eleventh promoted by the society and, notwithstanding a try- ing season, it was probably the best of the series. The entries numbered about 400 and were 50 in excess of last year's, while the judges considered the quality in most classes excellent. In some sections there was a lack of keen c enpetition but fortunately this did not signify any falling off in the standard of merit. The exhibits were effectively staged in three marquees and made a lovely array of variegated colour. The sweet peas were a splendid collection and Mr. F. Broady is to be congratulated upon his fine achieve- ment in again winning the silver flower bowl, pre- sented by Lady lreeriall, for the best nine vases of tho-e flowers. This is the third year in succession that Mr. Broady has secured the honours and the trophy therefore becomes his property. The peas which he showed included many of the leading varieties, notably "Helen Lewis" (pink) and "Nora Unwin " (white). Great taste was shown in the arrangement of flowers for table decorations, and Miss Hallows, whose exhibit was neat and artistic, was for the third year the winner of the handsome silver epergne presented in this competition. There were some very choice roses and the silver vase given by Colonel Alan Sykes, II P., for six distinct varieties, was awarded to Mr. A. J. Mottram. In the vegetable classes potatoes stood out the most prominently. Of the fruit, apples were particularly good. A highly pleasing feature of the society's work is the induce- ment offered to children to take an interest in the culture of flowers. This year the classes in the junior section were much better filled than ever before and there was strong competition for the prizes for the best collection of wild flowers, not exceeding twelve varieties. In the class for named varieties an exceedingly fine display was that of Ralph Acton. The premier prize for the best arrangement of grasses in vase was won by Miss May White whose exhibit was far superior to any of the others. A competition which called forth general commendation was that for colour and pen and pencil drawings by children of various ages. The entries numbered 160, and were judged by Mr. Ramsay, headmaster of the Greenock Art School, who, in his report, said the water colour work on the whole was very creditable. Some of the exhibits were sympathetically treated, and showed evidence that the pupils had observed the character of growth and pose as well as form arid colour. The pencil section was not so strong as the colour. The renderings generally were timid and not very expres- sive. The crayon drawings of sweet peas, hawthorn and Canterbury bells were very good indeed. The attractiveness of tha show was considerably enhanced by some magnificent exhibits which were not for competition. Messrs Dickson, of Chester, showed lovely roses —including "The Lyon "—and some striking gladioli, carnations and phlox. Messrs. Bees, of Liverpool, made a strong feature of their roses which were a wonderful and brilliant collection. of many varieties, one of the latest being the " Juliet " Mes,e-s Clibrans, of Altrincham, devoted their exhibit chiefly to herbaceous plants, and Mr. G. Tandy of Latchford, had a pretty display, much admiration being aroused by a capital specimen of the Hiawatha rambler rose. Mr. Frank Houghton sent a fine group of cacti, Mr. Robson J. Chorley beautiful roses, and Mr. Jas. Ryan choice violas, roses and dahlias. Mr. Ward, of Lower Walton, had an interesting exhibit of bees at work.</p>
        <p>The judging was ably undertaken by the following: _Exhibits at the show, Messrs J Stoney (Woolton), W Bramwell (Altrincham), E It Finch (Liverpool), 0 Bennett (Lyrnm) ; exhibits in junior section, Mr T Edden ; table decorations. Mesdames Woodcock, C Garton stone, S Wright and J H Fletcher ; judges of rutside exhibits, Messrs Poulton (Grappenhall), and Yarwood (Walton). In addition to the flower show, there were several entertainments for the visitors. The ;pupils of Mies Brown acquitted themselves most admirably in a series of fancy dances which were gracefully and skil- fully performed. Athletic sports were well contested and there were also the usual amusements of a fair- ground. The Pioneer band gave selections during the afternoon and played for dancing in the evening. The society is exceedingly fortunate in its officials and committee who have spared no pains to make its work successful. Special praise is due to the hon. secretary (Mr. E. G. White) who discharged his arduous duties with tact and thorough- ness. The complete list of officials is as follows : President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.O. ; vice- presidents, Revs James Collins, and H V Pigot ; Messrs H R Armitage, T Baxter, J H Clarke, L G Davies. Vere B Davies, E J Hall, W H J Jenkins, Roger C Parr, A J Willett and B. I Wynne-Jones ; committee, Messrs W H Woodcock (chairman), J H Fletcher (vice-chairman), F Baker, J B Baxter, J Bell, 1) S Birrell, J W Bouchier, E Bradshaw, F Brook, 0 A Dunlop, T Edden, R. Gorton C W Hallows, J Holt, Houghton, H B Houghton, d Hudson, F A Jenkins, J McDermid, J Owen, T H Plevin, F A Rathbone, W Skelton, F Timperley, E Vernon, A S Wright and W Varwood ; hon. treasurer, Mr D E Watson ; hon. secretary, Mr F G White. THE OPENING CEREMONY. There was a large and representative attendance at the opening of the show by Lady Greenall, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. (president of the society), presided and amongst others present were : Mrs Lyon, Mr and Mrs Glittiths, the Rev James Collins, Col. Alan Sykes, M.P., Mr PM Oliver, Mrs J E Cross, Mr Vera B Davies, Mr and Mrs T Baxter, Mr W Bainbridge, Mr James Bain- bridge, Miss Bainbridge, Mrs Pigot, Mr C S Burgess, Alderman Bolton, Councillor Jenkins, Mr Stanley Wright, Mr W H Woodcock (chairman),Mr J H Fletcher (vice-chairman), Mr D B Watson (loon treasurer), Mr E ' (.1 White (bon secretary) and members of the committee. Sir Gilbert Greenall, alter congratulating the society upon the favourable change in the weather, said the show hail pas,ed through troublous times but it was now, he thought he might safely say, rejuvenated and they were getting it on a sound financial basis. The support accorded to the society was also improving and this year the entries were in excess of those at the I show of 1911. (Applause.) That was a very satisfactory state of a fTairs. (Hear, hear.) The committee, who spared neither time nor trouble had done everything in their power to keep the society in the front rank of flower shows in that part of the world. No doubt they felt amply repaid by the fine show- they had been able to get together. They had in his friend Mr. White a most excellent hon. secretary, while in Mr. Watson they bad a man who looked very carefully after the finances. (Applause.) Sir Gilbert next announced that letters of apology for non-attendance had been received from Lord Wolmer, M.P., Mr. IL rold Smith, M.P.. the Mayor of Warrington, and many others. Messrs. Eckford, of Wem, and Messrs Youngs, of West Derby, who had promised to send exhibits, had fouud themselves unable to do so, owing to the trying weather. He WEIS sure, however, that on inspecting the tents the visitors would be satisfied with the flowers and produce on view. It was with great pleasure that he held the position of president of the society. The committee did their best to cater for every section of the members, and also for the exhibitors, and as long as they pulled together, as the present committee intended to do, he had no fear for the success of the society. (Applause). Lady Greenall congratulated the Society OD the num- ber of exhibits. "The president" she added " tells me they are fifty in advance of last year and as I believe he is a very truthful man, I must accept that." (Laughter). The love of gardening, continued her ladyship, was a taste which had grown all over the world, and in that part of England in particular great strides in horticulture had been made of late years. There was no more worth Pursuit in</p>
        <p>the world than ga,dening. It was a pleasure and a pastime alike for young and old. If there were any doctors present, she was sure they would forgive her paraphrasing some well known lines to read " Better to till the garden for health unbought Than fee the doctor for the nauseous draught."</p>
        <p>(Laughter and applause). She congratulated the Secretary on the great efforts be had made to bring the show to a successful issue. Unfortunately he could not control the weather— (laughter)—hut he controlled everything else in an admirable way—in fact he must have made some sort of arrangement with the clerk of the weather that day,for he had stopped the rain and brought out sunshine. (Laughter and applause). Little Miss May White then handed Lady Greenall a lovely bouquet of orchids (given by Alderman Bolton), with the words. Oa behalf of the Garden Society, I have much pleasure in presenting this bouquet to your ladyship. Colonel Alan Sykes, in proposing a vote of thanks to Lady Greeuall, referred to the great interest which her ladyship took in the encouragement of gardening in Stockton Heath and at Walton. Mr. P. M. Oliver seconded, and said he would like to add a personal piece of thanks to her ladyship for the rhododendrons on Walton Green. So far as he knew, those rhododendrons were the earliest flbwering blooms In that part of the country and the earlier a II )wer bloomed, the better they liked it. Probably they had bloomed earlier this yea! because of the eicelleut weather we had last year —(laughter).—He was Oftgoing to talk about this year's weather, because they were in the presence of ladies. (Laughter.) Fortunately the clerk of the weather had been much better inclined to them at Stockton Heath than he had been to other people, and In thanking Lady Greenall, they might also like to thank him. (Laughter and applause). The vote was heartily carried and Lady Greenall briefly responded. Mr W II Woodcock, in proposing a vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert Greenall, said they recognized his presid- ency of the society with very great pleasure, lie bed given the society very great support and they always felt stimulated to more enthusiastic efforts whenever he came amongst them. (Applause.) The work of the Garden Society was not all evident in that show. They were trying to make it useful in the district generally by encouraging people to beautify their gardens and to grow plants, not necessarily for show purposes. At the same time they were delighted to have such excellent exhibits and, considering the very indifferent weather, the show was a wonderful one for Stmkton Heath to produce. (Applause). Councillor Jenkins seconded and expressed the hope that Sir Gilbert would be spared for many years to be president of the Society. (Applause). The vote was passed with enthusiasm. In responding, Sir Gilbert made an appeal to all who took an interest in gardening to join the society and thus strengthen its influence.</p>
      </div>
