        <p>w P,over chose the site of Dutton chose tpol,n• Though its position seems modest itS prospect covers the valley of the bo' a lair piece of the Vale Royal, and thatunded by hill and mountain scenery Plakes a panorama of the rarest beauty. at n B,lll3Posed that Odard first made him- utitC nome here and built the home of the the °IIB. Probably he did. He was one of Co4ngentlemen who "came over with the ,Itafi',„tlerer and in the day when the country 'Vatted out in bountiful slices took this triir.Y bit of Cheshire as the reward for his Le,s• Nigel was a friend and comrade and K Halton and became its baron, and 1th01,,e,8 ern to have served under Hugh Lupus , Th:reame the Earl of Chester. 4tiper record in the Domesday Book is like tilt e,98 of others. The Saxon is flung out tlttN 'Norman enters. That is all. To Ntf„,,Man tribe plunder and conquest was a ilati''l37 fair game. They had won and the thhveshad lost. With untroubled conscience ~settled in the land to build up those Protlles from which our aristocracy are so Th" to trace their descent. 11)whI1a1ne comes from Dun-ton, that is the wieh;°n the down, and to go along with it ;.'e Norton, the north town, Halton the 'tWichwn and Walton, the well town or the walled town. itiqie traveller upon the Runcorn to North- -oad might easily pass the old hall "t°,n without a suspicion of its interest Y. Though within sight of the high- -1.0 it llows only its gable to the passer-by, 'lot looking complacently south-east it4',"Qe lovely valley of the Weaver. f",°llcl the trouble of making friends with ,',idea;ol dog who examines us with con- NT,'" suspicion and at uncomfortably Itßa,,,lllarters, we have no difficulty in gain- ,`lliflivess to the old Hall. We received a tcl Welcome from the occupier, and pro- N;i" examine this fine fragment of a Tudor</p>
        <p>as it is, it is only a fragment. It is ,e(1 that the hall once formed a quad- ' ''f Which the present piece is a small ilhe chapel and the other portions of 4111 have perished, leaving no trace or 3 ehind them. fine entrance hall remains, and to ,an hour within it is worth a long and e Pilgrimage. So also remains the r„Weudwork of the great hall, though '" by deplorable modern alterations. THE ENTRANCE HALL. ill first look over the Entrance Hall ale its historical flavour. Externally "Y beautiful and bears in bold letters</p>
        <p>RS DUTTON KNYGHT LORDE -uUTTON AND MY LADE DAME .1) HIS WIFF MADE THIS HALL lI4—„BUYLDYNG IN THE YERE OF Iar,,,LORDE GOD MCCCCCXLII WHO 14"KETH GOD OF ALL. '.l)le higher beams are the letters 1);':'• Which are taken as the initials of f,;tB Dutton Knight. On the lower beam Witter's knot binding the letters P. and J. Illfah"al the porch is the great oaken door, famous and beautiful. It is fi'l?4l.,ed to have been the door of the Abbey carried here by Sir Piers Dutton at Car,lll)lo,lution of the Monasteries. The sight arri4 Y door fixed in a private dwelling bk. iR "shack in swift historic flight. We DIN b,.the age of the Reformation when "t)'lllg Harry carried out his wholesale of ,the monasteries. GENTLE WAYS OF KING HARRY. :77. be„%rner of the country had its share "lilleaval and possibly its tragedy too. ' it certainly had if Sir Piers Dutton, 8 most worthy sheriff, arried out of his lord. The king's letter is 'ed and gives a fine sidelight upon 38. Sir Piers is ordered 'without (ler delaye" to take the Abbot of , and his head men and "cause them "411ged as most arrant traytores in ()Ildry places." Everything i that 'd to them was, of course, to be siezed Lk a, at the disposal of the royal t(l)lloCit.ler the sentence was carried out wo lnltha:'lle'w, but there is no uncertainty as Abltlie'rb,eeame of the lands. One wonders h 4ey he spoilers read the deeds of the h I Wh. le they tore to pieces with such }Lest. Had they done so they might 04-4% "",_ uneasy moments. The and of ea it said, with many surrounding :Nero given in pure alms. d if they to 15,e01l stolen Cheshire folkswould not tf).4 fo7,e Paying poor rates to-day. This I.4i.a;"oder wrote out in full his prayers Og the charity and engrossed them thkpe parchment. He besought thn s blessing upon all who maintained titcNlß asli,tY, and prayed in grim and awful 1)°1,111(11,"ilat any who took it from the poor "e Punished with Pilate and Judas in I,Th gOre • I,chtY didl3 , no mistaking that. But King t worry about a hereafter. l'hbh Out of the massive old Abbey door tliNt.abe.k that piece of history very freshly ti 4 'tilers Dutton who built the hall was riff 1/Irae4 t Who plundered the Abbey and b tet 400 r "e Abbot as part of the job, and the Val 1:1;ild wood work stands here as per- Ilteltoia",i'tlless. Like nearly every one of t'44lth families of Cheshire, he gained his Pell 'Sr that gigantic robbery of the tibi Poor. 1k11,,79Q, twv,.°„e Was quite sincere in carving as Olit '4 the door "Who thanketh God of thaL'lflod,,eeposidering that he had gotten it ilth.`trtießlllg the sick, the poor and the tr," is a motto that conveys no Posterity. THE GREAT HALL. .eit of the entrance we turn into 4k-q4vjl as. °nee the Grand Hall. The i 44tib, di,3lllars are there, but, alas, t has Igliaja,,lued into living rooms. It must 4,11gn witbila, ye been a small cathedral built uea,;",,mftY clustered columns support- Ntyke Walaful oaken roof. But to see the ve to ascend to an attic and the 'lc lam°. Pillars project in bedrooms and [444144 illiga. Here is one of the most 4Doile cild halls completely disguised htlis vied. To read the inscription upon the 'lllll.endilst travel from bedroom to bed- a,sie be, lush upon a landing. 41Rnear'r°0m inscription is worth copying. 4% kteats to p hav r round the beams of tt tR „ ha _ .e run llial'eat it and its conspicuous place sug- bw, Was a matter of some moment ilder. ,ERE OF HIS MOST GRACIOSE Alit 4 eRA'' THAT THIS TIALL. AND ALL UTE IIBS AND TNSLACONS WERE MADE 'eI46".ED ANNO MCCCCCXXXIX BI THE DEVISING OF SIR PIERS DUTTON</p>
        <p>KNIGHT AND DAME JULIAN HIS WIFF DOGHTER OF WILLIAM PONIES OF NORTHOK- GTTEN IN ESSEX ESQUIER MEMORAD THAT AFT' LONG SUTE MADE BI THE HEIR OF THE DUTTONS AGAINST SIR PIERS DUTTON THEN OF HATTON KNIGHT AND NOW OWNER OF THIS HOUSE AND HATTON BOTH DEPEND- ING COTINUETH BEFORE ALL THE NOBULLS OF THE KING MOST HONORABLE COUN . . . ALL THE JUGS OF THIS REALME BI</p>
        <p>. . THE SPACE OF VII. YERES AND ABOVE THE SAME SIR PIERS WAS APPOINTED HEIR MALLE AND RIGHTS INHERITOR OF THIS HOWSE AND ALL DUTTONS LAND, AND SO ADIUGD BI T' RIGHT HONORABLE AWARDE OF T' MOST FAMOSE PRINCE KING H VIII. UNDER HIS BRODE SEALE ALLOWED AND</p>
        <p>CONFERMED. Which means that there had been a law suit and that Sir Piers had won his title. That it lasted " seven years and above " says much for the skill of the lawyers, and the depth of medieval pockets. The inscription is nearly ninety feet long and cut in four-inch 3othic letters.</p>
        <p>A BIG EVENT AT DUTTON. After seven years of wrangling and the completion of his hall it would be a pleasure to Sir Piers to witness within it the wedding of his two daughters married there in 1542. It was a double event celebrated at the feast of John the Baptist and the minstrels of Cheshire marched in procession before the happy couple and then made these oaken rafters ring with shouts and music. The mention of minstrels reminds us that the minstrels' gallery can easily be traced at Dutton. The musicians occupied an exalted place over the entrance ball and looked down upon the lords and ladies who dined or danced below. And there is also another reminder arising from the wedding and its music. The lords of Dutton had the licensing of the country ministrels for many generations, beginning with the far-off days of Randle Earl of Chester.</p>
        <p>WHEN LANCASHIRE WAS SOLD FOR 40 MARKS. Perhaps our readers won't mind the story. It is worth telling as a bit of ancient Cheshire history. Randle Earl of Chester lived in jeopardy of the wild Welshmen. This Randle, by the way, was the man who once bought all the land between the Ribble and the Mersey. The price of this piece of Lanca- shire was 40 marks of silver, a sum of money that would buy 100 sacks of flour. Cheshire could look down on Lancashire in those days and its Earl could buy it just for fun out o his pocket money. The site of Manchester; and the potentiality of Liverpool, wii h a thousand coal pits, towns and villages thrown in—all for the price of 100 sacks of flour But this is a digression. Earl Randle wasn't happy though h 3 had bought Lanca- shire for a playground. Those horrid Welsh- men were too much for him. Whereupon Roger Lacey, who rejoiced in the nick-name Hellfire Roger, along with his steward, Dutton, of Dutton, came to his help. When Chester Midsummer Fair was being held they gathered the rabble together, made an amateur army, and went off to give the Welsh a severe thrashing. The Earl in gratitude conferred on Dutton the right to license the musicians of Cneshire or,in the stately language of the document,all "plessinge ye noble art, ye worthy science and high misterie of musique and misstrellzie pub'ely here to draw forthe their sundry instruments of musique and to play here publ'cy into ye court house. God save ye King's ma'ty his most hon'ble counsell and the Lord of Dutton and give us peace." Periodically the musicians gathered at Dutton to make things cheery for their patron. It appears to have been part of the bargain that they did so. They had to go to church ard play "a sett of lowd musi- que" upon their knees singing "God s-tve the King and the heir of Dutton." Then of course they went to the Hall and it ended in a big dinner with copious good cheer, after which the music would become very miscellaneous, Eight miles only of good cycling road separates Warrington from Dutton Hall. It would be the ordinary thing to advise every amateur antiquarian to lose not an hour in seeing it. But a heart of pity forbids. Dutton Hall is now a farmstead and while Mr. Baxter is the soul of courtesy there are limits to a farmer's time and patience. Only two or three weeks ago the Liverpool Historical Society paid a visit and some of its members ascended too high for safety. The weight of learning was too much for the ceiling and someone came through into the bedroom, bring the dust of a generation upon spotless counterpanes. Until ceilings and feelings are fully restored it may be well to ask only to see the ground floor of Dutton Hall. • -sr</p>
        <p>and there were other irresistable forms of entertain- ment. There was a tug-of-war for club members, and there were various competitions for both sexes, includ- ing ladies' hat trimming by eight or nine men. On the list of spot ts was a ladies' race, and as those who had the preliminaries in hand had vt ith rare humour laid cut the course over an uneven stretch of ground, so that some of the runners were tripped up, the fun grew fast and furious during these events. It was all very enjoyable, and thcsa who had flocked from the more distant parts of the division in order to partici- pate in the revelry returned home with the conscinus- ness that they had not undertaken the journey in vain, nor spent their strength—and incidentally their railway fares and a trifle for refreshments —for nought. GARDENING.</p>
        <p>From a Primrose League garden party to Monday's show (opened by Lady Greenall) in connection with the Stockton Heath Garden Society is not a very far cry, though, as it is not for ine to trespass on the domain of the regular reporter, I simply make the reference as a convenient "peg on which to hang a few things of which it reminds me. The growing army of enthusiastic horticulturists of the younger generalon may well be startled and somewhat incredul, us on reading that three centuries ago there were no flower gardens in England ! It is nevertheless a fact that what were then thought of as gardens were herbaria, places where rosemary, mint, rue, thyme, and sage grew, and perhaps a few primitive blooms, such as violets and primroses, were suffered to exist, much as poppies and cornflowers do to-day. Many well- known plants have been developed from sp-ci- mena discovered in various parts of the world, and there is no doubt that charming novelties are still lurking undiscovered in remote spots. What would the men of the Elizabethan age have said if someone had predicted that early in the twentieth century England would have bad nct only a Beard of Agriculture, but a special horticultural branch, and even an etymological section a bcdy of expert advisers to deal exclusively with questions relating to flower and fruit culture ? Yet so it is, as publicly announced by Mr. Walter llunciman the other week. However, I am just now thinking more particularly of amateur gardeners and the benefits of the annual exhibitions in which these are largely concerned. As a correspondent observed in relation to this subject a while ago, not one of the least of the pleasures of gardening is the opportunity it gives for experiments, in consequence of which one can seldom meet others and talk over matters, but you learn something new. One has probably put to the test something which Some other grower with whom he happens to compare notes may have been pi actising all along, just because he has brought up to such a system ; at the came time, the latter may by experiment have just discovered something which has been familiar to the other for some time. Or LK th again have struck some new ides, and both mean to give it an earnest trial. It is thus that amateurs pick up a large slice of their knowledge. Books are all very well, and garden- ing papers are up-to-date, but there is one thing about gardening which neither books nor papers can tell you, and that is, what is and what is not suitable to your own gart'en, to its peculiar soil, altitude, latitude, situation, advantages in the way of sunlight, and disadvantages in the way of wind, sweeping storms, and keen frosts. For this reason no two gardens are exactly alike. Being dissimilar, they must call for dissimilar treatment, and that treatment, to reach its highest success, must be the result of personal knowledge of the individual garden. Of tonne there are general principlrs that must be ipplied to all gardens and those who are responsible or our heal shows would naturally have much difficulty in judging otherwise than according to these general principles ; still, it is at least con- t eivable that the exhibits of some who have not the 'access of actual prize-winners may in a very real sense be not ',less meritoroua than those of more I wtunats rivals. WHY BATHE? To repeat a quotation from last week's " Notes," the declaration being that of a medical specialist, " We English people are not nearly so clean as we are supposed to be." Just so. Not very long ago, Mr. J. It. Newton, of Birkenhead, wrote to a contemporary —" I was engaged by this Corporation some years</p>
