      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</p>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL BUYS OLYMPIA.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenell, Bart.. C.V.O. has purchased Olympia, which has for long been the chief place in London for shows, exhibitions, tournaments and dis- plays on a large scale. The prcbase was arranged in June last by Sir Gilbert, and on its completion a private company was formed to carry on the business winch will be conducted on similar lines to those already existing. Associated with Sir Gilbert, who is chairman of the new company, are Sir Richard Cooper and Mr James Buchanan. The enterprise in its latest form thus enjoys the advantage of being under the direction of three gentlemen whose authority in regard to the breeding and exhibition of horses and cattle is universally recognised. Important organisations that have hitherto used Olympia for their annual displays such as the Royal Naval and Military Tournament, the Inter- national Horse Show, and the Motor Exhibilion—are giving Sir Gilbert and his confreres a most cordial support, and among the early changes to be expected is an improved railway service, via Addison-road,from all parts of the Metropolis. he new arrangements have been rendered possible in the complete form which they have now assumed owing to the recent death of the two principal share- holders,Sir Cuthbert Qu. liter and Mr. Walter Morrison. Shortly after Olympia. was first laid out, says the "Standard," there was, in 1889, an Irish exhibition, opened jointly by the Lord Mayor. of Dublin and the Lord Mayor tf London. Financially, it was not a i success, and as a result an indemnity fund had to be started two years later. Subsequently there was a series of more profitable and entertaining displays. Olympia was in turn a place for exhibiting the pictures rejected from the Royal Academy,. a modern repre- sentation of ancient Venice, with its gondolas and water carnivals ; Constantinople, complete with a bridge of boats across the Golden Horn ; " The Orient," a Turkish-Egyptian-Indian show ; Barnum and Bailey's 1,600 men and women performers and human monstrosities ; and Buffalo Bill's congress of the roughriders of the world. Not till within the last six or seven years, however, has Olympia been the home of the naval and military tmrnaments, the international horse shows, and the motor exhibitions which have made it successful and a popular rendezvous of society people. With its past half-dozen motor shows a wit has described it as the most " motorious " place in London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
