      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BULLS, &amp;c,</head>
        <p>BULLS, &amp;c,</p>
        <p>The amusing slip in a local auctioneer's sale bill somehow brought to my mind a bull perpetrated by a doctor not far from where I write. At a comparatively recent meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Council, a ceitain medicaloman complained of gutter spouting brought down by snow bei, g left in the streets. " Last night," he said, "1 fell headlong over some and injured my thumb. If I had broken my neck I should most certainly have commenced an action against the Council." Said another doctor: "Your husband, Mrs. Murphy, is suffering from a complicatioa of disemes. I must first make a diagnosis," and Mrs. Murphy responded: "Kin yer make it out of a bit of ould muslin, doctor? Oi haven't a bit of flannel in the house." There is a story of a man who on presentation to the Pope said "I'm proud to meet your Holiness. I had the honcur to know your father, the late Pope " Mrs. Earle, in her "Gardening for the Ignorant," tells of an old Scotch lady, who said to a minister who was admiring her flowers, " Ah, bit dcctor, ye ken the Latin names o' a' the flooers ; I ken bit twa, an' they are jist Aurora Borealis an' Delirium Tremens." WELL ENOUGH FOR ONCE.</p>
        <p>Last week's etoi y in the " Children's Corner" reminded me of one which might there be deemed out of place, but which may serve me by way of con- clusion here. It was a sultry day some years ago, a few of the thirstier souls on the staff of a certain newspaper were well nigh at their wit's end for a drink—and the wherewithal. Suddenly one of them had a brilliant inspiration, and invited his comrades to adjourn with him to their favourite "pub" in the assurance that all would be well. Perspiring but enheartened, they willingly accompanied, and presently, wondering and yet hope- ful they heard their leader thus address the landlord : „ We've a bit of a dispute on. There's a difference of opinion between one of these chaps and me as to whether one ought to say ' Gentlemen, what will -you have to drink,' or "Gentlemen what will you have to take," and we want you to decide between us." "That's soon done" said the unsuspecting landlord "Gentlemen, what will you have to drink ?' " The eager crowd promptly thanked their host in chorus, and each named his favourite "tipple." Boniface, handing the drinks out, laughingly admitted that they had " done " him, adding" It's the first and the last time, mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CRUST ALL OVER HER HEAD.</head>
        <p>CRUST ALL OVER HER HEAD.</p>
        <p>40, Gunner St, Kingston, Portsmouth, Hants, Eng.—" My little girl eight years old came home from school one day with a few spots on her head like watery blisters and when they broke it was in oue mass all over her head. She got so bad at last I could not take ber out for about three months. It was very irritating to her at night so we had to tie her hands up to prevent her from scratching. " 1 bought all sorts of ointment but they seemed to do her no good until I sent for a sample of Cuticura Ointment and the first time I used it I could see an improvement, it was in a crust all over her head but befoi e I had used one small sample of Cuticura Ointment it was beginning to get better. I bought two large boxes from the chemist and they completly cured her. I had to have all her hair off, but she has had no sign of the trouble since she was cured by Cuticura Ointment." (Signed) Mrs. E. Thresher, August. 11, 1011. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free frem nearest depot : Newbery, 211 Charterlicuse Sq London ; Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N. S. W. • Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; - , Muller, Maclean &amp; Co'., Calcutta and Bombay ; "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS. The St. Helens Corporation has appointed Mr Green- word, of Todmorden to be engineering Mr LI E Booth, of Rock Ferry, architectural assistant in the borough surveyor's department. assistant, and</p>
        <p>imMEMI</p>
        <p>e''s•rl,./‘ e•*-.-^'N,%•••e\ e•-",Ne''‘"•\,"••"•V CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>GOOD RESOLUTIONS FOR NURSES— AND FOR EVERYBODY. In Los Angeles there is a Training Home for Nurses and hanging on the wall there is •a printed card containing some resolutions, which they are asked to make and to observe. A friend of mine has sent these on to me and I am going to print them here in the hope that my readers may take them to heart : I will not permit myself to speak while angry. And I will not make a-bitter retort to another person who speaks to me in anger. I will neither gossip about the failings of another nor will I permit any other person to speak such gossip to me. Gossip will die when it cannot Ind a listener. I will respect weakness and defer to it on the street car, in the shops and in the home, whether it be displayed by man or woman. I will always express gratitude for any favour or service rendered to me. If pre- vented from doing it on the spot, then I will seek an early opportunity to give utterance to it in the most gracious way within my power. I will not fail to express sympathy with another's sorrow, or to give hearty appre- ciation of good works by another, whether the person be friendly to me or not. I will not talk about my personal ailments or misfortunes. They shall be one of the subjects on which I am silent. I will look on the bright side of the circum- stances of my daily life, and I will seek to carry a cheerful face and speak hopefully to all whom I meet. I will strive to be always prepared for the very best that can happen to me. I will seek to be ready to seize the highest oppor- tunity, to do the noblest work, to rise to the loftiest place which God and my abilities permit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LARGE CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>A LARGE CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>Orville Wright, the famous flying man said in a speech not long ago: "Our aim is to make flying safe. The important thing is not now to produce aeroplanes that will go 150 miles an hour, but to produce aeroplanes that won't fall down."</p>
        <p>Then Mr. Wright smiled. I'm reminded of a flying story," he went on; "a story about a man who called at a biplane selling_ageney and said:</p>
        <p>" 'My older brother bought a biplane here last month. You told him if anything broke you'd supply the missing parts.' Yes. Well?' said the manager. Well,' said the caller, my brother wants you to supply him right away with six new ribs, a left leg, half a dozen assorted fingers, and a medium-sized Roman nose.'" TAME ANIMALS I HAVE KNOWN. A thick fleeced lamb came trottirg by. " Pray whither now, my lamb ? " quoth I. " To have," sit he, with ne'er a stop, "My wool clipped off at the ba-ba shop." I asked the dog, " Why all this din?" Said he, " I'm fashioned outside in, And all my nights and days I've tried My best to get the bark outside." A hen was cackling haul and long. Said I to her, "'low strange your song ! She she, " "Lis scarce a song ; in fact, It's just a lay, to be eggs-act." I asked the cat, " Pray tell me why You love to sing." She blinked her eye. " My purr-puss, sir, as you can see, Is to a-mews myself," said she. I asked the cow, " Why don't you kick The man who whips you with a stick?" " Alas! I must be lashed," said she, "So I can give whipped cream, you see ! —NIXON WATERMAN.</p>
        <p>This little paragraph, which I cut from a newspaper last week will just suit my readers.</p>
        <p>TOMTIT AS LAMPLIGHTER.</p>
        <p>For several weeks past one of the public street lamps at Greenford, near Ealing, has been found alight every day, although the lamplighter extinguished the light each morning. The lamplighter set himself to capture the culprit, and found that a tomtit had built a snug nest in a corner of the lamp, and was in the habit of hopping on the ring attached to the chain of the incandescent by- pass, thus turning on the light.</p>
        <p>IF AND PERHAPS. G. C. STEBBINS. If everyone were wise anti sweet, And everyone were jolly; If every heart with gladness beat And none were melancholy; If none should grumble or complain, And nobody should labour In evil work, but each were fain To love and help his neighbour— Oh, what a happy world 'tvvould be For you and me—for you and me ! And if, perhaps, we both should try That glorious time to hurry; If you and I—just you and I Should laugh instead of worry ; If we should grow—just you and I Kinder and sweeter-hearted— Perhaps in some near by and by That good time might get started. Then what a happy world 'twould be For you and me—for you and me!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ALLEGED FAMILY THEFT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED FAMILY THEFT.</p>
        <p>Robert Twist. 30, Fisher-street, was on Tuesday remanded at St. Helens on a charge of stealing £3 in gold, and a cheque for .£5. the property of his step- brother, Hugh Lrster. Twist, it was stated, had been living at the same house as Lester. On Monday morn- ing he went cut early. It was found later that a drawer in the front room had been opened, Mr. Lester, sen., having left his keys on the table. Twist was arrested on a public-house bowling green, when be pointed out the place where he had put the cheque.</p>
