      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Abolition of Pauperism.</head>
        <p>Abolition of Pauperism.</p>
        <p>STATEMENT BY MR. LLOYD GEORGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George's attention having been called to statements by Mr. David Bloomfield, clerk of the South Manchester Board of Guardians, concerning the probable effect of the Insurance Act in diminishing pauperism, he has given the following interview to a correspondent : " I have seen Mr. Bloomfield's interesting statement, , and note that he says - I have no hesitation in expressing my belief that the National Insurance Act will do more to prevent and eventually to eradicate pauperism than all the Poor Law Acts on the Statute Book. "I have always felt confident," continued Mr. Lloyd George," that its operation must have the effect of relieving the rates. The evidence and the reports of the loyal Commission on the Poor Law make it abundantly clear that s;ckness is responsible for con- siderably more than half the pauperism of this country. I was informed by the Local Government Board the other day that consumption alone costs the rates about a million and a half per annum. "The National Insurance Act sets up a system by which a great national fund is raised for the purpose of helping the industrial classes of this country and pre i enting them in days of sickr ess from falling into the slough of pauperism. In addition to that it is one of the main objects of the Act not only to cure;but to prevent sickness. There are provisions in it which will undoubtedly have that effect. "Mr. Bloomfield is therefore perfectly right in assuming that the operation of this Act must neces- sarily have the direct effect of relieving ratepayers of a large proportion of the burden which sickness now casts on their shoulders. "In a few years the workhouse and the workhouse infirmary will be rrmoved entirely from the horizon of the industrious workman. The maintenance pro- vided in days of sickness by the Insurance Act and the institutions set up and subsidised out of its funds will be offered to the working man as substitutes for the workhouse and the workhouse infirmary - substitutes which will be consistent with the honour and dignity of labour. "The stain of pauperism will not attach to any of the benefits of the Insurance Act, and whatever any- one may say to-day about this measure, I predict with confidence that after a few years' experience of its working there will be no self-respecting citizen in this untry to utter one syllable in condemnation of the Act."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
