      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NEXT ELECTION BATTLE.</head>
        <p>NEXT ELECTION BATTLE.</p>
        <p>Fairplay at the Polls.</p>
        <p>The Postmaster-General, addressing the Cleveland Liberal Association, on Saturday, said that in al probability it would be two or three years before the again had an opportunity of meeting the enemy Before that, time there was reason to believe th Government would have effected many importan improvements in registration and electoral laws When that was done the Liberal party might look forward to having more fairplay at the polls than they had been able to command in the past.</p>
        <p>FOR WIIOSE BENEFIT? BISHOP OF AIANCEIE STER'S INDUSTRIAL QUERY.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Manoliestor, rddressing a large men's meeting in the Palace Theatre, Blackpool, on Sunday afternoon, said that the King had recently been taken down coal mines, through cotton mills, and elsewhere. But for whose benefit were great industries being carried on ? He would have to admit they were for the favoured few, while the great majority did not get more than half they deserved. (Applause.) Less enjoy- ent upon the labours of others was the need of to-day</p>
