      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>aria</head>
        <p>aria</p>
        <p>ee</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 10, 1912.</p>
        <p>tALL RIGIITS RESERVED j HER PRINCE.</p>
        <p>BY HAMILTON ORION.</p>
        <p>Moonlight, silvery as September stnlight, had enshrouded the earth with the purity of heaven. The mists of autumn had wet 'the grass, and sparkling drops of moisture hung on the petals of the flowers like tears dropped from angels' eyee. Horr Goshen's straight figure stood like a rook in the clear surroundings. His &amp;mo- tional face was looking upwards. His dark, passionate eyes were riveted on the beauty of the world without, and for the moment his thoughts were lost to the mansion which lay behind him—the mansion holding a little world within its fourwalls—a world of per- fume, and of paint and powder; of artifice and cruel charms; of satin slippers; and of jewelled hair; of untrue hearts and lives. The butterfly world, drinking from the cup of pleasure, as a bee drinks greedily- the honey from the flowers—La world in which home life meant nothing, and to the women of which motherhood had no sacred meaning. Herr Goshen lived in such a world, and he IN as of it, and yet he was ever conscious of a far greater world—of a far greater work which lay beyond the heated rooms, where, behind the scent of lilies and geraniums, he led his orchestra, and bowed to the -applause of a thousand lovely women. He was always striv- ing between two lives—the life lived for ap- preciation, and the greater life beyond it, where that part of his genius which too early a step to fame had pushed into the back ground could soar aloft, and where, no longer pandering to the public's whims, he could become the man that ho had dreamt be would be.</p>
        <p>For a few moments' rest during the inter- val, while his men were refreshing themselves with champagne and ices, he had strayed into the valley of solitude, sething his inspiration where hi always found it-:--amongst the trea- sures of Nature, and beneath the starlit heavens. His face, which in the full light had a touch of the coarseness of high living, was spiritualised by the beauty of the night. There was a smile lurking round his lips, lie was thinking of his music. He was no longer conductor now. He had the audience to. him- s2lf ; and once again, as his firm, light fingers swept the harp-strings, and his mag- netic eyes looked at the audience, he had that little world of women at his feet.</p>
        <p>Once again his proud heart was glowing over the small vanities of such a life; and, in spite of all, lie was swept back again by the stronger tide, and the flicker of the greater light far in the distancf.s. was washed away. He heard the soft approach of footsteps, and, almost before he knew it, a tiny hand, white as a sno‘vflake, was laid upon his arm. Of course, his whereabouts had been dis- covered. In spite Of all our loneliness in life, solitude when most desired is the rarest jewel we can find. But the first bird that calls us from our sleep, to tell us by its trilling note that darkness has broken and we must meet the dawn, could nct have broken more sweetly on this moonlit solitude than the woman who stood before him.</p>
        <p>Herr Goshen' s hat was in his hand. His bow as nearly to the ground, as he recog- nised in the small, slight figure, clad in the soft white clinging costume, with the golden hair falling loosely about her dimpled neck, the niece of the owner of the mansion--.-Lady Sylvia. Pretty and fair as a flower she looked after the women he had mixed with. Her great childish eyes, which held no secrets, turned fearlessly to his. The whole trust and con- fidence of her soul was in her face; and, as the little hands went out to him, Herr Goshen too&amp; them naturally—as naturally as he had taken the hands of many a beautiful wo- man in the past, on which he seldom cared to reflect.</p>
        <p>"I came to thank you for your music," she said, hurriedly; and he felt the little hands tremble as she spoke, and saw the rapid rise and fall of the hothouse flowers, sprayed upon her bosom. "Oh, that last thing the orchestra played! It made me want to cry, and cry my heart out. The room was hot and suf- focating; no one in there could shed a tear, and so I came away to be alone. I slipped away before they missed me—for, somehow, no one semis to cry but me. They say that it is childish. They do not understand. And sr:- when I long most to cry, I come out here; for Nature always sympathises with our moods— and have you noticed how she often cries for company? Herr Goshen was looking meditatively at her. In rapid lines he as pencilling out a map of her character with the quick intui- tion of the artist, sharpened by years ol practice in the world. "It is to Nature I had to come to-night for inspiration," he said, kindly. "Her moods are far more suited to my own than man's. There never was a sympathiser like her. Look how the little floWers droop their beads when you are dull and lonely • and when you are feeling good and sweet, they laden all the air with sweetest perfume. No woman's tears could drop more gently than the dew ; and in the touch of the gentle breeze we feel the sympathetic kiss of an unknown sympathiser— the hidden angel coming with soothing to the tired brain and heart."</p>
        <p>"How beautifully you put it murmured Lady Sylvia. "You make the poetry of night seem far more real when you talk hike that. There is no one else to see it as you have clone. They call it foolish when I dream of lovely things; and when I talk of all the mys- teries of life they do not understand. They say that I am young, and do 'net know the world. They want to make the moonlight look dark and cold. They 'want to take the glitter from the smashine. They say I on look at life through a mirror—that my vision of it is perverted—and they want to turn me round ta see it all another way." Herr Goshen had listened with amusement, which years of acting had taught him to con- ceal; but as the girl went on an unusual ex- pression came into his eyes, softening their fiery depths with a softness near to pity. "Poor lonely Hite child!" he thought. "The dreamer that I was years ago—before that other life blotted my best dreams into mist, and made me the man I was—the man I am—and not the man that God intended me to be." Again his expression changed, and, step- ping nearer to Lady Sylvia, he said: "In this life dreams are little use, my child. Most of us begin with them when we are children. We toddle into the world, and our baby hands are quick to build our castles in the air. At first we cry as we watch them fall to pieces—but, being only children, and full of hope, we pick up the scattered bricks and begin again. We leave off crying soon, and soon we leave off building, and we are content to SC/3 the ruins of our castles trampled under foot. You have your castles still, and they are cobwebbed in the air like fairy visions;_ but leave off building—let the castles go. They will only fall in the end and when they fall disillusionment will cad' den and hurt you." She was listening breathlessly. Her hands had left his now, and were clasped upon her breast. Her great eyes—from which the light of a woman's untempted soul looked out—were fixed on hl3 as if a magnetic focus held them there.</p>
        <p>"I had my castles once," he continued, "but they have gone long stnee, and I am letting out of the present what happiness I can. You do the came, little flower of the desert. We etViwiot be isolated in this life. We cannot make our own world, so the best thing for 1113 to (10 i 5 to • rub along as well as we can with this world—to take people as we find them, to try to wear a thicker skin, and harden ourselves to scratches." He drew out his watch hurriedly. It was time for him to return to his orchestra; but he was loth to tear himself away from the mystery of the night, and the witchery of an original woman. "I have always had my castles," she said. corny. I liv«I away tight in the country at the convent. I had never s2en the world. until one day a rich relation died and left me heiress of thii 1'111(.2. They told me then I need no longer dream, that the ra6tlea I had lived in had found ground at last. But whet my guardian came• and ,brougo/ me nere to wealth and, luxury, I foluid I needed my castle more than ever then. I wanted to get away</p>
        <p>longing gratinea, and perhaps because I long for love so much that is the reason why I do not get it." She looked down wistfully' at her hand as she. spoke, and, for the first time, Herr Goshen noticed the glittering diamond circle an the third finger. "I am going to be married soon," she con tinned, as wistfully as a child would have said "I am going to leave home for board- ing-school." "lam marrying my guardian. you Bee, they all wish for it so. And, after all, why should not I, for every woman marrigs? It is her lot in life, they say. Sometimes She finds her Prince and she is happy; but oftener, far oftener, her Prince will never come, or perhaps he comes too late. My Prince will never come. I know it. I meet him in my dreams, but he is gone with day- light.". "And the Prince of your dreams is ydfir ideal," he said, quietly. "You know you will not meet him in this world—but why not keep true to hid beautiful memory? If be never Comes to you, if silence never speaks, you will have the joy of knowing that you were faith- ful to that memory. If out of tte silence the voice comes at last, it will be perfectly beau- tiful if your soul and mind are ready for it." They walked back towards the house to- gether. The interval was over, the music must commence. Sylvia returned to her guests; Herr Goshen took his place in the orchestra— but his music was now for one alone.</p>
        <p>• When Lord Robertahaw met his inteilded wife the next morning he noticed a change in her. She was always a beautiful girl, but it seemed this morning as if a cloud which had hidden half her charms had rolled away. The dreamy look had gone out of her eyes. There was_ an unusual radiance in their depths. There was lees of the girl about her—more of the promice of the woman she would beedne in a few years time. Lord Robertshaw looked at her with pride, but not with love. The spirit of love, if there over had been one, was stifled—swamped within his heart. He had had an unhappy pact, and it had embittered his nature. He believed in no woman truly, but something in the innocence and svveetnecs of this dreamy, unpractical girl pleased him, and made him want to have her for his oWn. They kissed with no love, no warmthrtio passion. "White suits you, Sylvia," he said, simply, as he looked her up and down. "You are a pretty girl when you dreEs with taste. I have arranged for us to go bunting this morning, and for the afternoon I have an entirely 'dif- ferent programme. Herr 'Goshen has invited us to accompany him to the island." . .</p>
        <p>The faintest wave of colour swept her cheeks, and her eyes lit up as he said the words. "It seems, as well as a musician, he is a botanist like myself. He has recently dis- covered a water-plant upon the rocks, and he, wants my opinion on it. Now get on your riding-habit, and we will start for the field." She went like a submissive child in obedi- ence to his order. Lord Robertshaw held her will within hie hand ; he twisted it round his finger as easily as he would have twisted a luck of her silky hair. She did not care for hunting, she would, rather have stayed in* with her music and her books. She would rather have wandered amongst the flowers and dreamt her favourite dreams'. But his word to her was always law, and because he knew she would ever regard it as such he wanted her for his constant cbmpanion. He had Geen much of the tyrannical woman who believed in woman's rights' and fought for them; but he wanted only the woman who would lie be- neath his feet—the woman who would never struggle with his power. They spent the morning in the hunting field, and in the afternoon they met Herr Goshen on the shore. The boat was ready. They were about to start, when a boy in bright uniform carrying an orange-coloured envelope came hurrying towards them. Lord Robertshaw seized it et ' urerly and read in 9 pencilled words on the thin pink paper of the death of his brother. "It means my leaving at once,", he said, quickly. "I eannot go to the island to-day." Sylvia had rushed towards him with the first show of sympathy and love she had ever ventured to give, but he pushed her hurriedly aside and pulled out hie watch. "Five minutes off train time," he said. "I shall be able to do it," and he was gone like a dash.</p>
        <p>Herr Goshen stood in the middle of the boat looking after him. Then he turned to Sylvia and held out his hand. "Come, sucet wood elf," he said, gently. "The eert-nymphe ore calling 116 from the islond." She hesitated, and drew back, half afraid, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>from everyone, nod lock myself up there, and dream about the world you tell of in your music. And yet," she added, stretching out her little hands, "what could there be more beautiful than this? Yes, I have got ray castle, but it doesn't satisfy." As Herr Goshen looked and listened, he realised the only castle that a woman of this kind could make her home in—the only one which would give her refuge in life's batter- ing storms—and it 6 he realised it a strong temptation overcame him to show to her fresh young mind the beauty of this castle, to take her through the locked, doors into the sun- light of the locked rooms, and there to leave her.</p>
        <p>"Shall I tell you why you have not found your castle?" he asked quickly. And as he spoke the passion which ran through all his IllUt3iC glittered in his eyes, and deepened the soft notes of his mellow voice. "Shall I tell you why, like the poor little dove above the waters, you fly aimlessly on and on, always believing you see the hoped-for goal, but never reaching it? You want something which money will never buy—something outside the golden cage which cramps the wings from liberty—something which seldom lives . in, a castle, but makes its home in the strangest and met unlikely places. You want that something which makca you mount aloft on pinioes, and carries you. far beyond the smoke and dust and grime of sordid life. You want the arms of love around you—real love, strong, paesiono.te love—the love which, though of earth, soars up into the heavens. You have never felt a love like that." "I have felt it in my dreams," she said; "but it has made me feel so lonely when I wakened and found that lif 2 won real, and there was another day to go through—another long, long day a nothing. Yes, it has often come to me in dreams, but I have never felt it in life—never. We cannot have all our</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>but hie firm hand elbeed on nere, and he drew her over the side of the boat.</p>
        <p>They were out on the sea together. The cold wind was blowing on their faces; the sun was shining; sea and sky were blue as sapphires•, the seagulls' wings shewed white against the dark cliff, and like a green emerald in the distance lay the Wand to which they were steering. Both were supremely happy—she, Demise her long- dreamt dream was real at let—he, with the happy contentment of n selfish man revelling in the beauties of nature and the charms of woman. They wandered like children over the island, picking up the pink ohelle; holding up the brown oeaweed to the light; chasing the crabs which the waves had washed ashore; pulling the oyster-shells from the tangles of weed, to which they clung like jewels fastened in the shorn hair of dead sea-nymphs. They found the flower they scorched for, but re- verently left it growing. They explored the mysteries of the cave, until the sun began to sink and the tide to rise fast around the island.</p>
        <p>- . "It is time we went back," said Herr Gcshen, looking at his watch. "But— Good Heavens! What hos got the boat?" The boat had broken from its mooringa; it had drifted far out to sea.</p>
        <p>"The water does not cox'er the island," he said, reassuringly, when he caw her fright- ened face. "It only wenn our waiting until we can hail a paseing sail. Life would be dull without adventure. Are you afraid to stay with me alone?"</p>
        <p>"Afraid?" She turned her brimming eyes to him—eyes in which the light of an un- uttered secret burnt—and stretched forth both her little hands. "Afraid? Why should I be afraid of you?" she asked. "You have been so good to me, and yet I have only known you so short a time." "Does it only seem abort?" he gently said.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
