      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>TIIE DIJNMOW FLITCIT.</head>
        <p>TIIE DIJNMOW FLITCIT.</p>
        <p>The ancient Dunmow flitch ceremony, was revived •on Bank Holiday, and-thousands of people from all parts of the country met to witness the contest of the married couples for the matrimcinial prize. The flitch is given to the couple wlt9, are prepared to take oath that since they were married they have never</p>
        <p>offended each other by word or deed, or wished themsehies unmarried. Our photo- graph shows the winning couples carried in procession; the flitches of bacon are seen behind. The first couple are Mr. and Mrs. Smith, of Derby; and the second are Mr. and Mrs. Butcher, of Brixton.</p>
        <p>tier eyes were lOoking out to sea. lne dreamy look hod come to them again. "No:" she replied, half to herself (laid half aloud. "It seems tut if I had known you all my lift</p>
        <p>as if I had known you in my dreams, but I had only been waiting for you to come to me in reality. I was lonely, I was cold, I was desolate; and then you came, and the world was resurrected. I wish .it could end here," she continued—'' just with this happy realisa- tion of the dream. Bat it will be over with the passing boat, and I shall Lave to go back to my world again and you to yours." She shivered slightly, and he picked up her fallen cloak, placed it -en her shoulders, and stood regarding har through halfclesed eyes. Small child of nature, how beautiful she looked upon that lonely island—like some bright spirit risen from the sca, her soft hair blown about her face, her dress wind-swept, clinging whitely round her form. Child of innocence —child of ignorance unknowin3 temptress to the man who longed for one brief moment to take her in his arms, and in that moment let her live through ht. ppinees such as irony creatures spend their lives in searching for. but never find.</p>
        <p>'The Wind began to rise higher and blow around the island. Where the sun had set the moon had risen, and the sea was shimmering in its silver light. A huge wave smashed like a bubble on the cliff, and covered her dress with spray. She looked towards him like a frightened child, and held out her hands for protection. Fel- a moment he forgot every- thing, but that he was alone with her upon the island—that she was beautiful, and that she loved him with the first passion of 4 young love. The time of night, the mystery of the situation; the charm of the girl, over- came him, and he held out his arms to her. An intoxicating sense of bliss overcame her as she sank into them, and felt his tender kisses on her cheAr.</p>
        <p>"Poor little child!" he said, gently, draw- ing her down on the rocky.seat by the cave. "You are frightened. There—lean against me, little one. Let us be happy while vie can. You have found what you were searching for at last. Poor little bird, shut up in a cage of gold—you have broken through the bars, and got your freedom!" She fell asleep, with her head upon his breast, her fair young fare turned up towards the sky, her beautiful bosom heaving gently with each breath like a child in its first sleep of innocence. And the man of the world looked out towards the, sea, lost—deeply lost —in thought. The waves rose higher and higher. She stirred in hr Icep, and started at their swishing sound, but smiled when she saw his face.</p>
        <p>"Is not it beautiful?" she murmured. "What, sweetheart?". he asked, softly. "Oh, everything," she answered--" the WIITCS, the moonlight; the wind, and you." his grasp tightened round her, his lips were touching hers. "Listen to me, little one," he whispered. "This is only a fore- taste of love; this is but a peep through a half-closed door. , The great, broad, unlimited valley lice beyond us. Your little feet have never trodden there, but, by your ,will, they shall yet tread. I cannot promie2 you life-long renunciation. A past 'too black for sensitive eyes like yours prevents it—but I can promise you the happiness you have craved for. Will you come with me, little sweetheart? Will you risk the bitter dregs of the cup, for the sake of the sweet essenoe flow ing at the top?" She listened, 'and her breath came fast, in hysterical sobs. There was the sound of the splash of oars in the distance. Herr Goshen sprang to his feet, and a voice answered his call. The passing sail had come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>.tll.</head>
        <p>.tll.</p>
        <p>Lady Sylvia was dressed or the opera, and her maid regarded her with satisfaction as she pinned the spray of pink geranium near to the full white throat. She had noticed a. change in her mistress these last few days, and she went down to remark on it to the ser- ;tante</p>
        <p>"It isn't his Lordship that she's going to the opera with to-night," she said—" his Lordship's in the library—it's another gentle- man—that foreigner that's ,been so much here lately. It's a wonder his Lordship isn't jeal- ous, for her Ladyship was never better-look- ing than she is now; and hen a woman grows suddenly good-looking—nell, there's generally a cause for it. Lord Rohertshaw was not uneasy. lie had allowed Herr Goshen to come as he would to the castle. He had allowed Sylvia to go with him where she liked., "Cast every temptation in a woman's way," was his motto, "and let her prove her virtue. The untried woman is not virtuous at all." But he did not know how sensitive were the little feet he had placed the net for.</p>
        <p>In the closed carriage Lady Sylvia drove away by the side of the only man who had brought the sunshine into her uneventful life. They had taken the path by the sea. The tide was high, and the moonlight was shining upon the inwashing waves as if a white angel of purity had, over all, spread out her shining wings. It was the sight of this that awche the girl from her dream. '2lie vision came to her with startling reality. The prayer of St. Agnes had been unconegiously her prayer from birth. She bad wandered to the edge of the precipice, like one drugged by a powerful %narcotic; and now, as she opened her eyes te the depths beneath her, she started back, and, with a frightened cry, reached out both hands to the man who had led her blindfolded there.</p>
        <p>"What'are you going to do with me?" she asked. "I cannot marry you—l have given my word to another man." "Marry me!" he. answered, as he took her hands in his. "0 Heaven !—that I could offer marriage to you! A past too black pre- vents that, child. What can I offer you for your self-renunciation? What I have offered scores of others—a few weeks of delirious happiness, and then the darkness. That is all; but to many it is worth it." She shivered from head to foot, like a cen- sitive snoadrop struck by an icy wind. "Take me back to him," she murmured. "I gave my word of honour—l must be his wife." Her golden head had dropped upon her hands, but he raised it gently and kissed the tender little face. Then, quite suddenly, he pushed away the clinging little hands, and save orders to the coachman to drive back.</p>
        <p>Like a guilty thing Lady Sylvia crept up the wide staitcase of her own home, and, un- noticed, entered her bedroom. The blinds were up, and through the great bay windows the pure moonlight fell in silver sheets. It was very beautiful, but oh, how cold! For One moment she fell on her, knees, and buried her face in her hands. Then, strong, brave, and deterinined, she rose to her feet, and went softly downstairs. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Lord Robertshaw was seated at the table. He was writing, and he looked up impatiently at the interruption. She did not advance towards_ him—she stood at the bottom of the table, her white opera cloak falling off her bare shoulders, her hands clasped in intensity of feeling. "Aubery, forgive me!' she burst forth. "I have deceived you—you, who J would rather have died than have wronged—you, who are to be 'my husband." "Well?" he said, with icy gravity, as she paused. She came a few steps nearer, and faoed him bravely. "I told you I was going to the opera to-night, and I deceived you; but that was not all. I have been untrue to you. I forgot my promise—l forgot my pledge—and —I went with Herr Goshen." She stretched out.her arms towards, him as she said the words. There was eloquence enough in eyes and attitude to touch a harder heart than his; but he was not looking at her. "Where did you go?" he asked, shortly. though his heart was beating to suffocation as be awaited her reply. "We took the path by the sandhills," she replied. "The moon was up, and the water and the sands were white—and—and—then I • came back. Oh, Attbery, I cannot bear it! Let me go." She rushed towards the Poor, but he was there before her, and had placed his back againk. it.</p>
        <p>„ "Listen to me,” he cried, and he caught her wrists in a grip that hurt. "Do you think I have been blind to your doings all these weeks? Why did not I marry you at the be- ginning? Why did not I marry you when I brought you from the convent here—a swoet, untried, and unaffected girl? If I had eitirr4.l •10 honimmtaxo uu gum ‘puv ' „'utoril ;co q 60.1 eq; colt; ektu no.[ -2trlLllou Jo; op; Stu pull ;on am; i uatuom motti; I 'go •pui; It ;pun o; no quuottt puu 's.taug Sapling brit qjit no patiano; .timatt put; tioiltiuitua,i, •isag no 0.t0.n1 o; paiuumi -on lna ouop emu; vinous I nag; 'Jamul .tnoS Jo lista 2su4 eul ;no itsgust, he flung her from him and returned to his seat. "Oh Aubery "—her cry was like that, of a struck child—" I never meant to be untrue; but his love fascinated me. It did not alter my, feeling for you one bit, only Only you loved him mope?" "Yea," she faltered.</p>
        <p>"Then why in the name cf Heaven did ycru return to me?" he asked. "Why did you not finish as you had begun?" "Because I had given my word to you," she said. "And oh! Aubery, if you would bot take me to your heart and love me now, I would be good—l would be so tender with you, Aubery. No other man should be to me like you. I am dying for love. Aubery, kiss mo just this once, and tell me you forgive mc." She had flung her arms about him Her cheek, soft with all the romidnese of girlhood, was laid against his own, and he could feel it wet with tears. He 'Was very nearly losing self-control and taking her to his heart; but because lie was a man, and ashamed of the emotion that had swept over him like an en- gulfing wave, he pushed her roughly from him. She lay at his feet like a fallen angel— bid he set his teeth against her bitter cry, and went from the room.</p>
        <p>"Lord Robertshaw—Lady Sylvia." It wae close on midnight. Aubery was pon- dering over the library fire, when the' maid rushed unceremoniously in upon him. "Lady Sylvia," she continued, with startled eyes—"she was not well when 6110 went to bed. She complained of faintness, and now— Oh, my Lord, come to her!" He followed up the stairs into the bedroom. Sylvia was seated in her dressing-gown by the open window. The sudden spasm of pain had passed, but her face was white, and her eyes turned hopelessly towards her betrothed. "Sylvia, what is it?" he asked, with mi. usual gentleness as he went up to her. "It is death, I think," she answered, weakly. He took her bands in his and massagerl them strongly, bringing back again the dying circulation. "It is not death," he answered; and' be kissed her. "It is life, little, girl—life and love, Sylvia—sweetheart. I have never loved a woman truly in my life—l have believed Ahem all to be alike. But you came back to rne. I cannot forget that. I was not your ideal, but you came back to me, and if you will love me now I will love you with the love of a man who, like you, has been searching for his ideal. Oh! how weary have been those forty years of disappointment, but all are atoned for to-night. Sylvia, my quest is ended—my ideal is in you. She gave her two hands into his, stainlees and pure. She laid her cheek against his own. She surrendered herself—her dreams—to him Ind in him her ideal was realised—for every man is man from firet to last, and it is only troman'b eyesight that Leaked him argeL</p>
        <p>, ITEA isND.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>– EIGHT -HOUR DAY. --- INTERNATIONAL AGREEMENT NECEsSARY.</head>
        <p>- EIGHT -HOUR DAY. --- INTERNATIONAL AGREEMENT NECEsSARY.</p>
        <p>The Special Commission appointed by the Inter national Association of Labour Legislation (on which Mr. A. H. Crosfield is one of the English .representa- tives) to inquire into the hours of labour in continuous trades issued its report on Saturday. The Commission metin London in June. The Commission claims to have proved that a reduction of hours can be, in reality, economically profitable from the manufacturer's point cf view. Nevertheless such is the fear of foreign competition, they state, that a general introduction of the eight hour day is unlikely without the backing of an interna- tional agreement, by which the same standard may be introduced simultaneously in alrcompeting atatos. The Commission condemns 12-hour shifts, and recommends the acicption c.f an international treaty to enforce the eight-hour day in continuous industries, especially in the iron and steel trades.</p>
