      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE CARD EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>An instance in which an insurance card served to vindicate a man's character occurred at the Widnes Police Court on Monday. A labourer named Andrew McCann was charged with theft, and the prosecution, to substantiate the charge, alleged that he was a worth- less fellow who did nothing but lounge about street corners and would not work.—Prisoner indignantly refuted the charge, and, to confirm his denial, produced his insurance card bearing two of the regulation stamps. —The Bench accepted his proof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>On the a hole, the weather at Widnes on Bank Holiday was favourable to holiday enjoyment. Several of the works having temporarily closed down, and tradesmen having suspended business for the day, the inhabitants were afforded a chance of spending the day out of town. Special hand performances were pro- vided at the Promenade and Victoria Park. During the afternoon excellent suort uas witnessed in the yacht race under the auspices of the Widnes Mersey Yacht Club, which was keenly contested. The competitors were in close proximity during the whole course. Ilow- ever, when rounding up for the finish, the Red Rose, of Widnes, drew out and won handsomely. The Sykia was mooed, closely followed by the Sirdar. Other amuse- ments in duck hunts and swimming races were provided and met, with approval. RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL. SUNDAY TRADERS' PETITION. At the Runcorn Urban Council on Tuesday night the Parliamentary Committee reported having considered the petition from two Sunday traders, Messrs. Horrocks and Henderson, asking the Council to communicate with the Standing Joint Committee with a view to stopping the prosecutions each week. The committee stated that the enforcement of the law as to Sunday closing being outside the duties and powers of this Council, it is recommended that the letter lie on the table." The resolution was confirmed without comment. _ -</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the secretary to the Salt Union stating that at a meeting of his board a cordial vote of thanks had been passed to the Council by his directors for the valuable assistance given in support of the opposition to the Brine Bill. 'lle action taken by the Council was none the less appreciated inasmuch as they, like the Salt Union, would have to bear a portion of the costa incurred by the promoters of the bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DROWNED AT RUNCORN. OPEN VERDMT ON A HOMELESS IRON-</head>
        <p>DROWNED AT RUNCORN. OPEN VERDMT ON A HOMELESS IRON-</p>
        <p>MOULDER.</p>
        <p>An open verdict of.'" Found drowned" was returned at an inquest in Runcorn Courthouse on Tuesday on William Robert Redhead, an ironmoulder, of Blackburn, whose body was recovered from the Bridgewater Canal last Friday. James Redhead, of Blackburn, said his brother had been wandering about the country for the last two years. A lodging-house keeper said the deceased had stayed at the lodging-house for a week, and had been drunk every day. On the Saturday he had obtained work at Messrs Timmin's foundry, and went to work on Monday morning, but after obtaining a shilling from the fore- man left the works and was not seen there again. He was last seen by the lodging-housekeeper on Wednesday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE NEXT MAYOR OF ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At the close of the meeting of the St. Helens Town Council on Wednesday, Alderman Forster presented a requisition signed by all the members of the Council to Councillor James Crooks asking him to accept the office of Mayor. Councillor Crooks replied briefly and promised to undertake the office. Councillor James Crooks, J.P., is one of the most popular members of the Council. He has been coun- cillor for the Central Ward since 1902. He is chairman of the Gas Committee, an overseer for the township, and since 1903 has been on the roll of borough magis- trates. He is a native of St. Helens, but has resided for some time at Eccleston Park. He is a prominent member of the Conservative party. HOW BANK HOLIDAY WAS SPENT. Most of the townspeople having left for pleasure resorts, Bank Holiday was very quietly spent in St. Helens. Owing to the bad weather during the week- end, the Lowe House and Holy Cross schools field-day and sports at Denton's Green have been postponed until to-day, but • several other field-days were held. The children and Bible classes in connection with Holy Trinity Church, Parr-mount, bad a successful outing at Sherdley Park. The Congregational schools, with the men and women's classes, walked in procession to the Town Hall, where several competitions were carried out. The company afterwards returned to school for tea, followed by games in a field off Kin-lane, Eccleston.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS EDUCATION. NEW APPOINTMENTS. The St. Helens Higher Education Committees has appointed Mr C Parsons, 8.A., of Liscard, to be mathe- matical master of Cowley Secondary School, at a salary of £2OO a year ; and the following to be form masters : —Messrs A A C Burton, B.A. (Oxford), A Bury, B.A. (Cambridge and Dublin),and B Walker,B.A. (Liverpool). The committee has received sanction of the Board of Education for the equipment of a mechanical oogineer- ing and electrical laboratory at the Gamble Institute, and has decided to apply for a £I,OOC loan for the purpose. The Children's Care Committee has drcided to hold a joint, meeting with the Health Committee to go into the question of establishing a tuberculosis dispensary in connection with the proposed school clinic. The Sutton Manor Colliery Company has given notice of its intention to erect a school in the neighbourhood of Clock Face, to accommodate 700 children, and in the meantime the company intends to carry on a temporary school at lloughdale's Farm. CHIEF CONSTABLE'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>- ----- ADVANCE OF £ICO A YEAR AT ST. HELENS. There was a lengthy and animated discussion on Wednesday at the meeting of the St. Helens Town Council on a proposal of the Watch Committee that the salary of the Chief Constable, Mr, Enaringtaa, at present £4OO with allowances, £75 for house, etc., and £lO for uniform be £175, to be further increased by two annual increments of £25 to £525. Alderman Pilkington said Mr. Ellerington richly dererved the increase. He had improved the police force, he did exceedingly well during the coal strike. and the force was economically worked.</p>
        <p>, Mr Forshaw (Labour) moved that the resolution be referred back as there was no justification for the arguments advanced. The reason they had peace during the coal strike was not because there was such a person as the Chief Constable but because of the miners them- selves, and if anybody deserved an increase of wages they did. Other Councillors followed, and the amendment was defeated. Mr Forshaw then proposed a further amendment that only one increment ba given, and this was agreed to, making the Chief Constable's ultimate salary £5OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>THE TERRIFIED GOLIATH.</p>
        <p>'hich the Government are preparing has already struck terror into --ty and the great Laud Moropolists.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON'S DEMAND FOR ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>GRIEVANCE AGAINST THE BOARD OF TRADE. In the House of Commons on Wednesday Lord Wolmer raised the question of the refusal of the Board of Trade to give Newton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council a provisional order for the manufacture and distribution of electricity in their district. In 1903, he said, the Council had an order for this purpose, but on their coming to the opinion that there was a danger of a financial loss they did not avail themselves of it, and it was withdrawn by the Board of Trade in 1909. This year, when they applied for a new order, it was refused by the Board on the ground that it would not be a pay- ing proposition, and that the order having been once given and not used there was no reason to give it again. But the fact that the Council did not avail themselves of the first order showed that they, as business men. were not anxious to speculate, and the conditions had entirely changed. lhe population had increased largely. New works were coming into the district, and, more important than anything else, the cost of manufacture of electricity was now mucn less than it WAS six or seven years ago. Mr. J. M. Robertson (Parliamentary Seeretary to the Board of Trade) congratulated Lord Wolmer on the pertinacity with which he had advocated this matter. The Board of Trade could not, however, recognise that mere pertinacity could override principle. Lord Wolmer had asserted that the Council bad every facility for the manufacture of electricity, but that was not so. There was no facility for a good and cheap water supply, which was very important in these matters. The Local Government Board absolutely concurred with the Board of Trade as to the inexpediency of granting an order. 'There was a cheap gas supply in the district, and there was no general demand for elec- tricity. If, however, there was at any future time an indication that a certain number of consumers were prepared to take the electricity, the order might be reapplied for. Under the circumstances he had described I there would be a risk of insolveney,snd it was the duty of the Board of Trade to prevent any such risks being taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</p>
        <p>Some Notes from Prestatyn.</p>
        <p>One hundred and twenty-nine happy boys assembled soon after nine o'clock on Saturday morning at the Drill Hall, Legli-street, bound for the summer camp at Prestatyn.The officers in charge,Messre. Firth, England Wainwright, Mee, Phillips, Parsonage,dnd Groark. and Mrs. Firth—promptly appeared on the scene. The Commander-in-Chief (Mr. Firth), then had the boys martialled in order, four abreast, and in that forma- tion they marched off to the station, boarding the 10.5 train which started at 10.40. A rapid run was made to Prestatyn, only one stop, at Chester, taking place, and we reached Prestatyn at 11.45. Lining up again at Prestatyn (which some of the boys facetiously term, press-my-bat-in), we made for the camp which is about a mile and a quarter from the station. The camp, like Mount Zion, is beautiful for situation, though in this case it is in a field at the foot of the sandhills, by the silver sea. Realising that order is " Heaven's first law," and therefore worthy of copying here, Commander Firth had matters quickly arranged. Each officer was placed in charge of three huts. We quickly improvised a post office, bank, shop. &amp;c., as most of the boys had received monetary presents from their aunts, uncles, cousins, parents, &amp;c. The boys, to use their own words, have a "ripping '; time of it, and when in the camp cricket, football and swinging are indulged in by many to their hearts' content. It is a dry field—plenty of grass with a sandy foundation. Saturday was fine. Sunday was exceptionally wet and, as one described it in a postcard to his mother, " It rained heavens hard." We were anxious for the boys, but all went through it well, as the huts are rain proof. The Roman Catholics went to service in the morning. Monday was a grand day and we took the boys a walk through the pretty village of Meliden and round to Prestatyn. Tuesday was also fine, so in the morning we took all the boys to Dyserth Waterfall, over the hills and through a wood, where most of them indulged in a little " nutting." In the afternoon the boys bathed along with some of the officers. At night we arranged an impromptu concert, the boys supplying the artistes, and we discovered some really good talent. All received a small prize. There were 11 artistes.</p>
        <p>The above will give some of our readers an idea of what goes on daily in and about the camp, and parents may rest assured that their boys are well looked after. The food is good and there is an abundance of it. Commander Firth is thoroughly at home in dealing with boys. The officials all pull well together. doing their share of work heartily, and the boys are always attended to first. The health of the camp is good. Habits of cleanliness are encouraged and rewarded daily. As for the hut which is considered the worst kept, the boys in it have to walk round the camp in single file, carrying their beds &amp;c. This creates great fun as most of the boys march in procession behind them, singing derisive songs, &amp;c. That the boys are happy is evidenced by some of the postcards which they send home. The following are samples. : " Dear Mother,—We reached in safety, and we are going on all right. Maurice is getting fE.tter every minute. Please send me 3d.—Yours lovingly, Sx." " Dear Mother,—l think it is a very nice time. There is a swing. There are IS huts. My hut is so, 1. We are surrounded by hills and close to the sea. On our way we saw many coal mines.—Your loving son-7." May I add in conclusion that the Camp is a credit to Warrington and its subscribers. Any reader who has anything to spare cannot do much better than send some of it now to Mr. J. A. Ransome, the hon. treasurer, and to Mr. Jenkins, the hon. secretary. The camp is worthy of support, for to some of the boys it is an " educatioo " of the highest kind, and they are sure to profit by it. It is the first time many of them have gazed on the sea and the mountains. E. E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>JUDGE'S TRIBUTE TO STRIKERS.</head>
        <p>JUDGE'S TRIBUTE TO STRIKERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mien J. Lqwrie, in his address to the Grand Jury at London Sessions on Tuesday, pointing out that the calendar contained the names of ninety-seven prisoners, said the list was exceptionally light com- pared with past calendars. It was very gratifying that, notwithstanding the labour troubles and the consequent distress, crimes of larceny and violence arising from the strike had been comparatively few. He thought this showed that while the British workman was in employment he laboured honestly, and when he was not working he did not hastily resort to crime in order to supply his wants.</p>
        <p>'elsh Disestablishment. We are glad to notice, says St. Paul's Parish Magazine, what a large number of our people, and especially the Welsh people, have signed this petition against this iniquitous Bill. It behoves all Churchmen to be on the alert and watch very carefully the interests of the Church. Churchmen who are worthy of the name must put their religion first on this great question. The property belongs to 'he Church and ought to be as sacred to us as we wish our own property to be. No Government has any right whatever to touch it. Pro- tect your own rights and the rights of your children in this great crisis.</p>
        <p>tatyboattifeTt.M. 11; c _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Bishop on Bell Ringing.</head>
        <p>Bishop on Bell Ringing.</p>
        <p>About 600 members of tbe Chester Diocesan Guild!of Bell-ringers at Chester on Monday celebrated ,the twenty-fifth anniversary of the organisation which was founded in 1887 by the Archdeacon of Chester. Preach- ing at a special service in the Cathedral in the morning the Bishop of Chester said if bell-ringing was a good thing, and a thing to be recognised among the ministra- tions of the Church, bell-ringers ought to give of their very best. If they had a full peal of bells, and even if they had skilful ringers, and there was not in the belfry a spirit of reverence worthy of the high calling of bell-ringers, then the downfall was a sad and lamentabe one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Tubular Bells for Christ Church.</head>
        <p>Tubular Bells for Christ Church.</p>
        <p>Christ Church, Latchford, Magazine says : A faculty has been applied for at the Chester Consistory Court for the removal and disposal of the existing bell and the erection in its place of eight tubular bells. The next Court will not be held until the end of September or beginning of October, when no doubt the faculty will be granted. In the meantime it may be stated there is a sum of £5l in hand for this purpose from the the Jubilee Memorial Fund. The cost of a peal of small bells would be £l6O, or of one of the larger size £2lO. The committee were of opinion that it would be the wiser plan to choose the larger size and place the bells in position as the money was subscribed. Thus I for the present the plan would be to erect the necessary structure for the eight bells and place the large tenor C bell in position, the cost of which would be £35 ; the other bells will cost respectively £3O, £29, £27, £25, £23, £2l and £2O. But after putting the first bell in it would not be wise to proceed further until there is money enough to place at least four others in position, as a peal of less than five bells would be, to say the least, tantalizing and trying to the ear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>An Eventfu! Month.</head>
        <p>An Eventfu! Month.</p>
        <p>In the course of his monthly letter to his parishioners the Rev. H. B. Firth, Vicar of St. James', Latchford, refers to July as "an eventful month in more ways than one." First of all he alludes to the re-opening services held at Winchester Cathedral on St. Swithin's Day. Continuing, he writes :—But many people who do not knows much about Winchester will remember St. Swithin's Day, 1912 ; for on this day the National Health Insurance Act Came into operation, and although it would not be difficult to write a good many lines on the subject, I would merely say that our truest wisdom is to make the best of it and trust that it may do an immense amount of good to those on whose behalf the Act has been made. The Million Penny Fund of the Warrington Infirmary has received a very substantial sum of money from the Old English Fair lately held in Victoria Park and the promoters of the scheme are to be warmly congratu- lated on the success of their undertaking. The last day of the fair was a Saturday and on the day following a very noticeable diminution in the number of children at morning Sunday school and of grown-up people at the morning service was apparent. lam told that on the Saturday night crowds of people were wending their way home at night and that boys and girls were walking about the streets after twelve o'clock. The cld ques- tion forces itself upon us—" Have Christian people any business to tire themselves out with pleasure on a Saturday night so as to prevent their fulfilling their Sunday obligations ?" Another question that arises is this—" What are parents thinking of to allow their young people to stay out till these late hours ? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Fight Against Cancer.</head>
        <p>The Fight Against Cancer.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell has en article in the Burton- wood Parish Magazine, entitled " Into the jaws of hungry cancer." He writes :—The following authorita- tive statements should he well pondered by those who, having come to years of discretion, do not want to be delivered into the jaws of hungry cancer. "It is estimated upon present evidences that no less than 25,000,000 of the now living generation of mankind are doomed to die of cancer." "Cancer stands out as the one cause of death accounting for a really important and significant increase of mortality." "This malady, as is well known, is responsible for the most agonising ' sufferings in its victims, and provides excuse for operations of the most appalling character." "Of the 100,000 experiments upon living animals reported by British vivisectors during last year,approxi- , mately one half are attributed to 'Cancer Research' " Does it not seem that something should be done " when last year 34,000 men and women in England and Wales alone died from cancer ? " "Cancer is most certainly preventible, and is usually curable." Speed the good news far and near. Let the people hear the joyful gospel. "Ye shall not die butlive." Lift up your heads, ye women, who go in dread of cancer from the time of early womanhood ! Redemption from the cruel scourge has come ; you need not contract cancer and if contracted you need not fall into the jaws of death. Better far to claim a certifLtate of exemption. Why go in the path of the grim, hungry monster ? Why incur risk? Those who fear cancer and would escape its pain, its tragedy, must give good heed to the food they eat and the mastication of it. Our famous Dr. Robert Bell tells us the cancerous patient is mostly drawn from the ranks of the flesh eaters. Non. fishers are practically exempt. We believe him ; we pray God to bless his noble, unselfish efforts for suffer- ing humanity. "It is," says the " Herald of the Golden Age," " extraordinarily Interesting to see how the cancer curve follows the curve of luxury in food and drink—so much so that it can be predicted with certainty from the material prosperity of a nation whether the cancer death-rate will be high or low. Thus Switzerland Ints,the highest death rate in Europe. But not Switzerland as a whole. No ! only those cantons where the influx of visitors brings riches. In the poorer cantons which prosperity has passed by, the cancer death rate is low or normal though the same climatic conditions prevail. The rate is thus highest in Appenzell and Lucerne, lo west in Valais." These weighty words have been suggested by the Hon. Rollo Russell's new book, Preventative Cancer" (Longruan's, 4s. 6d.) This valuable work based upon indisputable fates gathered from Embassies and Legations, Government Departments, Assurance Societies and other reliable authorities should ring the death knell of vivisection and prove an eye-opener to the medical profession as a whole. But what a lot of "eye-opening 'statesmen, clergy, and the public need as well as the medicos. Of all alike it may be said with truth, "0, slow of eye to perceive ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday presided over by Mr. Joseph Owen, who spoke upon "The helpfulness of the brotherhood." Mr Hamblett took for the subject of his address" The oneness of the brotherhood." He pointed out that as members of one family, they were all dependant upon each other for individual effort to carry the Gospel to thme outside the family of Christ's followers. A collection was taken on behalf of the sufferers in connection with the dock strike in London, to be sent to the Rev. Bertram Portnell, 93, Canton- street,Poplar, E., whose helpers are feeding 900 children dail ,v and who had written for help. Mr. Joynson and the President took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Viner, who is acting as moderator of this church during the vacancy in the pastorate, has addressed to the congregation a letter in which he says: —I count it a privilege to be able in any way to serve the Church and to be associated with the good work done for so many years by your esteemed pastor, the Rev J Yonge. The life, work and worship of the Chinch are a trust in charge of the whole community. If one and aibprayerfully and diligently seek to culti- vate the spiritual life of the Church rind keep at a high level its fellowship and work, the time immediately before us will witness a development of power and will bring great blessing to. the Church. We are all anxious to secure a Minister suitable to the , position as soon as possible. The supreme need is, of course, that whoever comes, he should be the man sent of God. It is therefore most of all important that we should, by waiting upon God. prepare our minds and hearts to rocognise His leadership and know His will. An united people, a One equipment end a large oppor- tunity awe-t. the new leader. Meanwhile, we rejoice to knew that the Lord of the Church is with us. His presence has been its strength and glory in the past, it gives us courage now and is our hope for all tbe future. The Rev John Davidson of Newton-le-Willows. con- ducted the services at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday. As part of the morning service he addressed the children on " Opportunity " and emphasised the importance of seizing opportunities when they present themselves in all their beauty and freshness, otherwise they become ugly and repulsive when disregarded and contemned. Preaching from the text "Put on thy beautiful garments, 0 Jerusalem" lii. he spoke of the beauty of religion, the joy and brightness of it, and the need that Christians should realise that the inner spirit of true religion wove for itself a glorious outer apparel. The preacher's evening discourse was based on John xvi. 33, "Jesus said 'Be of good cheer for I have conquered the world." He said that iu life's difficulties, trials and temptations these words were an inspiration and an encouragement. Jesus was not a doleful pessimist but spoke of joy repeatedly and was cheerful and brave. It was easy to criticise, but to cheer was rare. Religion was not sorrowful but joyous. Ile had no sympathy with the</p>
        <p>theology of gloom. There was no holiness in melancholy. The truo Christian was bright and cheery and brave and realized the duty of giving encouragement, and not blame. The preacher for to-morrow (Sunday) is the Rev. William Evans, of Llandulas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SUCCESS OF KIWIC BIGGEST POSSIBLE.</head>
        <p>THE SUCCESS OF KIWIC BIGGEST POSSIBLE.</p>
        <p>For the past week, more people have called on the representative of the Kimoc Indian Medicine Co., at the Indian Village, Park- avenue and Knutsford-road, than he can attend to. Hundreds have come to inquire about the Kimoc Remedies and to get a trial bottle of Kimoc Blood Tonic, the Indian Medicine made from roots, herbs, barks, berries, leaves and gums—Nature's remedies for diseases of the stomach and liver. Derangements of these organs cause most of the diseases with which. People suffer, such as biliousness, indigestion, sick headache, rheumatism, palpitation of the heart, jaundice, malaria, loss-of appetite, and that out-of-sorts feeling. We believe that chronic ill-health is caused principally by stomach and livers disorders. We believe that very few people can be ill if their digestion is in good order. We know that Kimoc Blood Tonic will put the stomach and liver in good condition in four to six weeks. That is why it sells so wonderfully all over the world.</p>
        <p>under the treatment of two different doctors, and I was very discouraged. I heard of the Kimoc Indian Remedies, and I „o-ot a bottle of Kimoc Blood Tonic. I noticed an improve- ment from almost the very first dose. I began. to eat better, and gained my former strength and felt better in every way. My friends have noticed and spoken about my improved appeai-ance, and I thank the Kimoc Medicine for it, You can use this testimonial and wel- come.</p>
        <p>(Signed) Mrs. ALICE SUT.CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>"4, Plainp-ton-'stieet, The other Kimoc Medicines are also of the highest possible quality, efficiency and relia- bility. They are Kimoc Family Liniment, for aches and pains; Kimoc Tablines, for children's</p>
        <p>diseases:</p>
        <p>Kinme Ointment; Kimoe Cough ,Syrup, for coughs, colds, sore throats, &amp;c.; and Kimoe Pills. the finest laxative and cathartic kuown.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mice Sutcliffe, 4, Plumpton-street, War- rington, makes the following statement about Kimoc Blood Tohic:— „ _</p>
        <p>Kimoc Medicines are sold by all Chemists. Our representative will explain. Kimoc Medi- cines and give free trial bottles of Kimoc Blood Tonic to all who call at the Indian Village, Park-avenue and Knutsford-road, from. 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. daily. Healy and Bigelow, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>" July 30th, 1912.</p>
        <p>"I have been troubled for over three years with indigestion, stomach trouble and weak heart. I used to have pains and aches, and my fool did not do me any good. I have been</p>
