      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Decrease in Wesleyan Membership.</head>
        <p>The Decrease in Wesleyan Membership.</p>
        <p>An important pastoral, issued by the direction of the Wesleyan Conference, and addressed to the Methodist churches throughout Great Britain, will be issued shortly. The pastoral directs attention to the fact that a decrease in church membership has now been reported for six years in succession, and that in addition there are to-dey fewer children in Wesleyan Sunday schools, and .a lessened attendance at public worship. This decline is in part accounted for by the stream of emigration to Canada, Australia, and the United States. During the past twelve months, the Methodism of these lands las received at least 2,000 more members from this country than it did in a similar period 15 years ago. The pastoral further points out that during the first it t) years of its history the membership of the Methodist Church grew at a far more rapid rate than th. population of the country. The secret of this success was the spirit of evangelism that possessed preachers and people alike. Methodism was born in a i rival, and the evangelistic spirit is the health by which it lives. The pastoral, while admitting that ruin h which characterised early Methodism has gone never to return, affirms that at no period in its past history did the spirit of evangelism express itself in more numerous or more varied forms than in the Methodist Church of the present day. At the same time, that spirit is not as universal in Methodism as it once was. There has been a cooling in ardour, and therefore the Methodist people are urged to cultivate the evaugelistio spirit afresh. The pastoral closes with an appeal for support to the two great enterprises which are to mark the new ecclesiastical year -the movement among the young people, to which the president of the Conference especially intends to devote his year of office, and the centenary celebration of the founding of the Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Society, with the raising cf a quarter of a million sterling for the extension of its work abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FATALITY NEAR HOWLEY BRIDGE. Coroner Rebukes a Juryman.</head>
        <p>FATALITY NEAR HOWLEY BRIDGE. Coroner Rebukes a Juryman.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, had occasion to rebuke a jurymau for contradicting a witness at the inquest held at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday on William Eckersley (47), a carter employed by Messrs. Buckley and Co., and who resided at 31, Robson-street. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the relatives and Mr. A. J. Willett for the employers. Lucy Agnes Eckersley, widow of the deceased, stated that her husband had teen employed for four years by Messrs Buckley and Co. During the week-ends he was fairly temperate but at the week-ends he used to take " a glass or two' of beer. On Monday, July 22nd, at 5.30 a.m. he left home to go to his work, and at 12 30 p.m. she received information that he had met with an ' accident. Si.e saw him at the lofirmary, and when she asked him how the accident happened, he replied, Quite simply. It might happen to anyone. I was getting in the cart when the horse started, and I fell backward." He died on Saturday morning last at 6.30. John Litherland, labourer in the employ of the Corporation, living at 1, Nora-street, stated that for the past four mouths he had been employed on the approach to the new bridge between Howley and Latchford. Deceased was employed in carting cinders and it was witness's duty to book each load for the Corporation. At eight o'clock the deceased came up with his first load of cinders. Witness noticed that he had had some drink and on deceased asking him for the price of a drink, he replied "1 think you have had enough. You ought to leave it (the drink) alone this morning, or you won't be capable of driving a horse." Shortly after nine o'clock the man came with a second load, and at ten o'clock with a third load, and witness noticed that he stumbled about very much. He told him he had better go to Buckley's yard as he was not capable of driving a horse. The man swore, and told witness he was not the boss." " All right have your own way," witness replied and left him. At 11 o'clock Eckersley came with the fourth load, and he appeared to be worse than ever; in witness's opinion he was very drunk. He again told him he had better go to the yard. Eckersley then began to curse, after which he got on the cart and shouted for the horse to go on, which it did. When it had gone about five yards, he saw the deceased fall backwards out Of the cart on to the ground, the spokes of the wheel striking the deceased's head until witness stopped the horse. Ni'itness bathed his head with water. Eckersley was unconscious and was removed to the Infirmary by order of Dr. Anderson. By Mr. Willett: In his opinion the man was quite unfit to be in charge of a horse. By Mr. Steel : Witness gave the orders as to where the loads were to be put. He did not know whether the making of the roadway was a" rush job" or not. Mr. Steel : It had been arranged to open the bridge in time for the "Old English Fair "had it not ? Witness : I could not say. Do you know that his Worship the Mayor had fixed a certain day for the opening of the bridge ?—I don't know. The Coroner (to Mr. Steel) : You as a member of the Town Council ought to know more about the matter than anyone. Mr. Steel : Was there not a rush to get the bridge ready for opening ? Witness did not reply. Mr. Steel : I put it to you that this man was hurrying over his work. Witness: No, he was not hurrying. That was only his fourth load, and other drivers had brought five loads in. William Owen, labourer, gave similar 'evidence as to the accident, Mr Willett ; You are satisfied that deceased was not fit to manage a horse. Witness : 1 say he was capable. You say he bad had some drink ?—I said he was</p>
        <p>under the influence of drink, but he put the cinders where I told him.</p>
        <p>Further Questioned, witness repeatedly stated that he was the only one who saw the &amp;ceasol t'all off the cart</p>
        <p>Dr. Bishop, senior house surgeon at the Infirmary,. said the death was cue LO brot.k.atat p..eu.uunia, lug the injuries. The Coroner, alluding to the qaestions put by the solicitors, said the qoesticn of compensation bad nothing to do with the jury. It had, however, been the practice to allow the Court to be made use of on these occasions as a kind of fishing pond for the solicitors on behalf of the employer and employed, to get evidence to assist them in the County Court, when they went before the Judge. He had no doubt that Mr. Willett would set up that there had been wilful misconduct on the part of the deceased in getting drunk, while Mr. Steel would no doubt try and paint him as an angel with all the virtues in the world. Let those two gentlemen light the matter out between themselves in another Court. It did not affect the Coroner's Court. He said incident- ally that it would be manifestly unfair to an employer to say in the case of a man under the influence of drink, who had got into a cart, that drink had nothing to do with the accident. At any rate he was not the County Court Judge. If he were be would soon dispose of the point without very much difficulty. The witness Litherland was recalled at the request of a juryman, who asked him whether any policemen were on duty on the road which the deceased would have to traverse when he was alleged to be under the influence of drink. Witness said he had never seen an officer there, whereupon the juryman retorted " You ought to rub your eyes, then." The Coroner : The witness is entitled to respect and he must have it. You have no right to contradict him. Why don't you take his answer? Ihe Juror : Well I have taken his answer... . . . .</p>
        <p>The Coroner : Well, either believe it or disbelieve it. Don't tell him he is telling a lie. The witness has given his evidence very well. Another juror asked the witness if there was no supervision, so as to prevent a man from driving whilst under the influence of drink?</p>
        <p>Witness : It was my place to send him to Buckley's yard. I told him to 150 there, but he would not. The juror : I consider it was your duty to stop him at once, when 3ou found him under the influence of drink.</p>
        <p>The Coroner (to witness) : You had better take your directions from me. You were not his master in any shape or form and you had no right to stop him. The jury returned a verdict of " Ar:cidental death." Alr Willett, on behalf of the employers, expressed sympathy with the relatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING ASSOCIATION. (N.A. CENTRE).</p>
        <p>Owing to the unfavourable conditions we have had lately, angling has suffered to no small extent. The rivers, especially those fished by the locals, have been on the run with the fresh in them, and angling has been almost impossible. There is no doubt that those who spent the week-end canal fishing easily had the advan- tage : Chester, Mickle Trafford and Hartford have returned poor results and those who visited these places had anything but an enjoyable holiday. We have heard of members who have been away since Saturday at noon and have not returned with a decent fry. It was rumoured by several members that the canal Beeaton way bad become polluted, but on making inquiries we learn that it is not so and Northern Anglers Chester- way fail to see how this canal can get contaminated as there are no worse on the banks, in that district and the nearest works to Beeston is the Bone Works at Tatnall-road. It would be a very sad affair if this water did happen to get fouled in any way, as most of our members rate it as the best canal in the north for specimen fishing, as well as match fishing. The canal Acton Bridge 'way (Trent and Mersey) this week will be run off at different stages and from the reports shortly to hand we shall know whether it has done much good or not.</p>
        <p>The Dee from Saturday last nas been practically un- fishable. Members who visited here for bream fishing returned without wetting a line. But the canal here is fishing better than it bas done for several years, bream and ri,ach being plentiful.</p>
        <p>HARTFORD. The match at Hartford, arranged for the benefit of the Infirmary by the Northwich Northern Anglers, was the drawing card for our local match fishers but, owing to the fresh in the river, sport was entirely off and many of our class anglers never had a stir right through. the contest. Strange to say two fine specimens of perch were landed directly the contest was over, one of them drawing llb 2oz.</p>
        <p>MICKLE TRAFFORD. Members who intended spending their holiday angling the Gowy were greatly disappointed, tbe fresa in the stream upsetting their calculations. Several walked over and fished the canal with better results than they could expect from the Guwy under the cir- cumstances. LONG BARN. Long Barn has again recovered and several members had fair catches, the perch especially doing well. We have heard of one being hooked and brought along the surface of the water for several yards, but unfortunately the weeds and fish combined beat the angler. Those who saw it say it would have tnped the set board easily, and we have already some flue perch weighed in.</p>
        <p>NORTON.</p>
        <p>The canal here is not doing very well as regards numbers' but members are returning with a better class of fish. The roach weighed in up to the present for the August special turn the scales at lloz. SeverLl members inquired whether any pollution had got into this length, as several roach were marked red, similar to those taken at Hartford some 12 months ago. We have heard nothing from Runcorn end up to the time of writing and, as the fish have not gone entirely off feed, we fail to see any cause for alarm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>_ Club night Monday 10th at 8 p.m. All officials who intend fishing the match on August 18th at Hartford please hand in their names to the secretaries. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>Taxation of Land Values. A Part of the Liberal Programme. Sir,—There is a good deal of misapprehension in regard to the recent announcement in the Press that a campaign is to be started in favour of the agitation for the taxation of land values. This is regarded in some quarters as a new and dangerous movement, craftily set on foot by " that wicked Lloyd George." who is seeking to force an obnoxious policy on his colleagues. The only doubt in their minds is, whether he will remain in the Cabinet and work his sinister policy as he best can, or resign his position and pursue an independent campaign in the country, as Mr. Chamberlain did in regard to his scheme of Colonial Preference. The latter suggestion which has been gravely discussed by very eminent men in the journalistic world is really absurd. The taxation of land values Lever was Mr. Lloyd George's scheme ; it was a part of the Liberal programme that the Liberal Chancellor of the Exchequer had to carry out. Twenty-three years ago the Financial Reform Associa- tion, which had for long taken a leading part in the agitation for the taxation of land values, memoi ialised every Liberal sssociation, and all the leaders of the Liberal party, with a view to getting the abolition of "The Breakfast Table Duties" and the substitution of a tax upon the value of land—apart from buildings or recent improvements—definitely made a pa:t of the Liberal programme. The results were favourable,and both of these reforms were embodied in the official programme of the National Liberal Federation at the meeting. in ..lanchester in 1,559, and re-affirmed in the famous Newcastle prOgranatte pert year. When the I.iberals obtained office in 1892,th Chancellor of the Exchequer, Sir William IlarczAirt led two important f.nancial schemes before him, the reform of the " Death Duties," and the Taxation of Land Values. Be dealt first with the death dutica, thereby incurring at the time Es much obloquy as Mr. Lloyd George has incuired with his budget but with subsequent universal commendation, as will also be the case with Mr. Lloyd George. Sir William has stated that if the Government had remained in Office, he would have dealt with the question of land values. While the Liberal party was in opposition the ques- tion of the taxation of land values was vigorously agitated throughout the constituerc'es, and became so popular that resolutions were frequently brought for- ward in the House of Commons in favour of the measure. Kren in a Tory Parliament the adverse majorities became each year smaller until at laq the advocates of the reform had the satisfaction of know- ing that not only was it favoured by almost every Corporate body, but had actually been agreed to in principle hy the House of Commons. Now all this time Mr Lloyd George had taken no prominent part in the movement, but when he became Chancellor of the Exchequer he was brought face to face with this reform which had been so delayed as to cause great grumbling amongst an influential section of the Liberal Party. It was as the Chancellor of the Exchequer, and not as the Social Reformer, that Mr. Lloyd George took up this question. When it was introduced by a Liberal Government, those Conservatives who had voted for the resolutions in favour of land value taxation turned round and denounced the very thing many of them had made a prominent feature in their Election Addresses. They pleaded that they voted in Parliament for the tax as a. local source of revenue. Well, the proposed agita- tion is chiefly with a view to making the taxation of land values a source of local taxation and to secure Its extension to agricultural land to which at has all along been intended to apply. There is nothing revolutionary in the recrudes- cence of the movement, nor will there be any need for a member of the Cabinet to resign his office in order to carry on an agitation in favour ?f what is, and has long been, a recognised and important "plank" in the official platform. A renewal of the agitation has become necessary in view of the attempts made by the landowners to persuade the national or local authorities to buy them out, or lend funds to others to buy them out. If the taxation of land values is in force for a few years the pur- chasing will be at once easier and more equitable for the public.—Yours faithfully, EDMUND K. ITUSPRATT, Financial Reform Association, President. Liverpool, August 2nd, 1912.</p>
        <p>ll • fro ~ r , 1,, .\i to the , , • Jig 'I ~,I - 1 .P t ..... EDITOR, ,t_. ~ i ) ~ ~ ---!-----7-7"-t_ :-1-1- . ... ...----p. - . ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>LOCAL SUCCESSES.</p>
        <p>Business calls and the necessity for working alternate day and night turns have militated against members of the local athletic club in their training operations,and although competitive outings have been restricted owing to lack of cheap travelling facilities, yet a fair measure of success has attended their recent efforts as follows :—At èlostoo, J H Ardern won ODO cycle race and was second in the other and despite a penalty being deducted from his programme marks,got two second prizes at War rington and one at Rainford. At Huddersfield W Siddall ion the fastest sprint heat to the winner, by inches, but made no mistake in the quarter mile flat which he won comfortably by quite 12 yards in 52 3-ssecs. Again at Warrington he met the winner in the preliminary heat of the sprint and lost by inches but at Rainford, the following day made amends by winning the half mile, from 66 yards mark, in 1 min. 57 2-5 secs At Warring- ton Harold Povey won the 220 yards off 15 yards in 23secs. and probably missed a double event by anticipa- ting the pistol in the final of the 1( 0 yards. T Wall was third off 70 yards, in the half mile which was run at a false pace owing to the competitors realising the inadvisability of making the running against a trouble- some breeze, 2nain 31secs. S Siddall, who did the donkey work all the way compounded 20 yards from the finish. C Vose (pulled 45 yards) was third in the two miles Eat which was won off 163 yards in 3min 29secs (excellent time for the trick) by T Fennah, the winner of the " Collier Cup," who together with M Tracey, Manchester, 265 yards and .1 Hogan (a little" goodun") Bury, 255 yards, blotted the handicap. At Stoke, 11 Holbrook won the :2 mile steeplechase off 60 yards and R F Anderton was third in the 3CO yards hurdle handicap off 26 yards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MEMO OF MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MEMO OF MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The scholars seem to prefer a team race to a handicap and that is a very healthy sign. 100 yards time in beats and final :—Povey 81 yards, lOsecs ; Green 9: yards, yard outside lOsecs ; Jones 11 yards 10 2-ssecs; Thorpe 11 yards 11 1-10 secs ; Kelly 9yards 101 secs. Final Green, Kelly, Jones time 10 1-ssecs foot inches. Except when the first two heats were run the breeze was against the runners. 220 yards:—Bryan 17 yards 24secs ; Green 13', yards 24 I-ssecs ; Jones IS yards 23 7-10 secs ; Povey 15 yards 23 1-10 secs ; Porter 16 yards 2isecs; Poizier 16 yards 23 3-ssecs. Final Povey, Bryan, Porter ; time 23secs, 5 yards, 3 yards. This is a mest unsatisfactory dis- tance tor tracks less than five laps to the mile—Jost- ling lost J C Cartwright his heat and the spill at the first bend in the final might easily have had more con- sequences for Poizier than the loss of second place. 440 Yards.—With the liberal allowance of 28 yards, A Brown ;Broughton and Salford Harriers) did 52secs in his beat and 52 3-10 secs in final. One mile time 4nain 24 3-ssecs. won by J E Cleminson, Salford H, off 104 yards. This is the same runner that Arthur Siddall made such a plucky effort to concede 51 yards start to earlier in the season. The track was heavy and 'the conditions against fast times, therefore the cycling required care. Local athletes were justifiably wroth at some of the baadicapping and many refrained from competing so as not to sacrifice their starts for nothing. The sport W3EI quite up to Bank Holiday average but the gate was poor.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE OWNERSHIP OF ELEVATORS. The experiment of Government ownership of grain elevators made In Manitoba in the interests of the farming community has not proved a success. .Another method calculated to secure the object origingally striven for, namely, the relief of the farmer from the i exactions often arising under private ownership, s .to be adopted. The Government is ilf negotiation with the Grain Growers' Association of the province for tbe transfer of the Government elevators to that body on the basis of a rental representing 6 per cent. on their capitalisation, thus placing the handling of grain and the macagement of the elevators in the hands of the representatives of the farmers tbernseves. Much better offers than the above from private, individuals have been declined, for the reason that as the acquisition of the elevators was undertaken in the interests of the farmers, the farmers were entitied to obtain them at cost. In taking over the elevators of the Manitoba Government system, numbering in all 174, scattered throughout the province, the association will secure terminal elevators from the Canadian Pacific Railway at Fort William on a like basis. The Government pro- posal, however, is to lease the property of the Grain Growers' Association for a year on trial, against the alternative suggestion of five years put forward by the farmers' representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Territorials Equip Station on Blackpool</head>
        <p>Territorials Equip Station on Blackpool</p>
        <p>Tower.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire Territorials in camp at Kirkham are determined to understand the most modern scientific war-aids. Just at present it is not aeroplanes, but wireless telegraphy, which is engaging their attention. Their experiments are being carried out at Blackpool Tower, which is constituted as part of the camp, the intention being to carry out a number of important tests in wireless telegraphy. The apparatus, which is fixed at the summit of the tower, consists of a wireless station for field work, andis in charge of Captain DarrettnCoulston, R.E., and Lieut. W. Bottomley. The officers and men left the camp, about eight and a half mile 3 from Blackpool, on Monday, with a motor-waggon, covering the distance in thirty-five minutes. The experiments to be carried out are mainly over the sea, the apparatus being constructed for a range of 100 miles, although it has been worked at a cons:derably greater distance. In fact, 200 mile messages have been picked up by it elsewhere, and during these experiments it is hoped to get into touch with Clifton, on the west coast of Ireland, and with the Eiffel Tower, Paris.</p>
        <p>THE CAMPAIGN AGAINST THE RAT. No doubt thelocrurrence of plague at Liverpool will direct fresh attention to the campaign against the rat. 'this was taken up with great vigour when a few cases occurred in Suffolk ; hut if the rat is to be extermin- ated it will never be done by sudden bursts of energy, but by the carrying out of a consistent and determined policy. In a few districts rats have, it is true, been very nearly exterminated ; but this is of very little avail as long as there are others where the rodent is allowed to prosper and multiply. In these he is only liable to the casual mishap of falling upon the tender mercies of a sharp terrier, or being caught by the vermin trap. A great many country people scout the idea that the rat is the chief factor in conveying the infection of plague, and they argue that even if the animal were infested with the plague fleas, these have very little opportunity of transferring their lodging to human beings. Of conrse, this is a great fallacy. The flea when leaving the rat will make its lodging in the fur of any other similar quadruped, even in that of those animals which are used for human food.— "Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>•vowimenimmimml</head>
        <p>•vowimenimmimml</p>
        <p>'flt"7,xl"°,''</p>
