      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>THE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>ROUGH TIME IN CAMP AT KIRKHAM.</p>
        <p>In these days the Territorials have always to be prepared to " rough it" whilst undergoing their annual training under canvas. No longer can the encampment be scoffingly termed a " picnic " ; it means a fortnight of arduous military work, even when the conditions are at their hest. But unusually trying experiences have fallen to the lot of "our citizen" soldiers this week at Kirkham where, as part of the West Lancashire Division of the Territorial force, they are at present in training. The weather has, of course, been to blame. When the men reached the camp the prospect was cheerless and depressing, for rain was falling in torrents and some parts of the ground were soon churned into a quagmire. To make matters worse, a gale sprang up during the night, the result being that tent pegs were wrenched from the ground in hundreds, and huge marquees were lifted sky high badly or literally torn to tatters. The Warringtonians appear to have fared somewhat better than some other sections of the division but they are not likely soon to forget their first night in Kirkham camp. The writer of the Terri- torial notes in the " Liverpool Post and Mercury " thus comments on the opening of the camp "I am not going so far as to say that in all my experi- ence I have never seen a camping ground that at first sight looked so bad as did that where the West Lane I- shire Division is now under canvas at Kirkham, but I certainly do any that it would be a heavy tax upon the memoiy to recall a worse. Of comae, should the weather pick up a bit there won't be anything lunch to complain ab„ut ; but should it throughout the next fortnight continue as it was on concentration Sunday, and again on Monday morning—well, my heart gm s out in pity for the poot mudlarks. To begin with, the surface of the camping ground is very uneven—very, very uneven. In fact, there are in it as many inequalities as to the biased mind there are in the Insurance Act. It is full of ruts and trenches and 'hammocks and hollow, and they in their tura---at 1 events, the ruts, the tenches, and the lir,Rva -are just ncw fair t W4..'m Tbe 87-11 s Fits are full, the ' ditchee o.re full, *l-Id. v, 11.%6 —pretty tear, anyhow— is the low-13 ig °rinds on which the tents of the Western Telegraph Companies have been pitched. As a matter of fact, the soil is almost pure clay. If you had seen the state of my lutties and boots when I arrived home yesterday afternoon, you'd ask for, on this point no further evidence. A first glance at the manmuvricg are?,vvrites another correspondent, does not impress one too favourably. The ground seems far too restricted in area, and is flat and uninteresting. Since the inception of the Territorial Force the troops of the West Lancashire Division have had their share, and perhaps more than their share of hill-climbing. This is true of the Isle of man camps as also those at Caerwys, in the Lune Valley, and on the, Westmorland fells. Well, this year things will even themselves up a bit, for not I for many miles around here is there to be seen a hill !worthy of the name. I The division is not, of course, at its full strength. it is short of the Liverpool Scottish (who have already undergone their training), of the Ist West Lancashire Brigade, R.F.A , and of the Howitzer Brigade (who this year go through a course at Trawsfynydd, in North Wales), and of the Liverpool Brigade, the Liverpool Brigade Transport Company, and the 2nd West Lancashire Field Ambulance (who have been selected to represent the Territorial Force at the Army manceuvres, to be held in the neighbourhood of Cambridge next month). The units under canvas are the 2nd and 3rd West Lancashire Brigades, R.F.A., the Divisional Royal Engineers, the Western Telegraph Companies, RE., the oth King's aid 4th and sth Battalions South Lancashire Regiment (South Lancashire Brigade), the 4th and sth Battalions King's Own Royal Lancaster Regiment and the 4th and sth Battalions Loyal North Lancashire Regiment (North Lancashire Brigade), the Ist and 3rd West Lancashire Field Ambulances, and the West Lancashire Supply and Transport Column. It is estimated that Major-General Walter Lindsay, , C.B. D. 5.0., who recently succeeded Major-General Bethune as G.O.C. the West Lancashire Division. will have under his command during the fortnight not far short of 10,000 troops of various arms. ' Much has been maae of the proximity of the camp to Blackpool. The popular seaside watering-place is, however, some seven or eight miles away, which is fairly near, but not quite so near as could be wished. The Y.M.C.A. people have once again erected several large marquees for the use of the troops. During the latter part of the week the conditions have, happily, improved somewhat and some interest- ing exercises have been carried out. Our correspondent in the camp sends us the follow- ing notes on the doings of the Warriugton battalion : SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment I opened their annual encampment on Sunday last. Despite the inclement weather large crowds af people , witnessed the departure of the men from Bank Quay Station. Two trains were required to convey the troops, the first leaving promptly at 1.25 p.m. and the second at 1.40, calling at Newton to pick up the detachment from that town. A very quick journey was made and Kirkham was reached in good time. A march of a mile and a half brought the battalion to camp, which was found in a high position from which a good view of the surrounding country is obtained. Blackpool Tower is prominent from the battalion parade ground. Lieutenant-Colonel B Fairclough is in command of the local battalion and the other officers present are Major F M Appleton, Captains G R Crosfield, R Smith, P N Avery, Lieutenants F 11 Monks, McClure, M Woods, 0 L Whittle,B S Townroe G H Ingram (quartermaster),L S Henshall, Coiling- wood, C H Robertson, Lees, Seymour, Jones and Captain C F Cary (acting adjutant). 'On arrival in camp, the men were quickly told off to!their respective tents and supplied with bedding, &amp;c. After being medically inspected, all were free to settle down to their new life. The time was taken up in digging trenches round the tents to make them habitable. This was soon accomplished and " lights out " was sounded with the camp in a fairly comfortable condition, considering the heavy rain.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>When the troops awoke this morning, they found the rain still falling heavily, and no early morn- ing parade took place. 'i he scout signallers, machine gun section and other "special duty" sections assemb- led in the dining tent and were lectured by their respective commanders. The rain cleared off about 10.30 and the battalion were exercised on the parade ground under their company officers. After dinner, battalion and company drill was the programme, after which the men were free for the day. A large num- ber took advantage of the fine afternoon and left camp for Blackpool, Lytham and St. Annes. Those who stayed in camp were well catered for in the Y.M.C.A. Imarquee which is, as usual doing a good work among Ithe men.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>The early morning parade at b 30 was under com- pany officers and the men were generally instructed in the more serious work of the training. At 9 o'clock the battalion paraded in the company lines and marched out of camp. Avery .interesting morn- ings work was carried out in the formation of an attack ritli advance and rear guards. The cyclists and signallers of the whole division were exercised in an extremely interesting reconnaisance scheme. Six messages were delivered to various men four of whom were to be found in Garstang,and the other two were unknown. The cyclist had to find these men and receive the messages and hand them to the nearest signallers who had to transmit them through a chain of stations to the divisional headquarters. The Warrington signallers were in a good position on , a brick-kiln near IN harles and the general scheme was well carried out. The weather is now fine and every- thing points out to a good time in camp. WEDNESDAY. Opportunity was taken at the early morning parade for rehearsing for the great review at Blackpool on Saturday. The brigade formed up on the large parade ground near the camp, the 9th Liverpools being on the right, the 4th South Lancashires in the centre and the sth South Laccashires on the left. Colonel Thomas, V.D., was in command. Colonel Macfie, the brigadier, was received with the general salute and afterwards the whole brigade marched past in column and then dispersed to their respective grounds. The movement was well carried cut and presented a pleasing spectacle to the spectators. After breakfast the local contingent were served with an uncooked meat ration and marched in full marching order to the high ground near the Old Junction Brickworks. The men were engaged in a sham fight and heavy firing was soon in operation. The intention was to stay out for the day and cook rations in the field, but heavy rain came on and the battalion returned to camp. The signallers were engaged in a brigade scheme under Lieutenant Campbell. Stations were posted at Wesham Hall, Bibby, Great Plumpton, Westby Mills and in camp. The Warrington section occupied three stations</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NORTH-WEST MANCHESTER ELECTION.</head>
        <p>NORTH-WEST MANCHESTER ELECTION.</p>
        <p>and were very busy all the morning. The rain ceased in the afternoon and the weather though dull, was fine. Many men are leaving camp for the various pleasure resorts in the vicinity while football was played in the camp grounds. The 9,h I.iverpools have just left camp for a night's bivouac. The weather is now fine. The loeal signallers were called out very unexpectedly for night communication with the Liverpool's bivouac at Meorside, and received warm praise from the brigade signalliu,; officer. THMISDAY.</p>
        <p>The weather to-dab is a very pleasant change and almost perftct condltions prevail. The battalion were out in the early morning on the parade ground, and ' practised in general drill. After breakfast, the men turned out in full marching order and marched to Newton-in-Scales, about 41 miles. They were thrown out in skirmishing order under the company officers. Afterwards each company assembled in various parts of the field and was lectured by the officers on different topics. There is very little sickness in camp in spite of the bad weather experienced. A nasty accident occurred this afternoon in the dining tent. One of the orderlies was cutting bread in the machine when his hand slipped with the result that he has a nasty cut on the palm of the left hand. A diversion to-day was a garden party, given to the officers of the division by Lord Derby at Mowbreck Hall, which is about a mile from camp. Lord Derby was present to welcome his guests, but unfortunately the pioceedings were somewhat marred by a drizzling rain.</p>
        <p>Satut day's function at Blackpool when all the troops will leave camp and rerdezvons on the racecourse, promises to be a big affair.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>The weather to-day is fine and the battalion;Yas early astir. Breakfast was served at 6.30, after which the men proceeded through Kirkham and formed a line of outposts at VVharles. Being heavily pressed, the locals retired, the 'movement being carried out in a very orderly manner. Resistance was offered all the while until Inskip was reached, and the final bugle was sounded at 1.30. Camp was reached about four o'clock after a hard day's work.</p>
        <p>THE SMARTNESS OF TIIE WARRINGTON INVANTRY.</p>
        <p>A Manchester correspondent at Kirkham Camp, wiring on Thursday, pays the following tribute to the smartness of the Warrington men :—We were con- gratulating ourselves that at last the West Lancashire Territorials were to have a fine day when the clouds again began to gather. Towards noon, cycling along the road, to Kirkham, I passed the Warrington Infantry, under Colonel Fairclough. He ought to be proud of his new command. One of his officers called out : Don't the lads look well ?" They really did, stepping out jauntily on their way to the morning's drill. Their adjutant told me that Captain George Crosfield, the National Service enthusiast, was to qualify as major shortly. Captain Townroe, Unionist candidate for the Middleton Division, was as cheery as ever at the head of his company. Everybody seems in the best of humour this morning, probably owing to the glimpses of sunshine. Last night the Southport and Warrington Transport sergeants commandeered their three huge motor-waggons and invaded Black- pool, much to the amusement of the visitors, as the unusual cavalcade lumbered along the promenade. WIRELESS IN CAMP.</p>
