      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC MEETING IN ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Liberal Association is continuing its open-air campaign in all the wards of the town and on Thursday evening a good meeting was held on tbe vacant land at the corner of Longford-street. A great impression was created on the large crowd which assembled by a stirring speech from Councillor Middleton, of Oldham, who is a very popular speaker with the Warrington public. Mr. E. A. Sanders presided and in briefly introducing the speaker referred to the persistent misrepresentation of the Tory Party and said the Liberal Party were out to enlighten the people as to the true state .of affairs. (Hear, hear.) In promoting the true principles of the Liberal Party they felt sure they would have the hearty support of the people not only in Warrington but all over the country. (Applause). Councillor MIDDLETON gave a comprehensive review of the good work done by the Liberal party for the well-being of the great masses of workers in the country. We had never had a Government that paid more attention to the well-being of the little ones than the Liberal Government of the present time. (Hear, hear.) The speaker instanced the Children's Act, the provision of meals for poor ch.ldren, and the medical inspection of scholars. Any Government that made its first care that of looking after the children of the country was entitled to the support and confidence of the parents of those children. (Hear, hear.) The Liberal party also looked after each individual from birth to death, and the object of that party was to give the worker the best chance in life and to look after him in his struggle for a living. They had records of the height and weight of the children taken in the schools and in the future those statistics would show several very important things. They would help the Government to ascertain in which districts and under what conditions of life the children were better or worse in their physical condition. That was going to be a great help in the country in future years. (Hear, hear.) It was far more important that we should know something of what happened between the time of birth and death than to know how many people were born and how many died. (Hear, hear.) Rapid strides had been made by the Liberal Govern- ment during the last five years in the great programme of social reform. When they were engaged in such progressive work they were bound to tread on somebody's corns. They were bound to disturb someone when they were im- proving things and the people who were 'nut wished with all their hearts they had been left alone. (Hear, hear.) They wanted to stir the people up to the reali ties of their position and urge them to take advantage of the means provided for them by which they might improve their prospects. (Applause.) The policy of social reform caused a great number of people to be discontented and that WRI what was taking place in connection with the latest attempt at social legislation, the Insurance Act. (Hear, hear.) The Tory Party had not been able to exercise their imaginations on that measure and see how it would work. (Hear, hear.) They had said all along that the Act would not come into force for a long time—in fact at first they had said it would never come into operation at all. Impossibilities to Mr Lloyd George, however, were things that were to be done and difficulties were things to be overcome. (Applause.) Mr. Bonar Law was the man whom the Tory Party "swopped" for Mr Balfour who had expressed himself in favour of the Act, but the new leader had to do something different from his prede- cessor and had said the Act would never come into operation. He was considering the difference between ever and never. (Laughter.) He (the speaker) believed that some Conservative employers had been putting difficulties in the way of the smooth operation of the Act. (Hear, hear.) After a lengthy disclosure of the mean electioneering tactics of the Tory party in regard to the Act, the speaker said the Act looked after the interests of everyone. (Hear, hear.) It was real social reform they were working for; that which went down into the homes and the lives of the people, and the Liberal Party had had the Act explained all over the country because it was worth explaining. (Hear, hear.) They were striving for the time when employers and employees, capital and labour would be joined together working for the well being of the whole nation. (Applause). Questions were then invited, and to .each Councillor MIDDLETON gave a satisfactory answer. Mr P H BROWN, in proposing a vote of thanks to the speaker, said the Insurance Act was in advance of any legislation which had ever been attempted in this country. (Hear, hear). There was a great deal of insincere opposition to the Act but he believed it was for political purposes. Sir John Handles had admitted that what he was out for was to get the Governmant out of power. If the Act was going to be amended there was only one Party to do it and that was the Liberal Party. (Hear, hear.) He only hoped that when Warrington had a decision to make on that matter, the working men would remember the part played by the Liberal Government who had introduced that great beneficent measure. (Applause). The vote was heartily carried and on the proposition of COnneillOr MIDDLETON a hearty vote of thanks was seconded to Mr Sanders for his able conduct in the chair. The Councillor dealt at some length with the Insurance Act and explained the inner workings of the measure. He pointed out that no friendly society in the country could offer as good all round facilities as that great Act. (Hear, hear and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FIVE YEA lIS . FOR . SUFFRAGETTES.</head>
        <p>FIVE YEA lIS . FOR . SUFFRAGETTES.</p>
        <p>The trial concluded in Dublin on Wednesday of the militant suffragists arrested for the Theatre Royal out rage committed during the visit of the Prime Minister to the city last July. For setting fire to and causing an explosion in the theatre Gladys Evans and Mary Leigh were sentenced each to five years' penal servi- tude. Lizzie Baker, of Stockport, was sentenced to seven months' imprisonment for damaging property. In passing sentence on the two first named, Mr. Justice Madden said that the prisoner Leigh had put the thing in a nutshell when she observed that these crimes would cease if women bad the vote. It was, however, his duty to pass a deterrent sentence for the protection of public men and the public. He hoped that when the cause the prisoners had at heart was advocated by lawful and saner methods the sentences would be re- considered by the proper authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>OLD ENGLISH FAIR.</head>
        <p>THE OLD ENGLISH FAIR.</p>
        <p>MILLION PENNY FUND COMPLETED.</p>
        <p>£4OO Surplus.</p>
        <p>The result of the Old English Fair recently held in Victoria Park surpassed the most sanguine expecta- tions, for not only has the Million Penny Fund been completed but there is a surplus of £lOO. The latter SUM is to be devoted to two special objects which will add to the efficiency of the equipment of the Infirmary —the provision of a Rontgen rays apparatus and the! erection of a new mortuary. This decision was arrived at on Thursday evening at a meeting of the Million Penny Fund Committee held at the Infirmary to receive the complete statement of accounts. Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman of the committee) presided over a good attendance of workers who helped to make the fair such a conspicuous success. A letter was received from Colonel J. D. Buckton, hon. treasurer of the committee, in which he wrote :-" Please tell the committee that I was delighted to hear how successful the fair was. My one regret was that I could not share the labours, but I know from previous experience that the com- mittee must have worked like the Britons that indeed they ate and also how tired and hoarse and penniless and delighted they all were in the evening of the 13th ult. It will always stand to their credit that ' their j mt servic.-s have done great and unquestioa- able good in that part of the world in which they happen to be placed. The Board of Management and the committee will know what to do with the surplus:</p>
        <p>Mr. HENRY COLLIN (bon. secretary and acting hon. treasurer), then presented the statement of accounts. The total receipts were £1,167 0. 2d., and the expenses £302 103. 51., leaving a net profit of .t 864 ]Ss. 9d. Mr. W. LITTON moved the adoption of the accounts which, he said, were eminently satisfactory fr. ni every point of view. Great credit was due to Mr. Collin for having presented the statement in such a businesslike manner.</p>
        <p>• Mr. F. SHORT seconded and the proposition was agreed to. Mr. COLLIN then asked the Chairman's acceptance of a cheque, made out to the Million Penny Fund, for S:850 on account of the net prcfit. The CHAIRMAN, in accepting the cheque on behalf if the committee, said he would like to express what he knew was the feeling of all connected with the undertaking—the greatest possible gratitude to Mr Collin for tee work he had done. (Applause). He did not know what the committee could have done if they had not had the assi,tance of a gentleman like Mr. Collin who was prepared to devote so much time to its interests. The manner in which the accounts had been presentedlreflicted the greatest credit upon him. The Chairman next acknowledged the enormous amount of work done by the Old English Fair Com- mittee which was really the Million Penny Fund Committee with sundry other organizations cc-opted upon it. alley were also indebted to the Cycling Club and similar organizations which aid yeoman service and, last but not least, to the ladies and the nursing staff. Referring to the highly satisfactory profit from the bar, Mr. Snaethnrst said it was largely due to the excellent work of Mr. Finch, with the assistance of his brother-in-law, Mr. Throssell. In connection with the guessing competition, which was a very good source of revenue, very valuable assist- ance was rendered by Mr. H. Hope. Finally the Chairman paid a tribute to the co-operation of the Press in promoting the success of the enterprise. Mr. CowN personally thanked Messrs. Pearson, Bibby, Pemberton and Moseley for the magnificent help they gave him in the cash office. He also acknowledged the efficient services of the fourteen collectors who received the cash from the various , shows.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. JD STARKEY, seconded by Mr W OIVEN, a vote of thanks was passed to the boo. auditor (Mr R H Daintith). Mr. J H CLARKE (chairman of the Finance Com- mittee) informed the meeting that when the cheque for £B5O had been paid in, the amount standing to the credit of the Million Penny Fund would be about £.1,200. They were therefore in a position to hand over to the Chairman of the Infirmary sufficient money to complete the million pennies (about .:E4,000) which they undertook to obtain some two years ago. The actual amount that was required to complete the million pennies was 791 l3s. 4d. and he had pleasure in handing a cheque for that amount to Mr J H Smethurst, as chairman of the Board of Management. After handing over the cheque, they would still have .1:400 standing to their credit. (Applause). Mr. J. L. DAVENPORT, in seconding, said it was tbe "Joy Day" of the Million Penny Fund Committee. The CHAIRMAN then gave an interesting review of the committee's work.- He pointed out that on October 29th, 1909, a resolution was passed by the Board of Management that he (Mr. Smethurst) be empowered to form a Million Penoy Fund Committee. At that time he had no conception of the work that would be entailed in organizing and carrying through the effort. If it bad not, been for the splendid sup- port he had received from all the members of the committee, and notably from Mr. Collin, be himself could have done nothing. They had now the great satisfaction not only of having completed their fund but of placing a considerable balance to the good. ' (Applause.) Referring to the purposes to which it was proposed to allocate the surplus, Mr. Smethurst said that during the past few years the Infirmary had been considerably extended and much more bed accommodation had been provided. The conse- quence, naturally and unfortunately, was that the mortality was somewhat greater. lip to the present time, they had not made any preparation to deal with the question of improved mortuary accommodation. The provision of an X rays apparatus and of a new mortuary.had been receiving the most earnest con- sideration of the hoard of Management and the House Committee for several months. They were desirous that the hospital should be equipped with the most up-to-date appliances that it was possible to obtain. The honorary staff were extremely keen upon their work, and he was certain that if the committee would agree to the expenditure of the surplus in the way suggested, it would lead to greater efficiency, because they would be able to cope with all kinds of cases that needed examination before operations were performed. Mr. Smethurst then dealt with the need for a new mortuary, and pointed out that the House Committee had already adopted plans, but if there had not been a handsome surplus available they would not have been able to carry them out for some time. With regard to the finances of the Infirmary they estimated that during the present year they would not go farther behind than they had done in previous years. In Warrington thcy were extremely fortunate in the fact that the Infirmary was so well supported by the people generally, but they were not so well placed as some other hospitals in the matter of endowment. The Warrington Infirmary's endow- ment amounted to only £960, but he could give them the names of other hospitals, within thirty miles of Warrington, which had endowments of ;£2,200, £2,068 and £2,300 respectively. If the Warrington Infirmary occupied a similarly favourable position they would be able to open the vacant ward at the top of the new wing for the accommodation of patients. They had a right to expect that the people of Warrington and district should support them in the efforts they were making up to cope with the enormous number of eases of sickness and accidents that arose. They did not know what would be the result of the Insurance Act. They could only hope that it would not have any ill effects upon them. Within the next six months they would see the reselt of the stoppages that were being made from the workingmen's wages. The Workingmen's Committee had always done their best but be looked to them improving upon past achievements and providing the institution with more funds. On the proposition of Mr. A. C. RINKS, seconded by Mr. H. ROGERS, it was decided to devote the surplus of 400 to the two purposes mentioned.</p>
